window.BOTTLENECK_SEARCH={"built":"2026-11-23T12:21:13.084Z","pages":[{"id":"smile","href":"explainers/smile.html","title":"Who Keeps the Money? The Smile Curve of AI Chips","short":"Who keeps the money?","topic":"Semiconductors","category":"Chips & hardware","date":"2026-11-18","dek":"Eight companies, one chip, and why margin follows scarcity","sec":[{"id":"","h":"","t":"Investigation · Living document · Semiconductors Who Keeps the Money? The Smile Curve of AI Chips The world will spend about $1.5 trillion on chips this year. We followed one AI chip through eight companies to see who actually keeps it. The answer bends a thirty-year-old rule. The textbook smile (dashed) against the 2026 curve. Each dot is one step in making an AI chip, placed at the share of each dollar that step keeps. Graphic: Jun Wei Heung. By Jun Wei Heung · Words and graphics November 18, 2026 · 12 min read Copy link Sources This is an educational explainer, not investment advice. Margins are each company's latest reported quarter as of publication and will change with the next earnings season. Where a figure is a company's own claim, we say so. Key findings Global chip sales are forecast at about $1.51 trillion in 2026, up roughly 90% from under $800 billion in 2025. The classic \"smile curve\" says making things earns the least. In AI chips, the manufacturer in the middle, TSMC, keeps about 66 cents of each dollar in gross profit. Memory maker SK hynix went from 47 to 80 cents of operating profit per dollar in a year, and says the shortage lasts until 2030. The real low point is assembly: Foxconn keeps about 6 cents while building over 40% of AI servers, by its chairman's count. Margin follows scarcity, not position on the chain."},{"id":"s1","h":"Why everyone wants chips","t":"This year, the world will buy about $1.5 trillion worth of semiconductors. Last year it bought less than $800 billion. That is almost double, in a single year, according to the industry's own forecasters . A silicon wafer: hundreds of chips are printed on one disc, then cut apart. This one carries older Intel Pentium chips; today's AI chips are far larger. Photo: Naotake Murayama , CC BY 2.0 , via Wikimedia Commons. The chip market nearly doubled in a year Worldwide semiconductor sales, US dollars $0 2025 $0 2026 (forecast) +90% Memory, 2026 (above $800B) Everything else, 2026 All chips, 2025 Source: WSTS Spring 2026 forecast; 2025 sales of $795.6B. Memory's share is shown as about half the 2026 total. The reason is artificial intelligence. Every AI model is trained and run on chips, racked by the thousand inside data centres. More users, bigger models and more companies building them all point the same way: more chips. And the best ones are sold out. Memory alone is set to pass $800 billion, up about 250%. Nvidia's data-centre business made $89 billion in the single quarter that ended in July. So where does all that money go?"},{"id":"s2","h":"The smile curve","t":"In the early 1990s, Stan Shih, the founder of the Taiwanese computer maker Acer, drew a curve. Line up every step of making a product, from the first idea to the final customer, and plot how much profit each step keeps. The two ends are high. Ideas, brands and software are hard to copy. The middle is low: if you can build a factory, so can someone else. Drawn out, it looks like a smile, and for three decades it explained why designers and brands got rich while manufacturers fought over thin margins. \"If you can build a factory, so can someone else.\" The logic of the smile curve One chip, seven stops Each worker keeps a share of every dollar as the chip rides past. Illustration by Jun Wei Heung. Jar heights use the margins in the walk below: gross margins, except memory (SK hynix operating margin, Q3 2026). Margins measure different things, so read it as a sketch, not a ledger. Now let's walk it, for one AI chip. Scroll through the eight stops below. The picture shows what physically happens to the chip at each stop, and each dot lands at the share of every dollar that step keeps. The textbook This is the smile we were promised: high at the ends, low in the middle. Keep the dashed line in mind. Stop 1 · Chip design Someone has to design the chip. Nvidia, AMD and Broadcom draw the blueprints. They don't own factories. 75¢ Nvidia, gross margin Nvidia, AMD, Broadcom Stop 2 · EDA software To design a chip you need special software, called EDA. Two companies, Synopsys and Cadence, hold about 30% of the market each. Software is cheap to copy, for its owner. 88¢ Cadence, non-GAAP gross margin Synopsys, Cadence Stop 3 · Equipment The machines that print chips. Only ASML makes the EUV machines the newest chips need, and it says demand will outrun supply through 2028. 57¢ ASML, gross margin ASML, Applied Materials, Lam, TEL, KLA Stop 4 · Wafer fab Actually making the chips. This should be the bottom of the smile. But TSMC makes 72.5% of the world's contract-made chips. 66¢ TSMC, gross margin TSMC Stop 5 · Memory High-bandwidth memory is stacked right next to the chip to feed it data. A year ago, SK hynix kept 47 cents of operating profit per dollar. 47¢ SK hynix, Q3 2025 SK hynix, Samsung, Micron Stop 5 · Memory, one year later Now it's 80 cents, higher than any other step except chip-design software. SK hynix says the shortage lasts until 2030. 80¢ SK hynix, operating margin, Q3 2026 Stop 6 · Packaging Package the chip and its memory together. ASE, the biggest outside packager, keeps a fraction of what its neighbours do. 22¢ ASE, gross margin TSMC CoWoS, ASE Stop 7 · Rack assembly Bolt 72 chips into a server rack and ship it. Foxconn builds over 40% of AI servers, by its chairman's count. This is the real low point of the smile. 6¢ Foxconn, gross margin Foxconn, Quanta, Wistron Stop 8 · Software The last step is the one customers touch. Developers build on Nvidia's CUDA, so switching chips is painful. CUDA isn't sold on its own. It's why Nvidia's 75 cents stays 75 cents. CUDA (Nvidia again) Part 4 · The 2026 curve Put all the dots together. This isn't the smile we were promised. The middle has climbed: TSMC and SK hynix earn like idea companies. Why Because they're bottlenecks: sold out, booked for years, hard to copy. A new fab takes years and billions; so does a new memory line. The steps that are easier to add capacity to still earn little. Profit kept per dollar, by step From idea (left) to customer (right) Latest reported quarter per company. Gross margins, except SK hynix (operating margin)."},{"id":"s4","h":"The 2026 curve","t":"The textbook smile dips in the middle. The real 2026 curve does not. TSMC and SK hynix earn like idea companies because nobody can replace them quickly: a new fab takes years and tens of billions of dollars, and so does a new memory line. SK hynix says its long-term supply deals are booked into 2029. The true low point sits further right, at packaging and assembly, where adding capacity is easier and customers have more choices. Eight stops, one chip Share of each dollar kept, latest reported quarter Stop Company Kept Gross margin unless noted. SK hynix reports operating margin; its gross margin is higher. Cadence figure is non-GAAP. CUDA is not sold separately and is counted inside Nvidia's margin. Margin follows scarcity, not position."},{"id":"s5","h":"The takeaway","t":"So who keeps the money? Not whoever is first or last in line. Whoever the line can't do without. In 2026 that means ASML, TSMC and SK hynix as much as the designers at the top of the curve. Next time a chip stock jumps, ask two things. QUESTION 1 Where is it on the curve? QUESTION 2 And can anyone replace it?"},{"id":"sources","h":"Sources","t":"Each number and where it comes from. Several will change with the next earnings season; Nvidia reports its next quarter shortly after publication. Market size, $1.51T in 2026, +90%, memory above $800B: WSTS Spring 2026 forecast via TelecomLead ; 2025 sales of $795.6B: Hedge Fund Alpha Nvidia, $89.0B data-centre revenue, quarter ended July 2026: StorageNewsletter / Nvidia release ; 75% gross margin: Tom's Hardware , Zacks Cadence, 88% non-GAAP gross margin, Q1 2026: Zacks ; Cadence and Synopsys about 30% of EDA each: Nasdaq / MarketBeat ASML, 57.5% gross margin Q3 2026, supply short of demand in 2027–28: ASML Q3 2026 release (SEC 6-K) , StorageNewsletter TSMC, 66.4% gross margin Q3 2026: TrendForce ; 72.5% foundry share Q2 2026: Focus Taiwan SK hynix, 80% operating margin Q3 2026 vs 47% Q3 2025, shortage through 2030: Webull / SK hynix results , TechPowerUp ASE, 22.1% gross margin Q3 2026: Finviz / ASE release Foxconn, 6.18% gross margin Q1 2026: I-Connect007 ; over 40% of AI servers (chairman's claim): TVBS \"72 chips in a rack\" refers to Nvidia's NVL72 rack. The smile curve itself (Stan Shih, Acer, early 1990s) is general business history. Educational only. Not investment advice. This is a living document and will be updated as companies report."}]},{"id":"gpu-unlock","href":"explainers/gpu-unlock.html","title":"Unlocked, and Still Out of Reach: The Hidden Compute in Gaming GPUs","short":"Unlocked, and still out of reach","topic":"GPUs","category":"Chips & hardware","date":"2026-11-18","dek":"A compute unlock, a used-card rush, and why gamers lose the bidding war","sec":[{"id":"","h":"","t":"Explainer · Living document · GPUs Unlocked, and Still Out of Reach An open-source unlock nearly doubles the AI compute of RTX 30 and 40 graphics cards. Gamers get 0% more frames, and used prices keep climbing. Here's why the unlock helps the people who need it least. Peak dense BF16 matrix compute with FP32 accumulation, before and after the unlock, at stock power limits. Sources: gpu-unlock and gpu-unlock-ada project benchmarks (see Sources). Graphic: Jun Wei Heung. By Jun Wei Heung · Words and graphics November 18, 2026 · 10 min read Copy link Sources Performance figures come from the unlock projects' own benchmarks and user reports, plus one news write-up; they haven't been independently lab-tested. The wafer figures in Part 4 are my own estimate, built from published inputs plus two stated assumptions you can change. US-dollar prices are converted at about S$1.29 per US dollar (mid-November 2026), rounded. This explains what happened; it isn't a guide to modifying hardware, and it isn't investment advice. Key findings An open-source unlock lifts the RTX 3090 from 75 to 125 TFLOPS of dense BF16 matrix compute (+65%), and the RTX 4090 from 165 to 329 (about 2×), with no VBIOS flash and no patched driver. AI work speeds up by roughly 25–55% in user reports. Games and chatbot replies speed up by 0% . Used prices are going up, not down : a used RTX 3090 now averages about S$2,100 on eBay, up about 29% in 90 days and above its launch price. Nvidia made US$193.7 billion from data centres last fiscal year and US$16.0 billion from gaming. Gaming is about 7% of the business."},{"id":"s1","h":"What got unlocked","t":"The unlock doesn't overclock anything. According to its authors, there's no patched driver and no VBIOS flash , and the card's normal power management and throttling stay in place. What changes is the speed of one kind of matrix maths on the card's tensor cores. An RTX 3090, one of the cards the unlock works on (this one is a Gigabyte Eagle OC model). Photo: PantheraLeo1359531 , CC BY 4.0 , via Wikimedia Commons. GeForce RTX 30 and 40 cards run half-precision matrix maths that adds up its results in higher precision (FP16 or BF16 with FP32 accumulation) at half the rate of the same maths adding up in FP16. That's in Nvidia's own architecture whitepapers. It's a product decision, not a physical limit: the unlock projects describe GeForce tensor cores as capped to half speed for this path. Same silicon, different speed setting Illustrative RTX 3090. The part games use ships at full speed; one AI maths path is held back. As shipped Unlocked Games (shader & RT cores) No change AI maths (BF16, FP32 accumulate) 75 TFLOPS Power limit Same Dense BF16 with FP32 accumulation, stock power limit: 75 → 125 TFLOPS. Squares are illustrative, drawn in proportion. Source: gpu-unlock benchmark table . \"You paid for the whole flat. The master bedroom was locked.\" An analogy for product segmentation Picture a developer who builds one flat, sells it to companies at full price and to families for less, and puts an electronic lock on the master bedroom for the families. The room is there; the key isn't. One flat, sold twice The same floor plan for both buyers. Only one gets the key to the master bedroom. Use the unlock ↻ Replay An analogy, not a measurement. The master bedroom stands for the tensor cores' full-rate FP32-accumulate path; everything else, like the shader cores games use, is in both flats. How does the unlock open the door? Hardware Busters describes it as a firmware routine that runs before Nvidia's driver loads, flips the chip's \"speed-select\" setting, resets the card, and steps aside. The unmodified driver then takes over a card running at the full rate. It's Linux-only for now, and the authors list the RTX 30 series as stable with 100+ user reports and the RTX 40 series as experimental. Caveats from the projects themselves The setting is lost on sleep, so the unlock has to be re-applied on resume. The RTX 4090 runs out of power before it runs out of tensor cores, so large jobs at stock power gain much less than the doubled peak suggests: one owner's real-world matrix benchmarks gained 12–34% . The authors say outputs are bit-identical before and after, but none of this has been independently lab-tested."},{"id":"s2","h":"Who actually gains","t":"If your card nearly doubled overnight, you'd expect games to fly. They don't. Scroll through what user reports show for each kind of work. Same card, two desks Watch what happens when the lock comes off. Illustration by Jun Wei Heung. The 72 → 45 second image job is one RTX 3090 Ti user report (Hardware Busters); games are listed as \"no change\" by the unlock project. Animation sped up. AI images SDXL, SD 1.5 and similar image models in ComfyUI ran 30–55% faster. One RTX 3090 Ti job dropped from 72 to 45 seconds. The authors report bit-identical outputs, so the card isn't cutting corners to go faster. +30–55% Fine-tuning LoRA and full fine-tuning in BF16, the bread and butter of customising a model, ran 25–50% faster. +25–50% Long prompts Reading a long prompt in one go (prefill) is matrix-heavy work. It got 25–55% faster, and the wait for the first word of a reply fell by 25–30%. +25–55% AI video Video models like Wan 2.2 gained less, about 15–20%. +15–20% Compressed local models Most people running models at home use compressed (quantised) versions through llama.cpp or Ollama. Those mostly use other maths paths: 0–15%, and only at long context. INT8, FP8 and INT4 models: no change. 0–15% Games Rendering, lighting and ray tracing run on the shader and ray-tracing cores, which were never held back. The projects list gaming as \"no change\". 0% frame rate Chatting with a local model When a model types its reply word by word, each word means reading the whole model out of memory. The bottleneck is memory bandwidth, not compute: a supercar with its speed limiter removed, stuck on a single-lane road. 0% reply speed The pattern Every gain lands on work that's limited by matrix compute and runs in FP16 or BF16 with FP32 accumulation. Everything a gamer touches was never locked. Speed-up from the unlock, by task Gold: work limited by matrix compute · Grey: everything else User-reported ranges from the gpu-unlock project . Not independently benchmarked. The lock was cut around gamers, not through them. That's the real story: the segmentation was precise. Everything games use shipped at full speed. Only the path professionals rely on was held back. So when the lock came off, the people who gained were the ones who'd otherwise have bought a more expensive card."},{"id":"s3","h":"The used-card rush","t":"Plenty of gamers hoped this would break Nvidia's pricing. More compute for free should mean cheaper cards, right? The opposite is happening. Used RTX 3090s were already climbing on AI demand, with buyers after their 24 GB of memory, and the unlock gives AI buyers one more reason to want them. A six-year-old card now costs more than it did new Used RTX 3090, average eBay US asking price, Singapore dollars S$0 Sep 24, 2026 S$0 Nov 14, 2026 +29% in 90 days Launch price, 2020: US$1,499 (about S$1,930) US$1,463 ( Sep ) and US$1,626 across 313 listings ( Nov ), converted at about S$1.29 per US dollar. These are listing averages, not confirmed sales. The 29% is the tracker's 90-day change. Size check: an RTX 3090 (top) and an RTX 4090 (bottom), the two generations in this story. Photo: Benlisquare , CC BY-SA 4.0 , via Wikimedia Commons. Modding shops in China went further, soldering extra memory onto gaming cards so that bigger models fit on one card, and fitting them with server-style blower coolers. Gaming cards, rebuilt for AI Memory-upgraded cards reported on sale Card Stock memory Modded · reported price RTX 4090 (blower, memory on both sides) 24 GB 48 GB · S$4,400–7,100 RTX 4080 16 GB 32 GB Sources: Tom's Hardware (48 GB teardown), TweakTown (US$3,400), 3DTested (up to US$5,500), TweakTown (4080 32 GB). Grey-market cards; prices vary by cooler and date. Why would an unlock that adds no frames push prices up? Because the card's buyer changed. A gamer asks whether a card buys enough evenings of fun, and walks away if not. For someone doing AI work, the card is a machine that earns: it replaces cloud rental fees, takes commercial work, and can be rented out by the hour. Tell that buyer the same old card now does up to twice the work, and they'll outbid every gamer in the market."},{"id":"s4","h":"The wafer math","t":"Maybe you skip the used market and wait for new cards to get cheaper. They probably won't, and the reason is a choice Nvidia makes with every wafer it buys from TSMC: data centre or gaming? Last fiscal year, data centres brought in US$193.7 billion and gaming US$16.0 billion. Nvidia was projected to take about 77% of the wafers made for AI chips in 2025. One wafer, two destinies Estimate · see assumptions What one 300 mm TSMC wafer could earn Nvidia, cut into data-centre B200s or into RTX 4090 chips. Move the sliders below; the bars update. B200s · revenue per wafer – B200s · gross profit per wafer – RTX 4090 chips · revenue per wafer – Data centre (B200) Gaming (RTX 4090 chip, sold to board makers like ASUS and MSI) Input Value Basis Wafer 300 mm, 3 mm edge excluded Standard chips-per-wafer method ( Silicon Analysts ) B200 2 chips of ~830 mm² Reported 814–850 mm² ( PCGH / Morgan Stanley ) B200 price · cost US$30–40K · ~US$6,400 Analyst estimate ( FourWeekMBA ); cost includes memory and packaging RTX 4090 chip (AD102) 608 mm² 608.44 mm² ( Geektopia, citing AnandTech ) Share of chips that work 80% Assumption. Not published; applied to both What Nvidia gets per 4090 chip US$400 Assumption. Not published Gross chips per wafer = π(D/2−E)²/S − π(D−2E)/√(2S), with D = 300 mm, E = 3 mm, S = chip area. Gaming profit per wafer isn't shown because Nvidia doesn't publish a margin for it. A B200's price also pays for memory and packaging Nvidia buys in, so revenue per wafer flatters the data-centre side somewhat. Converted at about S$1.29 per US dollar. Change the assumptions The B200 price range is reported; the other two sliders are guesses, so try the extremes. B200 selling price US$35,000 Share of chips that work (assumption) 80% What Nvidia gets per RTX 4090 chip (assumption) US$400 Working chips per wafer – Data centre vs gaming, revenue per wafer – Revenue given up per gaming chip – Revenue given up per gaming chip = (B200 revenue per wafer − 4090 revenue per wafer) ÷ working 4090 chips per wafer. It's the data-centre revenue Nvidia forgoes each time a wafer becomes gaming chips instead. On middle-of-the-range assumptions, a wafer cut into B200s brings in roughly 30 times the revenue of the same wafer cut into RTX 4090 chips. Economists measure the true cost of a choice by the best thing you gave up. By that measure, every gaming chip Nvidia makes costs it thousands of dollars in data-centre sales it didn't make: roughly S$15,000 at the default settings above. Then there's the floor under the price. Shrinking transistors no longer makes them cheaper: TSMC's 2nm wafers are reportedly priced around US$30,000, about 50% more than 3nm , and analysts argue that at the leading edge, smaller no longer means cheaper . New flagship cards are built on a cost base that only goes up."},{"id":"s5","h":"How we got here","t":"There's an irony here: the customers now priced out are the ones who carried Nvidia's AI platform in the first place. 2006 Nvidia unveils CUDA, letting graphics cards do general-purpose computing. Jensen Huang has said it was then a company with a 35% gross margin , and the bet raised costs while margins shrank . 35% 2008 Through the financial crisis and earnings misses, Nvidia's stock falls about 80% from December 2007 to December 2008 . −80% Who carried it CUDA shipped on every GeForce card, so gamers' purchases put it in front of developers and researchers. Huang has credited GeForce with carrying CUDA to the people who built today's AI on it. 2025–26 Nvidia becomes the first company worth US$5 trillion (about S$6.5 trillion). In fiscal 2026 it makes US$215.9 billion (about S$279 billion). Gaming is US$16.0 billion of it. 7% The unlock changes what an old card can do. It doesn't change who the card is worth most to. As long as a wafer is worth far more as AI chips, and an old card is worth more to someone earning money with it, gamers will keep losing the bidding war. Next time a hardware \"unlock\" makes headlines, ask two things. QUESTION 1 Is the work limited by compute, or by memory? QUESTION 2 And who else wants this chip more than you?"},{"id":"sources","h":"Sources","t":"Each number and where it comes from. How the unlock works, supported cards, RTX 30-series before/after (RTX 3090: 75 → 125 TFLOPS BF16), user-reported gains by task, \"no change\" for gaming, decode and INT8/FP8/INT4, bit-identical outputs, sleep/resume caveat: Zagitta/gpu-unlock (project README; author and user reports). RTX 4090 before/after (165 → 329–331 TFLOPS), owner-run real GEMM gains of 12–34%, power draw: JustAResearcher/gpu-unlock-ada (project README). News write-up, mechanism (\"speed-select\" flip, function-level reset), RTX 3090 Ti +80%, ComfyUI 72 → 45 seconds: Hardware Busters, Oct 25, 2026 . Half-rate FP32 accumulation on GeForce tensor cores: Nvidia Ampere GA102 architecture whitepaper ; RTX 3090 tensor peaks also in Wccftech's RTX 3090 review . Used RTX 3090 prices (US$1,463 in September, US$1,626 in November 2026, +29% over 90 days): OpenClawDC price tracker ; see also MindStudio on AI demand. RTX 3090 launch price US$1,499: Wccftech . Modded cards: Tom's Hardware , TweakTown (US$3,400) , 3DTested (up to US$5,500) , TweakTown (4080 32 GB) . Nvidia fiscal 2026 (year to January 2026): revenue US$215.9B, data centre US$193.7B, gaming US$16.0B (7.4%): Nvidia Q4 and fiscal 2026 results . First US$5 trillion company, October 29, 2025: AP via Yahoo Finance . Wafer calculation inputs: B200 chip size and Nvidia’s 77% share of AI wafers: PC Games Hardware (Morgan Stanley) ; B200 price and cost: FourWeekMBA (an analyst estimate); AD102 size: Geektopia, citing AnandTech ; chips-per-wafer method: Silicon Analysts . Share of working chips and Nvidia’s price per gaming chip are my assumptions, adjustable in the calculator. Rising cost at leading-edge nodes: SemiWiki , Simon-Kucher (both report or argue; wafer prices are media estimates). Nvidia history: OnMSFT (35% gross margin, Huang interview), Wccftech (CUDA on GeForce), Stratrix (−80%, Dec 2007–Dec 2008; other measures put calendar 2008 at about −76%). Currency: about S$1.29 per US dollar, mid-November 2026 mid-market ( currency.wiki ). Figures are rounded, so they move with the exchange rate. Educational only. Not investment advice and not a guide to modifying hardware. This is a living document and will be updated as figures are checked."}]},{"id":"stargate","href":"explainers/stargate.html","title":"The Gate That Didn't Open: How Stargate Shrank","short":"The gate that didn't open","topic":"AI infrastructure","category":"AI & capital","date":"2026-11-19","dek":"A half-trillion pledge, a debt mismatch, and who ends up holding the buildings","sec":[{"id":"","h":"","t":"Explainer · Living document · AI infrastructure The Gate That Didn't Open In January 2025, four men stood in the White House and promised US$500 billion of AI data centres. Twenty-two months later, one Texas campus is running, the joint venture behind the name has barely started, and the building happens under other contracts. This is how the biggest number in tech shrank. Squares drawn to scale by power capacity. Target and planned capacity: OpenAI, September 2025. Running capacity: an independent tracker's estimate for Abilene, September 2026, not a company figure. Sources below. Graphic: Jun Wei Heung. By Jun Wei Heung · Words and graphics November 19, 2026 · 13 min read Copy link Sources \"Stargate\" means three things: a joint venture (Stargate LLC), a brand OpenAI puts on its data-centre deals, and a political announcement. I count something as Stargate if OpenAI or its partners called it that in public. Much of the 2026 reporting comes from Bloomberg, The Information and the Wall Street Journal; where I've relied on write-ups of their reporting, I say \"reportedly\". Site status comes from company statements where they exist and independent trackers where they don't. US-dollar figures are converted at about S$1.29 per US dollar (mid-November 2026), rounded. This explains what happened; it isn't investment advice. Key findings Stargate launched in January 2025 as a US$500 billion (about S$645 billion), four-year pledge from OpenAI, SoftBank, Oracle and MGX, with US$100 billion said to be available immediately. By September 2025 it had grown on paper to nearly 7 GW of planned capacity and over US$400 billion across six US sites, with a 10 GW target. By February 2026 the joint venture itself reportedly had no staff and no data centres under construction . Most real building ran through separate OpenAI–Oracle and OpenAI–SoftBank deals. In spring 2026, an Abilene expansion was dropped , Stargate UK was paused and Stargate Norway went to Microsoft . OpenAI says it has still secured over 8 GW of compute, mostly outside the joint venture. The Abilene flagship was due to be finished by mid-2026 . By November 2026, trackers count four to six of its eight buildings live."},{"id":"s1","h":"The promise","t":"21 January 2025, the Roosevelt Room Four men, one podium, one very large number. Illustration by Jun Wei Heung, based on news photos of the announcement. Left to right: Donald Trump, Masayoshi Son (SoftBank), Larry Ellison (Oracle) and Sam Altman (OpenAI). Not a likeness. The day after his inauguration, Donald Trump stood in the Roosevelt Room beside Sam Altman of OpenAI, Masayoshi Son of SoftBank and Larry Ellison of Oracle, and announced the largest AI infrastructure project in history. The name started as an internal code name: Altman has said an early data-centre layout looked like the gate from the Stargate TV series. The number was US$500 billion over four years. No government money was involved. The cash was meant to come from the companies themselves, with SoftBank in charge of the finances and OpenAI in charge of operations. Abu Dhabi's MGX came in as a fourth backer. Even the number grew in the room. Son later told Bloomberg that after the election he had promised Trump US$100 billion . Trump told him to go for 200. He came back with 500. Asked how it added up, Altman said it covered the capacity OpenAI expected to need for the next few years, and admitted he wasn't sure even a trillion dollars could be spent profitably that fast. Within a day, people were asking whether the money existed. Elon Musk posted that the backers \"don't actually have the money\" . Microsoft's Satya Nadella, whose company was missing from the stage, had a shorter answer . \"I'm good for my $80 billion.\" Satya Nadella, Microsoft, January 2025 An analogy: the engagement announcement Picture a couple announcing their engagement before agreeing where to live, whose name goes on the deed, or who pays the mortgage. The announcement is real. Everything that makes it a household is still to be negotiated. Stargate in January 2025 was the announcement."},{"id":"s2","h":"The land grab","t":"Why so big? Because OpenAI kept running out of chips. When ChatGPT's new image generator went viral in March 2025, the Studio Ghibli craze, Altman said the company had to borrow computing power from its own researchers and slow down other features to keep up. His argument for building anyway: if AI cost a tenth as much, people would use it twenty times as much, so OpenAI would still need twice the compute. For most of 2025, Stargate looked unstoppable. Scroll through the year it grew. Abilene, Texas. The first Stargate campus is being built on open land outside the city, not in the downtown shown here. Photo: Michael Barera , CC BY-SA 4.0 , via Wikimedia Commons. January 2025 · Abilene, Texas The flagship existed before the name did. Crusoe started building on 1,200 acres of bare scrubland in June 2024. The plan, as Crusoe described it : eight buildings, up to 400,000 Nvidia chips, 1.2 GW of power and about 2,200 workers on site, all finished by mid-2026. Its nickname was \"Project Ludicrous\". US$500B pledged July 2025 · The Oracle deal OpenAI agrees to take up to 4.5 GW of extra capacity from Oracle, a contract reportedly worth about US$300 billion . It's one of the largest cloud contracts ever signed. 4.5 GW 23 September 2025 · Five new sites Shackelford County, Texas. Doña Ana County, New Mexico. A Midwest site. Lordstown, Ohio. Milam County, Texas. Picked from over 300 proposals in more than 30 states, OpenAI says . ~7 GW planned Late 2025 · \"Ahead of schedule\" OpenAI says the sites add up to over US$400 billion of investment over three years, and that it's ahead of schedule to lock in the full 10 GW and US$500 billion by the end of 2025. US$400B+ Going global Stargate UAE, Stargate Norway and Stargate UK are announced with local partners. The name stops being one project and becomes a franchise. Stargate on paper Grey outline: the pledge · Gold: what's been announced so far Announced and planned figures from OpenAI and partners. Planned is not built. The name was everywhere. The company behind it barely existed."},{"id":"s3","h":"The power problem","t":"Chips are only half of a data centre. The other half is electricity, and AI needs far more of it than anything that came before. Crusoe's founder, Chase Lochmiller, told Bloomberg that power has become \"the major bottleneck\" . One rack of servers, then and now Power budgeted for a single rack, kilowatts Typical data-centre rack, about 20 years ago 2–4 kW Latest Nvidia AI rack, as Crusoe budgets it 130 kW 1.2 GW Abilene's planned power supply: a 200 MW substation, then a 1 GW one. Roughly what 750,000 homes use, by Bloomberg's comparison. Rack figures and site plan: Crusoe's Chase Lochmiller, interviewed by Bloomberg Originals (2025). The 2–4 kW bar is drawn at 3 kW. Inside a data centre: every rack like these draws power around the clock. A generic data centre, not a Stargate site. Photo: Brett Sayles / Pexels . That's why Stargate went to West Texas. Abilene sits among wind farms that produce more power than the area uses, so a data centre there buys cheap electricity that would otherwise go to waste. Even so, Crusoe is building a gas power plant on site as backup. The cooling system is closed-loop: it is filled with about a million gallons of water once, instead of evaporating that much every day. Every gigawatt a site like this adds lands on a power grid that is already stretched, and not every country has spare wind. When OpenAI later paused Stargate UK, high energy costs were the first reason it gave."},{"id":"s4","h":"Who was paying?","t":"Here's the part the press conference skipped. A data centre this size costs tens of billions of dollars before it earns anything. Somebody has to borrow that money, and lenders want to know who pays it back. Follow the money How a Stargate site gets paid for, simplified. Every arrow points back to one source of cash. Crusoe and other developers Build the shell and power, then lease it to Oracle Oracle Fills it with Nvidia chips using borrowed money, then rents the compute to OpenAI OpenAI Uses the compute, and loses money every year Subscriptions and API sales The only cash that pays for all of it The landlord signed for longer than the tenant Contract lengths as reported. Years from the start of service. Oracle's data-centre leases ~15 years OpenAI's contract with Oracle ~5 years Who pays for years 6–15? Year 0 5 10 15 Simplified: real deals involve more lenders, landlords and chip suppliers. Contract lengths are as reported by analysts and press, not from published contracts; see Luminix and Startup Fortune . That mismatch is the whole risk in one picture. If OpenAI's revenue grows as fast as it hopes, everyone gets paid. If it doesn't, Oracle is left with buildings full of chips and a lot of debt. Lenders noticed. The price of insuring Oracle's debt against default climbed to a record in July 2026, roughly four times its level a year earlier. What lenders charge to insure Oracle's debt Five-year credit default swap spread, basis points (1 bp = 0.01% a year) 0 bp Mid-2025 (approx.) 0 bp 17 July 2026 ≈4× · record 198.23 bp on 17 July 2026, a record, and \"roughly quadrupling since mid-2025\": Bloomberg data reported by Startup Fortune . The mid-2025 bar is implied from that and is approximate. A rising spread means lenders are more nervous; it doesn't mean a default is coming. An analogy: the landlord who bought the furniture Imagine a landlord who takes out a huge loan to buy a building and fill it with expensive furniture, because one tenant promised to rent it. The tenant is famous, growing fast and losing money every year. The landlord's whole plan rests on that one tenant staying. One group gets paid up front, whatever happens next: the chipmakers. Bloomberg's reporter on the story put Abilene's 400,000 chips at a conservative US$15–20 billion , most of it to Nvidia. And the town took a risk too. To win the project, Abilene agreed to waive 85% of the property tax on the data centre. The mayor's view was that 15% of billions is still a lot of new money for roads and police. No one would give him a firm number for how many permanent jobs it would create."},{"id":"s5","h":"The stall","t":"A year after the White House event, The Information reported that the Stargate joint venture had hired no one and wasn't developing any data centres itself. The partners had reportedly spent months arguing over who would own and control the sites . The building that did happen mostly ran around the joint venture: direct contracts between OpenAI and Oracle, and between OpenAI and SoftBank's energy arm. Stargate the brand kept going. Stargate the company barely started. Then, within a few weeks in spring 2026, the retreats began. The warnings were there in 2025 In January 2025, China's DeepSeek showed a leading AI model could be built with far less computing power, and people started asking whether projects this big were needed at all. Microsoft paused or cancelled some of its own data-centre leases. Tariffs raised the cost of the steel, aluminium and chips a data centre is made of. OpenAI had lost about US$5 billion in 2024. And when Bloomberg asked Altman what could go wrong, he named it himself: that people might stop wanting to pay for AI services . March 2026 Abilene expansion dropped Oracle and OpenAI scrap a planned add-on next to the flagship after talks over financing and OpenAI's changing demand forecasts break down, Bloomberg reports . The existing campus keeps building. Dropped 9 April 2026 Stargate UK paused OpenAI puts the UK project on hold , citing energy costs and regulation. It says it will move ahead \"when the right conditions\" are in place. Paused 14 April 2026 Norway goes to Microsoft Microsoft takes over the capacity at Nscale's Narvik site that was meant for OpenAI. OpenAI says it will reach Norway through Microsoft's cloud instead. Handed over 2026 The spending target shrinks OpenAI reportedly tells investors it's aiming for about US$600 billion of compute spending by 2030 . Months earlier, Altman had talked about US$1.4 trillion of commitments over eight years. The two aren't measured the same way, but the direction is clear. Cut 2026 The money question goes internal The Wall Street Journal reports that OpenAI missed internal revenue and user targets , and that its finance chief worried about paying for future compute contracts. Altman and the CFO call the report's framing \"ridiculous\". It is close to the risk Altman named a year earlier. Disputed Nobody cancelled Stargate. It got smaller every time someone had to sign a cheque."},{"id":"s6","h":"What's left standing","t":"Here's every site that has carried the Stargate name, and where it stands as far as public sources show. Filter by status. Stargate, site by site Status as of November 2026. \"Announced\" means no confirmed public update on building. All Running or building Pulled back Site Partner Status Abilene, Texas Flagship, due to be finished by mid-2026. Trackers count four to six of its eight buildings live. Oracle, Crusoe Running (partly) Milam County, Texas SoftBank owns and develops it; OpenAI designs it and leases it long-term. SoftBank (SB Energy) Building Lordstown, Ohio The least public information of any US site. SoftBank Building Shackelford County, Texas Oracle Announced Doña Ana County, New Mexico Oracle Announced Midwest site Location not named at announcement. Oracle Announced Stargate UAE G42, MGX Announced Abilene expansion Planned add-on dropped, March 2026. Oracle Dropped Stargate UK On hold since April 2026. Nscale, Nvidia Paused Stargate Norway (Narvik) Capacity taken by Microsoft, April 2026. Nscale Handed over Sources: OpenAI (site list), Stargate.how tracker (build status), Tom's Hardware (Milam terms), plus the reports in Part 5. Trackers and companies disagree; I'll update this table as sites report. Start with the one site that's real. In 2025, Crusoe said all eight Abilene buildings would be done by mid-2026. That date has passed, and independent trackers count four to six of them live. That's fast by normal standards, and a long way short of ludicrous. OpenAI's own scorecard looks very different. In April 2026 it said it had secured more than 8 GW towards its 10 GW US goal, with over 3 GW added in 90 days. Both accounts can be true. OpenAI is getting compute, just not mainly through the venture announced at the White House. So Stargate didn't fall the way companies usually fall. There was no bankruptcy and no single cancellation. What collapsed was the idea that one joint venture, with one headline number, would build America's AI backbone. What replaced it is messier: dozens of separate leases, loans and rental deals, each priced by lenders who care about one thing, whether OpenAI's revenue can cover the bill. There were two bottlenecks all along: the power to run the chips, and someone willing to carry the debt for them."},{"id":"s7","h":"How we got here","t":"Jun 2024 Crusoe starts building on bare scrubland in Abilene, Texas, before the Stargate name exists. 21 Jan 2025 Stargate is announced at the White House by OpenAI, SoftBank, Oracle and MGX. US$500B 22 Jan 2025 Musk: \"They don't actually have the money.\" Nadella: \"I'm good for my $80 billion.\" Jan–Apr 2025 DeepSeek rattles the industry, Microsoft pauses some data-centre leases, tariffs push up building costs, and the Ghibli craze leaves OpenAI short of GPUs. Jul 2025 OpenAI agrees to take up to 4.5 GW of extra capacity from Oracle, reportedly worth about US$300 billion. 4.5 GW 23 Sep 2025 Five new US sites. Stargate stands at nearly 7 GW and over US$400 billion, on paper. ~7 GW Feb 2026 Reports say the joint venture has no staff and isn't building anything itself. Mar 2026 The Abilene expansion is dropped. Apr 2026 Stargate UK is paused and Stargate Norway goes to Microsoft. OpenAI says it has secured over 8 GW anyway. 8 GW+ Mid-2026 Abilene's promised finish date passes with four to six of eight buildings live. 4–6 of 8 Jul 2026 Insuring Oracle's debt costs a record 198 basis points a year. 198 bp Nov 2026 You are here. Next time someone announces a number this big, ask two things. QUESTION 1 Who has actually signed to borrow the money? QUESTION 2 And whose revenue pays it back?"},{"id":"sources","h":"Sources","t":"Each number and where it comes from. Announcement, US$500 billion over four years, private funding: CBS News, Jan 21, 2025 ; MGX and US$100 billion \"immediately\": VentureBeat . Musk and Nadella reactions: TweakTown , Yahoo News , VentureBeat . Five new sites, nearly 7 GW, over US$400 billion, 300 proposals, 10 GW target: OpenAI, Sep 23, 2025 ; Bloomberg . Oracle's 4.5 GW and about US$300 billion: SCMP . Joint venture stall and partner disputes (reporting by The Information): The Decoder , Tom's Hardware . Abilene expansion dropped (reporting by Bloomberg): Tom's Hardware , Enverus . Stargate UK paused: Semafor, Apr 9, 2026 ; The National . Stargate Norway to Microsoft: Bloomberg, Apr 14, 2026 ; CNBC . US$600 billion compute target versus US$1.4 trillion of commitments: Techzine , CNBC . Missed targets and CFO concerns (reporting by the WSJ): DatacenterDynamics . Over 8 GW secured, 3 GW in 90 days: OpenAI, Apr 29, 2026 . Oracle credit default swaps (198.23 bp, Jul 17, 2026): Bloomberg data via Startup Fortune . Lease-length mismatch and OpenAI concentration: Luminix (analysis, not company disclosure). Site status, including Abilene running capacity (about 618 MW by September 2026): Stargate.how tracker ; background: Wikipedia, Stargate LLC . Tracker figures are estimates and conflict with each other. Abilene plan (June 2024 start, 1,200 acres, eight buildings, up to 400,000 chips, 1.2 GW, 2,200 workers, mid-2026 target), rack power (2–4 kW then, 130 kW now), on-site gas backup, closed-loop cooling, 85% property-tax abatement, the name's origin, Son's US$100B → 200 → 500 account, the Ghibli compute crunch, Altman's view of the risks, the US$15–20 billion chip estimate and OpenAI's 2024 loss: Bloomberg Originals video report from the Abilene site (2025) . These are 2025 statements by people involved in the project. Currency: about S$1.29 per US dollar, mid-November 2026 mid-market. Figures are rounded. Educational only. Not investment advice. This is a living document and will be updated as sites report and figures are checked."}]},{"id":"glass-cloth","href":"explainers/glass-cloth.html","title":"The Cloth Inside the Chip: One Japanese Mill and AI's Quietest Shortage","short":"The cloth inside the chip","topic":"Advanced packaging","category":"Chips & hardware","date":"2026-11-19","dek":"A woven glass sheet, a 1918 textile firm, and a shortage nobody sees","sec":[{"id":"","h":"","t":"Explainer · Living document · Advanced packaging The Cloth Inside the Chip Under every advanced AI processor sits a sheet of woven glass thinner than paper. One Japanese textile company, founded as a silk spinner in 1918, reportedly makes about 90% of the best grade. Nvidia, Microsoft, Google and Amazon are reportedly queuing for it. 100 squares stand for 100% of T-glass supply; 90 are gold. Share is a TrendForce estimate, also reported by Tom's Hardware; it is not a company figure. Lead-time figure is for copper-clad laminate, per Tom's Hardware. Sources below. Graphic: Jun Wei Heung. By Jun Wei Heung · Words and graphics November 19, 2026 · 11 min read Copy link Sources This is about one material, glass cloth, and mostly one company, Nitto Boseki (\"Nittobo\"). Nittobo's share figures come from industry researchers (chiefly TrendForce) and trade press, not from audited filings, so I say \"reportedly\" or \"an estimate\" where that applies. Its expansion plans come from the company's own August 2025 filing. Where sources disagree on dates or prices, I give the range. Yen and US-dollar figures are converted at the mid-November 2026 rates of about ¥159 and S$1.29 per US dollar, and are rounded. This explains an industry; it isn't investment advice. Key findings Advanced AI chips sit on a package substrate whose core is resin reinforced with woven glass cloth . The grade that keeps big, hot packages flat is called T-glass . TrendForce estimates Nittobo holds about 90% of T-glass and 60–70% of the next grade, NER glass . Trade press calls it the only firm able to make the top grade stably at scale. Nittobo is spending about ¥15 billion (about US$94 million, S$121 million) on a new Fukushima building that could, on its own filing's terms, take output to roughly three times today's level. Relief is not expected before mid-2027 , and some forecasters say 2028. Meanwhile, laminate lead times reportedly stretched from 8–10 weeks to over 20 , and Nvidia reportedly went to the cloth maker directly. That is unusual this far upstream."},{"id":"s1","h":"The cloth nobody sees","t":"Start with why you've never heard of it. Glass cloth is a component of a component of a component. It is woven into the core of a substrate, which holds a chip package, which sits on a board, which goes into a server. Nobody buys it. Nobody puts it on a slide. It doesn't show up in a teardown photo unless you slice the package open. It is also a small business. BofA analyst Takashi Enomoto estimates Nittobo's whole electronic-materials segment made about ¥40.9 billion (about US$257 million, S$331 million) in 2025, according to Tom's Hardware . Nvidia's fiscal-2026 revenue was US$215.9 billion . Different year-ends, so treat it as rough, but that is about one-thousandth. A very small supplier is holding up a very large industry. From sand to sheet How glass cloth is made, simplified. The furnace temperature is from Tom's Hardware. Melt Silica-rich glass in an electric furnace at 1,600–1,700°C Spin Molten glass is drawn into fine fibres, then twisted into yarn Weave Yarn goes on looms and becomes ultrathin cloth Soak Cloth is impregnated with resin to make sheets for substrates and boards Core The stiff middle layer of a chip substrate Melting, spinning and weaving: Tom's Hardware . Resin step: MPBxChange describes laminates and prepreg made with glass cloth. Steps simplified by me. An analogy: rebar in concrete Resin on its own is like unreinforced concrete: fine until it heats up and moves. Woven glass is the steel mesh inside. Without it, the slab bends. T-glass is the premium mesh, used when the slab is huge and the load is extreme."},{"id":"s2","h":"Inside the package","t":"Here is where the cloth sits. Scroll through the layers of a simplified AI accelerator, from the chip you've heard of down to the layer you haven't. Layer 1 · The chip The processor and its stacked high-bandwidth memory. This is the layer with the famous names and the big margins. It cannot work alone. Layer 2 · The interposer A slab of silicon that wires the processor to its memory. Nvidia's data show interposers growing from 814 mm² in Hopper to 1,700 mm² in Blackwell, per Tom's Hardware . 814 → 1,700 mm² Layer 3 · The substrate core Underneath is an organic substrate. Its core is resin reinforced with woven glass cloth. For big, hot AI packages, that cloth is T-glass, with low thermal expansion. This is Nittobo's territory. ~90% T-glass share, est. Layer 4 · The board Below that, the circuit board carries signals at high speed. Low-dielectric glass cloth helps there. Nittobo says its version has gone into AI-server switch boards since summer 2023. TrendForce estimates its share of that NER grade at 60–70%. Why it matters · Heat Run the chip and everything heats. Silicon barely expands; plain resin expands a lot. Where materials disagree, the package bends. A warped substrate can crack tiny solder joints, so yield falls. T-glass is there to stop the bend. Anatomy of an AI package Highlighted layer follows the story Schematic only. Layer roles from Tom's Hardware and Nittobo's August 2025 filing ; not to scale. The most advanced chip on Earth still depends on a woven sheet to stay flat."},{"id":"s3","h":"Why bigger chips need it","t":"Nobody needed to think about glass cloth when chips were small. Cheap E-glass, the standard grade, is used in lower-end parts, such as microcontrollers and older mobile processors. The trouble is that AI chips keep growing, and a bigger package has more room to warp. The interposer doubled Interposer area in Nvidia's accelerators, square millimetres 814 Hopper 1,700 Blackwell +109% Nvidia data as cited by Yole Group's Bilal Hachemi in Tom's Hardware , March 2026. The same article says later generations are expected to grow further. Only two data points are published here, so only two bars are drawn. Substrate makers run on thin margins, says Hachemi, so a modest rise in demand can tip them into shortage. Then there is the second effect: newer racks reportedly use more layers and more boards. TrendForce points to Nvidia's Rubin generation, whose substrate has seen \"significant increases in both area and layer count\", and to cable-free rack designs that need extra midplanes and backplanes. So each package needs more cloth, and there are more packages. Demand grows on both axes. Supply, as the next part shows, grows on neither."},{"id":"s4","h":"One company, one town","t":"Nittobo is a century-old Japanese company. According to a Wikipedia entry and a company-history summary, it was founded in April 1918 as a silk spinner in the city of Fukushima, and by 1938 it had become the first in Japan to industrialise glass fibre. It is now headquartered in Tokyo, but the plant that matters for this story is still in Fukushima. Fukushima City, where Nittobo was founded in 1918 and where the plant in this story still operates. Photo: Purplepumpkins , CC BY-SA 3.0 , via Wikimedia Commons. One mill, one long queue A century-old textile firm weaves the cloth; the biggest names in tech wait outside. Illustration by Jun Wei Heung. Customers shown are those reported to be seeking supply (see Sources); figures are generic, not real people. Who makes the cloth? Nittobo's estimated share of global supply, by grade T-glass (low thermal expansion) ~90% NER glass (low dielectric) 60–70% TrendForce estimate, April 2026 ( TrendForce ); also reported by Tom's Hardware and MPBxChange . The 60–70% bar is drawn solid to 60% with the range shaded. These are analyst estimates, and I found no audited company figure. Why can't others just copy it? According to Tom's Hardware, Nittobo has a proprietary glass composition and a direct-melting production method that rivals have struggled to replicate. Taiwan Glass, Grace Fabric and several Chinese makers are trying, but low yields have so far deterred meaningful entry. A November 2025 TrendForce news item, citing Taiwanese press, calls Nittobo the only company able to stably mass-produce top-grade T-glass. A recipe anyone can read is not a moat. A recipe that only works at scale in one factory is. An analogy: the one bakery with the sourdough starter Anyone can buy flour and an oven. What's hard is the culture that took decades to keep alive and the recipe that makes the loaf rise the same way every day. Competitors have the same ingredients. They don't yet have the same yield."},{"id":"s5","h":"The queue","t":"When one maker supplies almost everything, demand doesn't compete on price first. It competes on place in line. Nikkei, as relayed by TrendForce, reported that Nvidia, Microsoft, Google and Amazon had booked most of Nittobo's premium cloth capacity for 2026. Yole's Hachemi told Tom's Hardware that Nvidia contacting an upstream supplier like Nittobo directly is unprecedented in this part of the chain. The wait for laminate Lead time for copper-clad laminate (the sheet glass cloth goes into), weeks Normal 8–10 weeks 2026, T-glass tight over 20 weeks 20–30% Reported price rise for the material. A Yuanta analyst said suppliers \"are no longer providing lead times\". Tom's Hardware , March 2026. Bars drawn at 9 weeks and 20 weeks; the second is a floor (\"beyond 20\"). These are laminate figures, not glass-cloth-only. The squeeze has spread beyond T-glass. MPBxChange's roundup collects supply-chain reports that thin E-glass styles, such as 1080 and 2116, were the tightest by late October, with a survey cited by Commercial Times putting the cumulative rise in style 1080 cloth at about 300% from low to high. Glass cloth is about 19% of laminate cost, per TrendForce, so price rises feed through. The same roundup says weaving looms are a binding constraint: global output of the relevant looms is about 2,400 a year, possibly 3,600 in 2027, according to supply-chain sources. Treat those as reported, unaudited figures."},{"id":"s6","h":"The fix, and why it's slow","t":"Nittobo is building. On 29 August 2025 its board approved a new two-storey building at its Fukushima Enterprise Center, with new equipment, for about ¥15 billion (about US$94 million, S$121 million). Japan's trade ministry is expected to chip in up to about ¥2.4 billion (about US$15 million, S$19 million) under a plan to ensure T-glass supply. Construction runs October 2025 to December 2026, with production due to start in the fourth quarter of fiscal 2026, meaning January to March 2027. The promise, in multiples Nittobo's T-glass output: today versus the best case from the new building 1× Current level ~3× If all new capacity goes to T-glass conditional Nittobo's own wording ( filing, 29 Aug 2025 ): \"If all the expanded capacity goes to T-glass cloth for cutting-edge logic IC applications, output could reach roughly three times the current level.\" Secondary sources say the aim is about three times the year-to-March-2026 level by the year to March 2029; see MPBxChange . A target, not output. It is also adding a second source for the weaving step. Nan Ya Plastics, part of Taiwan's Formosa Plastics Group, said in November 2025 it would weave about 20% of the specialty glass cloth Nittobo supplies by 2027, with Nittobo supplying the yarn. Tom's Hardware adds that Nittobo is doubling raw-yarn capacity at a Taiwan plant. Note what this does and doesn't do: more weavers help, but the key know-how, the glass itself, stays with Nittobo. The relief date keeps slipping into the future. Yole's Hachemi expects the imbalance to last until the second half of 2027 at the earliest. MPBxChange's roundup cites two European laminate suppliers expecting no significant easing in glass fabric before 2028. Meanwhile demand is moving too: JPMorgan, per Tom's Hardware, reports rising demand for ultra-thin T-glass, a harder product to make. What the bottleneck is worth Nittobo electronic-materials sales, ¥ billion: reported 2025 vs a BofA forecast ¥40.9B 2025 (about S$331M) ¥87.7B By March 2028, forecast (about S$710M) BofA's Takashi Enomoto, via Tom's Hardware . The 2028 bar is a single analyst's estimate, shown lighter. The same note expects operating margins to approach 48%. Conversions: ¥159.4 and S$1.289 per US$1 ( open exchange-rate data , 19 Nov 2026). One more point. Nittobo's own filing notes that it has a medium-term plan of ¥80 billion of capital spending over four years. A company that dominates one grade has every reason to build carefully. Secondary write-ups say it is also scaling back low-margin E-glass to focus on T-glass, which, if true, shifts the tightness onto the cheaper cloth too."},{"id":"s7","h":"How we got here","t":"Apr 1918 A silk spinner is founded in Fukushima city. It later becomes Nitto Boseki. 1938 Nittobo is reported as the first in Japan to industrialise glass fibre. Summer 2023 Nittobo's low-dielectric cloth starts going into AI-server switch boards, per its filing. 2024 Advanced packaging goes into full-scale adoption, needing larger substrates. 29 Aug 2025 Board approves the ¥15 billion Fukushima building. ¥15B 28 Nov 2025 Nan Ya says it will weave about 20% of Nittobo's cloth by 2027. ~20% Mar 2026 Tom's Hardware reports lead times beyond 20 weeks, with new supply not due before mid-2027. 20+ wks Apr 2026 TrendForce: Nittobo about 90% of T-glass; no new capacity before mid-2027 at the earliest. ~90% Nov 2026 You are here. Dec 2026 Construction of the new building is scheduled to finish. Jan–Mar 2027 Production due to start. Relief expected later. So the bottleneck isn't a chip or a fab. It's a mill, a glass recipe and a yield rate. Next time a shortage hits something that looks simple, ask two things. QUESTION 1 Who else can make the top grade at a usable yield? QUESTION 2 And how many months until their output is qualified by the customer?"},{"id":"sources","h":"Sources","t":"Each number and where it comes from. ~90% T-glass and 60–70% NER share; no new capacity before mid-2027 at the earliest; Rubin demand: TrendForce, 30 Apr 2026 . Interposer 814 → 1,700 mm²; furnace 1,600–1,700°C; prices +20–30%; lead times 8–10 → 20+ weeks; BofA ¥40.9B → ¥87.7B and ~48% margin; Nan Ya ~20%; Taiwan yarn doubling; competitors and yields: Tom's Hardware, 9 Mar 2026 . ¥15.0B Fukushima building, ¥2.4B subsidy, Oct 2025–Dec 2026 construction, ~3× capacity, switch-board use since summer 2023, ¥80B capex plan: Nittobo notice via MarketScreener, 29 Aug 2025 . Nan Ya partnership, 28 Nov 2025: TrendForce News . Customer bookings (via Nikkei), thin-style tightness, 1080 cloth ~+300%, ~19% of laminate cost, loom counts, 2028 easing views, ~3× by year to March 2029: MPBxChange roundup . This is a secondary compilation; I treat its numbers as reported, not verified. Scaling back E-glass, Nvidia and AMD as customers: The Buildout, 5 Aug 2026 . Secondary and unverified. Founded April 1918: Wikipedia: Nitto Boseki . Silk-spinner origin and 1938 glass-fibre industrialisation: company-history summary . Dates differ slightly across sources. Nvidia FY2026 revenue US$215.9B: my GPU explainer . Currency: about ¥159.4 and S$1.289 per US$1, open exchange-rate data , 19 Nov 2026. Figures are rounded. Educational only. Not investment advice. This is a living document and will be updated as new capacity comes online and figures are checked."}]},{"id":"implants","href":"explainers/implants.html","title":"Chipped at Work: How Close Are Bosses to Putting Tech Inside You?","short":"Chipped at work","topic":"Neurotech and work","category":"Neurotech","date":"2026-11-19","dek":"Hand chips, brain implants, EEG caps, and the laws that got there first","sec":[{"id":"","h":"","t":"Explainer · Living document · Neurotech and work Chipped at Work A Wisconsin company once threw a party to put microchips in its staff's hands. Today, brain implants let paralysed people move cursors with their thoughts. So how close are we to a boss asking you to get one? Closer for some kinds of tech than you'd think, and much further for others. How deep tech goes, and how many people have it. Sources: Wenco/SmartCap, MIT Technology Review, European Parliament study, Neuralink via Wikipedia (see Sources). Implant counts are estimates. Graphic: Jun Wei Heung. By Jun Wei Heung · Words and graphics November 19, 2026 · 12 min read Copy link Sources \"Cyborg implant\" here covers two very different things: small RFID chips under the skin, and brain-computer interfaces (BCIs) placed in or on the brain. I also cover EEG wearables, which read brain signals without surgery, because that's where workplaces actually are today. Implant counts are estimates: nobody keeps an official register. Company figures come from company statements reported in the press. This is an explainer, not medical, legal or investment advice. Key findings Workplace chipping has already happened, voluntarily. In 2017 about 50 staff at Three Square Market in Wisconsin had a rice-sized chip put in their hands; by 2018 it was about 80 of 250 . Brain implants are still medical experiments. Neuralink reported 21 trial participants in January 2026, and no brain-computer interface has full FDA approval for permanent use, let alone for healthy workers. The brain data is arriving without surgery: miners and truck drivers already wear EEG caps that score their fatigue , for about US$1–2 per worker per day. The law moved first. At least a dozen US states ban employers from requiring implants, Washington joined in 2026, and Colorado, California and Montana now treat neural data as sensitive."},{"id":"s1","h":"The chip party","t":"On 1 August 2017, Three Square Market, a company in River Falls, Wisconsin that makes self-service kiosks for office break rooms, held what it called a \"chip party\". About 50 employees volunteered to have a chip the size of a grain of rice injected between their thumb and forefinger. Each one cost about US$300 , and the company paid. 1 August 2017, River Falls, Wisconsin The chip party: queue, injector, door. Illustration by Jun Wei Heung of Three Square Market's 2017 chip party, based on press reports. Generic figures, not real people. Chips cost about US$300 each (SiliconANGLE). A year later, MIT Technology Review found about 80 of its 250 staff had one. The chips came from BioHax, a Swedish company whose implants were already in use at Epicenter, a Stockholm office hub where members used them to open doors. What a hand chip actually does A passive RFID/NFC tag, the same kind of technology as a contactless office badge. Tap the buttons to see it work. Open a door Pay at a kiosk Track my location? The reader on the door sends out a weak radio field. It powers the chip for an instant, reads its ID number, and unlocks the door. Simplified. Passive chips have no battery or GPS; they only work a few centimetres from a reader. Some newer payment implants, such as Walletmor's, work with contactless card terminals. Sources: NPR, MIT Technology Review, Finextra. What a hand chip really looks like: an implant next to a reader (left), and an X-ray of one sitting between thumb and forefinger (right). These are hobbyist implants, not Three Square Market's. Photo: Amal Graafstra , CC BY-SA 2.0 , via Wikimedia Commons. Photo: WCusr2019 , CC0 , via Wikimedia Commons. That's the first thing to understand about hand chips: they are far less sinister than they sound. A passive chip can't follow you home. It's a door badge you can't lose. The bigger worry is what it signals: if a company can ask you to put its badge inside your body, what else might it ask? The idea never really spread. The Polish company Walletmor, which sells payment implants, announced its 1,000th sale in 2022. A European Parliament study in 2018 put the number of people with chip implants worldwide at somewhere between 2,000 and 10,000, and later estimates run into the tens of thousands. Nobody really knows."},{"id":"s2","h":"The brain race","t":"Hand chips are the toy version. The serious money is going into brain-computer interfaces: implants that read signals from neurons and turn them into actions, like moving a cursor or typing. Right now, every one of them is a medical device being tested on people with paralysis or other severe conditions. Scroll to see how far each one has got. Neuralink's surgical robot, built to insert the implant's hair-thin threads into the brain. Photo: Leijurv , CC BY-SA 4.0 , via Wikimedia Commons. The road A brain implant has to pass through every stage on the right before an employer could even think about asking for one. Most of the industry is still in the first half. Neuralink Elon Musk's company implanted its first patient in January 2024. Weeks later it said some of the device's hair-thin threads had pulled out of the brain . By January 2026 it reported 21 participants in its trials. 21 people, Jan 2026 Synchron Synchron's device goes in through a blood vessel, without opening the skull. It has been tested in trials in Australia and the US, and has FDA Breakthrough Device designation, but no full approval yet . Precision Neuroscience Precision has the most concrete green light: FDA clearance for a thin electrode sheet that can sit on the brain for up to 30 days , for diagnosis. That's temporary, not a permanent implant. 30 days, max Paradromics Paradromics got FDA permission to start a feasibility study in November 2025, and surgeons completed its first long-term implant in 2026, according to company and press reports . The gap Not one of these devices is approved for permanent use in patients, let alone for healthy people. And getting there means brain surgery. The last two stages on the right are empty, and they may stay that way for a long time. 0 approved for healthy people From lab to workplace Where each brain-implant company stands, November 2026 Positions are simplified from public trial and FDA status. Sources in the step cards. The brain implant you've read about is a medical trial. The one at work is still science fiction."},{"id":"s3","h":"No surgery required","t":"Here's the twist: you don't need an implant to have your brain read at work. EEG (electroencephalography) sensors pick up faint electrical activity through the scalp, and they already fit inside caps, helmets and headphones. A lab EEG cap. Workplace versions like SmartCap hide fewer sensors inside an ordinary cap or hard hat. Photo: Chris Hope , CC BY 2.0 , via Wikimedia Commons. In Australia, mining operators including Rio Tinto have used SmartCap , a cap that turns brain signals into a fatigue score so tired truck drivers can be stood down before they crash. In 2018 the South China Morning Post reported that Chinese factories and state firms had put EEG sensors in workers' hats to watch for fatigue and emotional changes, with managers sending workers home or moving them when the system raised an alarm. The price of reading a brain at work Rough cost per worker, US$ EEG fatigue cap, per worker per day (trucking) ~US$1 EEG fatigue cap, per worker per day (mining) ~US$2 Hand chip, one-off (2017) ~US$300 Brain surgery No price exists for a brain implant in a healthy person, because no one is allowed to sell one. Every implant so far has been part of a medical trial. SmartCap prices as reported by its maker to Equipment World ; chip price from SiliconANGLE. Bars to scale. An analogy: the breathalyser in the truck Some trucks won't start until the driver blows into a breathalyser. Few people object, because the test checks one thing, for one clear safety reason, and nothing else. A fatigue cap is the same deal, as long as it only measures tiredness. The fight starts when the same signal is used to rate how focused, happy or loyal you are. The scientific evidence for those bigger claims is thin. MIT Technology Review pointed out that scalp EEG is very limited in what it can detect, and that the link between its signals and emotions isn't well understood. But a measurement doesn't have to be accurate to be used against you."},{"id":"s4","h":"Would you say yes?","t":"Most people wouldn't. In a Pew Research Center survey of more than 10,000 American adults in late 2021, a majority said widespread use of brain chips to boost thinking would be a bad idea for society, and most said they wouldn't want one themselves. Brain chips to boost thinking: good idea? US adults, November 2021 56% Bad idea 31% Not sure 13% Good Would not want one for themselves 78% Would want one 20% Survey of 10,260 US adults, 1–7 November 2021: Pew Research Center . The question asked about chips that let people process information faster and more accurately. But \"would you?\" is a different question at work. When the person asking also decides your pay and your promotion, saying no isn't free, even if the offer is called voluntary. Nevada took that argument to its logical end and banned even voluntary workplace chip programmes."},{"id":"s5","h":"The law got there first","t":"Unusually for new technology, lawmakers moved before the problem was widespread. A growing list of US states bans employers from requiring an implant as a condition of a job. States that ban employers from requiring implants As listed by SHRM and other legal trackers. Tap a highlighted state for the detail. Tap Nevada or Washington. Sources: SHRM , LexisNexis State Net , Carnegie Council . Trackers count differently; check each state's statute before relying on it. The next fight is over the data. Even without an implant, headphones and caps can collect brain signals, and a Neurorights Foundation review found 29 of 30 consumer neurotech companies it studied had access to users' brain data. Colorado (2024) and California (2024) now count \"neural data\" as sensitive personal information, and Montana followed in 2025. Abroad, Chile wrote mental privacy into its constitution in 2021, and in November 2025 UNESCO adopted the first global recommendation on the ethics of neurotechnology ."},{"id":"s6","h":"So how realistic is it?","t":"It depends on which version you mean. Pick a scenario to see where it stands today. The realism meter My judgement, based on the evidence in this explainer Wear an EEG cap Offered a hand chip Required hand chip Required brain implant An editorial judgement, not a forecast. Each verdict is explained under the gauge. The pattern is clear. The tech that's easiest to adopt is the tech that doesn't go inside you: caps, headbands and earbuds. They're cheap, they come off at the end of a shift, and they're sold as safety gear. That's where the real questions about brain data at work will be fought over the next few years. Implants are a different matter. A hand chip is a solution looking for a problem: a phone or a badge does the same job. A brain implant means surgery, a device no regulator has approved for healthy people, and a growing pile of laws in the way. If a boss ever asks for one, the realistic answer for now is that they can't. The boss who reads your brain won't use a scalpel. They'll use a hat."},{"id":"s7","h":"How we got here","t":"2015 Epicenter, a Stockholm office hub, offers members chip implants to open doors. 1 Aug 2017 Three Square Market's \"chip party\": about 50 staff get hand chips. ~50 2018 Reports of EEG caps on workers in China; about 80 of Three Square Market's 250 staff now chipped. 80 / 250 2019 Nevada bans even voluntary workplace chip programmes. 2021 Chile protects mental privacy in its constitution. Pew finds 56% of Americans think brain chips for thinking would be bad for society. 56% Jan 2024 Neuralink implants its first patient. Some threads later pull loose. 2024–25 Colorado, California and Montana start treating neural data as sensitive. UNESCO adopts neurotech ethics rules. Jan 2026 Neuralink reports 21 trial participants. 21 2026 Washington becomes the latest state to ban employer-required implants. Nov 2026 You are here. No brain implant is approved for healthy people. If your company ever pitches brain tech at work, ask two things. QUESTION 1 Could I say no without it costing me? QUESTION 2 Who owns the data, and what else will it be used for?"},{"id":"sources","h":"Sources","t":"Each number and where it comes from. Three Square Market chip party, about 50 volunteers, 1 August 2017, BioHax partnership: NPR , MPR News ; US$300 per chip: SiliconANGLE ; about 80 of 250 staff by 2018: MIT Technology Review . Epicenter's door-opening chip implants (2015): BBC News . Walletmor's 1,000th payment implant (2022): Finextra . Worldwide implant estimate of 2,000–10,000 (2018): European Parliament study via Statewatch ; higher later estimates: CPA Canada . Neuralink's first implant and thread retraction: Quartz ; 21 participants as of January 2026 and Blindsight status: Wikipedia, Neuralink (citing company updates). Third-party trackers report higher later counts that Neuralink hasn't confirmed. Synchron, Precision Neuroscience and Paradromics status: BCI FDA approval tracker , Wikipedia, Precision Neuroscience , Wikipedia, Paradromics . Check company and FDA announcements for exact dates. SmartCap at Rio Tinto: Best Practice AI ; pricing of about US$1–2 per worker per day: Equipment World ; EU-OSHA case study: Wenco . EEG caps on workers in China (2018): South China Morning Post ; limits of scalp EEG: MIT Technology Review . Public opinion (56% bad idea, 13% good, 31% unsure; 78% wouldn't want one): Pew Research Center, March 2022 . State bans on employer-required implants, Nevada's 2019 ban on voluntary programmes, Washington HB 2303 (2026): SHRM , LexisNexis State Net , Davis Wright Tremaine , Carnegie Council . Neural data laws (Colorado HB 24-1058, California SB 1223, Montana SB 163), Chile, UNESCO and the Neurorights Foundation review: Arnold & Porter , CBS News , TrustArc . Educational only. Not medical, legal or investment advice. This is a living document and will be updated as trials report and laws change."}]},{"id":"anthropic-ipo","href":"explainers/anthropic-ipo.html","title":"Who Owns Anthropic? Inside the $1.5 Trillion IPO","short":"Who owns Anthropic?","topic":"AI and capital markets","category":"AI & capital","date":"2026-11-19","dek":"The cap table, the voting structure, the price, and the lock-up calendar","sec":[{"id":"","h":"","t":"Explainer · Living document · AI and capital markets Who Owns Anthropic? The maker of Claude pulled off the biggest IPO on record: 980 million shares at $83 each, raising $81.3 billion, and the stock is now trading on Nasdaq as ANT. Amazon and Google own more than a quarter of it. Seven co-founders own about an eighth. But only one of those groups gets to vote, and it isn't the one with the most shares. Owning versus controlling Anthropic after the IPO. My calculation from Anthropic's amended S-1 (10 November 2026) and its pricing release (18 November 2026), at $83 a share, before the underwriters' extra-share option. Graphic: Jun Wei Heung. By Jun Wei Heung · Words and graphics November 19, 2026 · Updated November 20, 2026 · 13 min read Copy link Sources Ownership figures are my own calculations. I started from the principal-stockholder table in Anthropic's amended S-1 (filed 10 November 2026, holdings as of 30 September 2026) and updated it for the final deal in Anthropic's 18 November pricing release: 956.4 million new shares, $83 a share, and a $26 billion private placement at the same price. They assume the underwriters don't buy their 147 million optional extra shares. The final prospectus may differ slightly. \"Fully diluted\" counts options, unvested stock awards and Amazon's unconverted notes. ANT began trading on Nasdaq on 19 November 2026. The day-one and later prices mentioned below will keep changing; this is a snapshot, not a live feed, and not investment advice. Key findings Anthropic sold 980 million shares at $83 , above its $70–78 range, raising $81.3 billion , the largest IPO on record. That values it at about $1.5 trillion fully diluted. Amazon (about 14.5%) and Google (about 13.4%) are the biggest shareholders, worth roughly $159 billion and $147 billion at $83. Their shares carry no votes . The seven co-founders own about 1.8% each (roughly $20 billion apiece), but a single Class F share gives them 50.1% of every shareholder vote . A separate trust elects a majority of the board. Only about 7% of the company is trading. Some 15.5 billion locked-up shares become free to sell in about 25 waves over the next year."},{"id":"s1","h":"The biggest IPO ever","t":"On the evening of 18 November 2026, Anthropic set the price of its first public shares at $83 each . A week earlier it had told investors to expect $70 to $78 . Demand was strong enough that it raised the price and sold more shares too: 980 million instead of 825 million. Pricing night, New York The order book closes. The price is set. Trading starts the next morning. Illustration by Jun Wei Heung, based on Anthropic's pricing release. Generic figures, not real people. Goldman Sachs and Morgan Stanley led the deal. Do the multiplication and you get $81.3 billion , the biggest initial public offering on record. CNBC reports it tops SpaceX's listing in June. The stock starts trading on Nasdaq under the ticker ANT . Not all of that money goes to the company. Of the 980 million shares, 956.4 million are new shares created by Anthropic, worth about $79.4 billion. The other 23.6 million come from the seven co-founders, who are each selling 3,378,378 shares, or about $280 million each. Their money goes to them, not to Anthropic. The Nasdaq MarketSite studio in New York, where companies traditionally mark their first day of trading. Photo from 2015, not of Anthropic's listing. Photo: Luca Marfè at Italia all'ONU , CC BY 2.0 , via Wikimedia Commons. Alongside the IPO, a group of chip and cloud companies agreed to buy $26 billion of shares directly from Anthropic at the IPO price. Google and Nvidia are putting in $10 billion each. AMD, Broadcom, Micron, Microsoft and TSMC split the rest. At $83, that's about 313 million more shares , and they aren't sold to the public."},{"id":"s2","h":"Who owns what","t":"The filing lists the big holders, and some of the numbers are startling. Here is the whole company split into 100 squares, each worth about 1%. Switch between who owns the shares and who gets to vote . Anthropic after the IPO, in 100 squares About 13.2 billion shares outstanding at $83, before the underwriters' option Who owns it Who votes Rounded to whole squares. \"Everyone else\" includes employees, early staff and venture investors such as Spark, Menlo, ICONIQ, Lightspeed, GIC, Coatue and Fidelity; the filing doesn't list their stakes, because none holds more than 5% of the voting shares. Source: my calculation from Anthropic's amended S-1 and pricing release. Three things stand out. The two cloud giants own more than the founders. Amazon will hold 1.92 billion shares , about 14.5% of the company. Google will hold about 1.77 billion, around 13.4%, including the shares it is buying in the placement. Both bought in early through loans that turned into shares. Amazon's notes, TechCrunch reports, convert at about $2.09 or $5.05 a share . At $83, the cheaper ones are worth roughly 40 times what Amazon paid. Amazon could own even more. It still holds notes that would convert into another 1.56 billion shares , but it can't convert them yet without going over an ownership cap in its agreement. If it converted everything, it would own nearly a quarter of Anthropic. The company gave 5% of itself away. Before the IPO, Anthropic put 846.8 million shares into the Anthropic Public Benefit Fund, a subsidiary that funds its stated mission. At $83, that's about $70 billion. The board can top it up by up to 2% more at a time. The named shareholders Shares after the IPO and placement, and what they're worth at $83 Holder Class Shares % owned Value at $83 % of votes Co-founder holdings include options and stock awards that vest within 60 days of 30 September 2026, as the filing counts them, after the shares each is selling. Google's total adds about 120 million placement shares to the 1.65 billion it held before. Vote shares are my estimate from the 50.1% / 49.9% split. Sources: Anthropic amended S-1, pricing release, TechCrunch."},{"id":"s3","h":"Owning isn't controlling","t":"Here's the twist. Amazon and Google own more than a quarter of Anthropic and get no votes at all . Their shares are a special kind, Class N , that pays out like ordinary shares but can't vote. If they ever sell, the shares turn into normal voting stock in the buyer's hands. Meanwhile, the seven co-founders hold a single share between them, through a company called the Founder LLC. It's a Class F share, and it carries 50.1% of the votes on almost everything shareholders decide. Everyone who owns ordinary Class A shares, including the people buying in the IPO, splits the other 49.9%. Think of it like a members' club Ordinary members each get a small ballot. The founders hold one giant ballot that always outweighs everyone else put together. The two richest backers paid for a big share of the club, but they came without ballots. And a trustee holds a separate key: the right to pick most of the board. That trustee is the Long-Term Benefit Trust , a group of five people with no financial stake in the company. It holds one Class T share, which lets it elect and remove a majority of the board . So the founders control shareholder votes, and the trust controls who sits on most of the board. Anthropic is a public benefit corporation, so its board must weigh its mission alongside profit. The founders' grip is tighter than 50.1%. They also own Class A shares, about 1.67 billion between them, which carry roughly 11% of the votes on top of the founder share. Under Nasdaq's rules, that makes Anthropic a \"controlled company\" , which lets it skip some of the usual requirements for independent directors. Why it was built this way Anthropic says the structure is meant to stop the company being steered by whoever owns the most shares, including its biggest commercial partners. Critics say it leaves IPO buyers with money at stake but little say. Both are true: it's a trade-off, and the S-1 lists it as a risk."},{"id":"s4","h":"Where the $81 billion goes","t":"Anthropic is raising this money because building frontier AI costs a fortune, and its revenue is growing faster than almost any company in history. The S-1 reported $16.3 billion of revenue in the first half of 2026, up from $1.2 billion a year earlier. The second quarter alone brought in $11.6 billion, and it was its first quarter with an operating profit. The numbers behind the price From the S-1, in US$ billions Revenue, Jan–Jun 2025 $1.2B Revenue, Jan–Jun 2026 $16.3B Revenue, Apr–Jun 2026 (one quarter) $11.6B Net loss, Jan–Jun 2026 $80.7B …of which non-cash accounting charges $72.4B $974M Operating profit in April–June 2026, Anthropic's first profitable quarter in the six the filing shows. The giant net loss is mostly the paper cost of its convertible notes and other instruments rising in value as its valuation soared. Source: Anthropic S-1, as reported by The VC Corner (pages 23 and 158). Bars are to scale against the largest figure. The net loss looks catastrophic until you see what makes it up. Anthropic's actual operations ran at a small profit for the half-year; the loss is almost entirely a paper charge. This bridge walks from the one to the other. How a small operating profit became an $81 billion loss First half of 2026, US$ billions The non-cash charge is the rising fair value of Anthropic's convertible notes and similar instruments as its valuation soared; it is an accounting cost, not cash leaving the company. \"Other items\" is the balancing figure between reported operating income and net loss (taxes, interest and remaining fair-value items). Source: my arithmetic from Anthropic's S-1 figures as reported by The VC Corner. The filing is vague about exactly how the money will be spent: \"general corporate purposes\" , including investment in computing power and infrastructure, and hiring. One specific item stands out. When Anthropic's employee stock awards turn into real shares at the IPO, the company has to pay the tax it withholds on them. At the midpoint price that was $3.8 billion . At $83, by the filing's own sensitivity figure, it's about $4.3 billion . Here's the full flow of money. Who gets the money Gross amounts at $83, US$ billions New shares sold to the public → Anthropic $79.4B Private placement (Google, Nvidia, AMD, Broadcom, Micron, Microsoft, TSMC) → Anthropic $26.0B Underwriters' option, if fully used → Anthropic up to $12.2B Co-founders' shares sold → the seven co-founders $2.0B My arithmetic from the pricing release: 956,351,354 new shares, 23,648,646 sold by the co-founders, and an option for 147 million more, all at $83. The placement is a fixed $26 billion. These are gross amounts, before bank fees and expenses. Stacking the same figures into a waterfall shows how the cash into Anthropic builds up to the full deal size. How the deal adds up for Anthropic Gross cash raised by the company at $83, US$ billions The first two steps are what Anthropic receives at pricing; the underwriters' option is a maybe, so it is drawn lighter. The co-founders' $2.0 billion sits outside the running total because that money goes to the seven founders, not to Anthropic. Gross amounts, before bank fees and expenses. Source: my arithmetic from Anthropic's pricing release."},{"id":"s5","h":"What is a share worth?","t":"$83 was only the starting point. Trading opened on 19 November, and from that moment the market, not the bankers, sets the price. To see what different prices would mean for the company and its biggest holders, drag the slider. The markers show prices implied by Anthropic's earlier funding rounds and by the IPO range. Pick a share price What Anthropic and its biggest holders are worth at that price $83 IPO price Fully diluted value Shares outstanding value Times yearly revenue* Amazon's stake Google's stake Each co-founder A calculator, not a forecast. Fully diluted shares are estimated at 18.1 billion from the roughly $1.5 trillion value at $83 reported by CNBC. Shares outstanding are about 13.2 billion. *Price-to-sales uses April–June 2026 revenue times four ($46.2 billion), a rough yearly rate; the S-1 doesn't give an official one. Round markers use today's share count, so they only roughly show what those rounds were worth per share. At $83, investors are paying about 32 times Anthropic's current yearly revenue rate. That's expensive by normal standards, but the revenue is growing more than tenfold a year. If growth slows, that multiple will look very high. If it doesn't, it could look cheap. Look at the markers too. In February, investors in the Series G round paid a price worth about $21 per share in today's terms. In May, at the $965 billion round, it was about $53. Some IPO watchers had talked about a $2 trillion valuation, about $111 a share. The IPO came in between. Amazon and Google own a quarter of the company. Seven people and one trust run it."},{"id":"s6","h":"What happens next","t":"The IPO wasn't one event. It's the start of a year in which most of the company's shares slowly become sellable. Trading has begun; the rest of the sequence, from the filing, is still ahead. Day one, now done. On 19 November, Nasdaq ran an opening auction to find the first trading price, which could land well above or below $83. Up to 20% of the shares had been set aside for retail investors, TechCrunch reported . For 30 days after the debut, the banks can still buy 147 million extra shares, which they use to steady the price if it sags. The lock-up. Almost everyone who owned shares before the IPO agreed not to sell for a while. That covers about 15.5 billion shares , counting Amazon's unconverted notes and unvested staff awards. Most are locked for 180 days; the co-founders and other large holders for up to 365. But instead of one big release date, Anthropic staggered it into small waves. Some are tied to when it reports earnings. The unlock calendar Shares that become free to sell, in millions, versus the 980 million sold in the IPO Source: \"Shares Eligible for Future Sale\", Anthropic amended S-1. Days are counted from 18 November 2026. Waves tied to earnings use my estimated report dates: the full-year 2026 results around day 100, then roughly every quarter after, with the filing's backstops at day 180 and day 365. The filing gives each wave as \"up to\" a number of shares. The biggest single wave, up to 3 billion shares , comes after the first-quarter 2027 results or at day 180, whichever is first. That's three times the entire IPO. Unlock dates often put pressure on a stock's price, because some holders sell. Not all will: Amazon and Google are strategic partners, and the co-founders have pledged 80% of their equity to charity. Other shares that can appear later: Amazon's 1.56 billion note shares, more Class N shares for the Public Benefit Fund (up to 2% at a time), and new stock awards for staff. Each adds shares and dilutes existing holders a little. The first earnings report as a public company, for the 2026 full year, is the next big test."},{"id":"s7","h":"How we got here","t":"2021 Seven former OpenAI staff, led by siblings Dario and Daniela Amodei, found Anthropic as a public benefit corporation. 2023–24 Amazon and Google invest through notes that can turn into shares. Amazon's convert at about $2.09 and $5.05 a share. Mar 2025 Series E round values Anthropic at $61.5 billion. $61.5B Sep 2025 Series F: $13 billion at a $183 billion valuation. $183B Feb 2026 Series G: $30 billion at $380 billion. $380B May 2026 A new round values it at $965 billion. $965B 1 Jun 2026 Anthropic files for an IPO confidentially. 26 Oct 2026 The S-1 goes public: ticker ANT, Nasdaq. 10 Nov 2026 Roadshow starts: 825 million shares at $70–78. 18 Nov 2026 Priced at $83, upsized to 980 million shares. $81.3 billion raised. ~$1.5T 19 Nov 2026 ANT starts trading on Nasdaq. The market takes over from the bankers. 20 Nov 2026 You are here. Now the one-year unlock calendar begins to run. If you're thinking about the stock, ask yourself two things first. QUESTION 1 Am I comfortable owning a company where my vote can't change anything? QUESTION 2 What happens to the price when billions of locked shares become sellable?"},{"id":"sources","h":"Sources","t":"Each number and where it comes from. Final price, share count (956,351,354 new; 23,648,646 from selling stockholders), 147 million share option, Nasdaq \"ANT\", first trade 19 November: Anthropic, pricing release (18 Nov 2026) . Original 825 million shares at $70–78: Anthropic, roadshow release (10 Nov 2026) . S-1 filing: Anthropic (26 Oct 2026) . Principal and selling stockholders, Class A/N/F/T rights, Amazon's 1.92 billion shares and 1.56 billion unconverted note shares, Google's 1.65 billion shares, the Public Benefit Fund's 846.8 million shares, lock-up schedule, use of proceeds and the $3.8 billion tax figure: Anthropic, amended S-1 (SEC EDGAR) . $81.3 billion raised, record size, about $1.5 trillion fully diluted, co-founders' share sale: CNBC ; ABC News ; CBS News . $26 billion placement and its buyers, up to 20% for retail, $965 billion May round, $1.404 trillion at the top of the range: TechCrunch (10 Nov) . Amazon 14.7%, Google 13.7%, Amazon's note conversion prices and 23.8% fully converted: TechCrunch (17 Nov) . Its percentages use the $74 midpoint, so mine at $83 differ slightly. Founder LLC, Long-Term Benefit Trust, controlled-company status, co-founders' 80% pledge: Commons Capital . Revenue, operating income and net loss figures from the S-1: The VC Corner . Earlier rounds: Series E and Series F (Anthropic); Series G (Pulse 2.0) . Educational only. Not investment, legal or tax advice, and not a recommendation to buy or sell any security. Trading is now live and prices move constantly; the figures here are a snapshot from the IPO and will be revised as the final prospectus and later filings come in."}]},{"id":"amd","href":"explainers/amd.html","title":"From a $2 Stock to the Trillion-Dollar Club: How AMD Came Back","short":"How AMD came back","topic":"Semiconductors","category":"Chips & hardware","date":"2026-11-19","dek":"A $1.62 stock, the Zen bet, a $49B share swap, and the AI race","sec":[{"id":"","h":"","t":"Explainer · Living document · Semiconductors From a $2 Stock to the Trillion-Dollar Club: How AMD Came Back In July 2015 AMD's shares closed at $1.62 and analysts wondered if it would survive. Eleven years later it briefly topped $1 trillion, sells AI chips to OpenAI, Meta and Microsoft, and has just bought a company that builds AI models into chips. This is the story of the comeback, told against Intel’s, and of the engineer who ran it, Lisa Su. By Jun Wei Heung · Words and graphics November 19, 2026 · Updated November 23, 2026 · 20 min read Copy link Sources Share prices are closing prices from StatMuse and StockAnalysis, not adjusted for anything other than splits. Market-share figures come from Mercury Research, as reported by the trade press; Mercury measures \"unit share\" (chips shipped) and \"revenue share\" (money earned) separately, and I label which is which. Some of the history and anecdotes draw on three video profiles of AMD and Lisa Su, listed in the Sources. Figures change every earnings season. Educational only, not investment advice. Key findings AMD's lowest close was $1.62 in July 2015 , nine months into Lisa Su's time as CEO. Its whole market value was under $2 billion. Its near-death came from two directions: the $5.4 billion ATI purchase in 2006, much of which it later wrote off, and a collapse in server chips, where its share fell from about 26% in 2006 to 1–2% by 2018 . The comeback ran on the Zen chiplet design (2017) and EPYC server chips. By early 2026 AMD earned a record 46.2% of all x86 server-chip revenue . The Xilinx deal was paid in shares, so AMD's rising stock lifted its value from about $35 billion when agreed to about $49 billion when it closed in 2022. AI is now the main event. Data-centre sales hit $8.5 billion in July–September 2026, nearly two-thirds of revenue, and AMD's value passed $1 trillion on 21 September 2026. Its shares peaked at $649.42 on 6 October, then fell about 35% in a November chip selloff. Its newest bet is on inference , running AI models rather than training them. In November 2026 AMD completed its purchase of Taalas , a Toronto start-up that etches a whole model into a chip, for about $2.9 billion . Taalas's first chip runs one small model at roughly 17,000 tokens a second for a single user, about 70 times what a top GPU manages."},{"id":"s1","h":"Two cousins, two chip giants","t":"The two chip designers riding the AI wave hardest are Nvidia and AMD, and the people running them, Jensen Huang and Lisa Su, are distant relatives. Both were born in Tainan, Taiwan. Su has confirmed the family link in interviews, and said they didn't grow up together or meet at family dinners. Nvidia turns up all over this series; this page is about the other one. Most people know AMD for gaming graphics cards. That undersells it. AMD is one of only two companies that matter in PC and server processors (CPUs), and one of two that matter in standalone graphics chips (GPUs). For most of its half-century, the rival it could never shake wasn't Nvidia. It was Intel."},{"id":"s2","h":"The timeline","t":"Scroll through fifty-seven years of AMD. On the left, AMD’s and Intel’s share prices from 1980, drawn up to the moment you’re reading about; on the right, what happened. 1969 AMD Intel Share price, US$, log scale. Not company size: the two have different numbers of shares. 1968–1971 Born in Intel’s shadow Both companies trace back to Fairchild Semiconductor, often called the cradle of Silicon Valley. Its best-known founders, Robert Noyce and Gordon Moore, left to start Intel in 1968. A year later Fairchild’s sales chief, Jerry Sanders, started AMD. The two were uneven from the start: Intel had the famous scientists and easy money, while AMD was one of dozens of small chip start-ups scrapping for funding. One of Sanders’s early backers, according to the video profiles, was Noyce himself. AMD’s first products were logic chips, many of them second-source versions of other companies’ designs, sold on a promise of stricter testing than the originals. Its first chip of its own design, the Am2501 counter, followed in 1970. 1972–1980 Going public by copying Intel AMD went public in 1972. Its early business was making cheaper, compatible versions of other companies’ chips. In 1975 it released the Am9080, a copy of Intel’s 8080 processor that AMD’s engineers worked out by studying the chip itself. Rather than fight, Intel signed a patent cross-licence with AMD in 1976, giving it rights to make Intel-compatible processors legally. That agreement is the root of AMD’s place in the x86 business today. By 1980 AMD was a well-established chipmaker, still far smaller than Intel. 1981–1983 Riding in the slipstream When IBM built its PC it didn’t want to depend on a single chip supplier, so Intel agreed to license its x86 designs to a “second source.” IBM launched its PC in August 1981 with an Intel processor; AMD signed the second-source deal in February 1982 and built Intel-compatible chips alongside Intel as PC sales took off. An analogy: riding in the slipstream A cyclist riding close behind another uses far less energy, because the leader takes the wind. For years AMD rode behind Intel, making Intel's own designs as a licensed second supplier. The catch: you can't win a race from the slipstream. Sooner or later you have to pull out and pedal into the wind yourself. 1984–1995 The licence war The second-source deal soured when Intel decided to keep its next chip, the 386, to itself. In 1987 the two companies went to arbitration over whether AMD’s licence covered it, a fight that lasted about seven years. A retired judge ruled largely in AMD’s favour in 1992, granting it a licence to the 386 design but not to later chips. AMD launched its own Am386 in 1991. In January 1995 the companies settled everything: AMD got a royalty-free right to make 386 and 486 chips, provided it didn’t copy Intel’s internal microcode, and agreed not to copy the microcode of the Pentium. From then on AMD would have to design its own chips, which led straight to NexGen and the Athlon. 1996–2006 Pulling out ahead AMD eventually pulled out. It bought the chip-design firm NexGen in 1996 and in 1999 launched the Athlon, faster than Intel’s equivalent and cheaper. In 2000 it made close to $1 billion in profit. In 2003 it launched Opteron for servers, and by mid-2006 Opteron held about 26% of the x86 server market . By the end of 2006 AMD had a quarter of all x86 chips. 2006 The bet that nearly killed it A computer needs a CPU, the general-purpose brain, and a GPU, the graphics processor. AMD saw a prize in owning both, and with Nvidia too big and too expensive it bought the other serious GPU maker, ATI, for about $5.4 billion : roughly $4.3 billion in cash, much of it borrowed through a $2.5 billion loan, plus 58 million AMD shares. The strategy was sound, and it’s why AMD can make graphics chips today. The price wasn’t: within about a year AMD began writing ATI down, by billions of dollars over 2007 and 2008. Days after the deal was announced, Intel launched its fast, power-efficient Core 2 chips and pulled away again. 2007–2009 Selling the factories Now AMD was designing and manufacturing at the same time, with debt approaching $5 billion. In 2009 it spun off its factories into a new company, GlobalFoundries, and kept only chip design. That looked like retreat and turned out to be wise, because it later let AMD use whichever factory was best. Its shares ended 2008 at $2.16. November 2009 Intel pays up In May 2009 the European Commission fined Intel €1.06 billion for using rebates and payments to keep PC makers from using AMD chips. In November, Intel settled all of AMD’s antitrust and patent claims by paying AMD $1.25 billion in cash and agreeing to a set of rules on rebates that would last ten years. The money arrived when AMD needed it most. Neither side admitted wrongdoing, but the deal put a price on years in which AMD said its chips had been locked out of the market. 2010–2012 A flop and a slide In January 2011 AMD’s board pushed out its chief executive, Dirk Meyer; Rory Read took over in August. The year brought one success and one failure. The “APU” chips put a CPU and a GPU on one die, the original logic of buying ATI, and sold reasonably well. The Bulldozer desktop chips, launched in October, were slower than Intel’s and often slower than AMD’s own older chips. In 2012 AMD paid about $334 million for SeaMicro, a maker of dense servers, a bet it later shut down. Layoffs followed, and the shares fell from about $8 at the end of 2010 to $2.40 at the end of 2012. 2013 Consoles, and selling the headquarters The bleeding continued and server share fell to almost nothing. In 2013 AMD sold its Austin campus and rented it back to raise about $164 million in cash. The same year brought the lifeline: AMD’s custom chips went inside both new consoles, Sony’s PlayStation 4 and Microsoft’s Xbox One. Only AMD could offer a CPU and a capable GPU on a single chip, thanks to ATI. Console sales gave it steady income through the lean years ahead. October 2014 Lisa Su takes over Lisa Su became CEO in October 2014. She is an MIT-trained electrical engineer with a doctorate, who worked on chips at Texas Instruments, IBM and Freescale before joining AMD in 2012. She told Bloomberg the job was a dream come true, even though she inherited a company trading around $3. Su and Jensen Huang, who runs Nvidia, are distant relatives, both born in Tainan, Taiwan; she has said they didn’t grow up together. Lisa Su speaking at Computex in Taipei in June 2015, a month before AMD's shares hit their low. She became CEO in October 2014. Photo: Gene Wang , CC BY 2.0 , via Wikimedia Commons. July 2015 The bottom: $1.62 Nine months into Su’s tenure, AMD’s shares closed at $1.62 , its lowest ever. The whole company was worth under $2 billion. After the basics (simplify the company, keep key customers, cut costs) Su made two bets, each hard on its own and far harder together. 2016 Raising cash, waiting for Zen With Zen still a year away, AMD raised money where it could. It sold 85% of its chip assembly and test plants in Penang and Suzhou to Nantong Fujitsu Microelectronics for about $371 million , and licensed server-chip technology to a Chinese joint venture, THATIC, for about $293 million in fees over several years. Its Polaris graphics cards (the Radeon RX 480) went after the mid-range market. Investors began to bet on the turnaround early: AMD’s shares roughly quadrupled during 2016, from $2.87 to $11.34. 2017 Bet one: Zen AMD had drifted into the cheap end of the market. Su aimed it at top-end performance instead. The payoff was Zen : a clean-sheet design later built from small dies called “chiplets,” stitched together into bigger processors. Chiplets are cheaper to make, because a defect ruins one small die instead of one huge one. AMD had aimed for 40% more work per clock cycle; it delivered 52%, and the first Ryzen chips matched Intel’s eight-core Core i7 at around half the price. 2017: the Zen launch A target of 40%, a result of 52%, and a price that undercut Intel. Illustration by Jun Wei Heung, based on AMD's Zen launch claims (Wikipedia, Tom's Hardware). Generic figures, not real people. An AMD EPYC 7702 server chip (2019) with its lid removed: the large central input/output die is ringed by eight small \"chiplets,\" each holding eight processor cores. Photo: Fritzchens Fritz , CC0 , via Wikimedia Commons. 2017–2019 Bet two: consoles and the data centre Su also widened the business. Because the ATI deal gave AMD both CPUs and GPUs, it could build a whole console chip, and it won the PlayStation and Xbox (and later the Steam Deck): steady income through the lean years. The bigger prize was the data centre, which Intel owned almost entirely. AMD’s pitch for its EPYC server chips was total cost of ownership : for a data centre, the chip’s price is the small part, and power, cooling and space over years of nonstop running are the rest. AWS, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud all began using EPYC. 2019 Intel trips Much of AMD’s rise came from Intel’s stumble. Intel’s move to a 10-nanometre manufacturing process ran into deep problems, leaving it on 14 nanometres for years, and in 2019 it wrote to customers to apologise for chip shortages. That’s the risk of making your own chips: when the factory stalls, the whole product line stalls. AMD, without factories, simply used the best one, and from 2019 that was TSMC’s 7-nanometre process. 2018–2026 The server share climbs From about 1–2% of x86 server chips in 2018, AMD climbed past a third of units shipped and nearly half the revenue by 2026. AMD's share of x86 server chips The fall, then the climb after Zen and EPYC. Mercury Research estimates. Unit share (chips shipped) Revenue share (money earned) Only reported data points are plotted; the dashed line from 2006 to 2018 just joins two of them, with no data in between. 2006: Mercury via eWeek. 2018: Mercury via Wccftech (\"1–2%\"). 2022–2026: Mercury via Tom's Hardware, The Register, Fudzilla, Heise, Wccftech and TechPowerUp. Revenue share runs higher than unit share because AMD sells more of the expensive, many-core chips. 2020 Zen 3, and a deal for Xilinx The pandemic set off a boom in PCs and cloud computing, and AMD was ready for it. In November 2020 its Zen 3 chips took the lead over Intel even in single-threaded tasks such as gaming, long Intel’s last stronghold. A month earlier AMD had agreed to buy Xilinx. Its shares doubled during the year, from $45.86 to $91.71. 2021 The shortage boom A global chip shortage meant AMD could sell nearly everything TSMC made for it. Revenue rose 68% to about $16.4 billion, and the shares ended the year at $143.90. The Xilinx deal waited on regulators in the US, Europe and China. Feb 2022 The Xilinx trick, and passing Intel To widen once more AMD bought Xilinx, the leader in FPGAs, chips that can be rewired after they’re made. It paid in its own shares , a fixed 1.7234 AMD shares per Xilinx share. Announced in October 2020 at about $35 billion , by the close on 14 February 2022 AMD’s stock had risen about 40%, so the same ratio was worth about $49 billion . When your stock is soaring, it becomes a currency. With the Xilinx shares added, AMD’s market value passed Intel’s for the first time. On the chart the two lines don’t cross, because they show each company’s own share price, not its size. Paying with stock that keeps rising Same 1.7234 AMD shares per Xilinx share; a higher AMD price, so a bigger deal. Bars to scale. Sources: AMD press release, AnandTech, Reuters. May–Dec 2022 An exascale first, then a PC bust In May 2022 Frontier, a US government supercomputer built with AMD EPYC processors and Instinct accelerators, became the first machine officially measured at over a quintillion calculations a second. Then the pandemic PC boom ended. Sales of PCs and their chips fell sharply, and AMD’s shares lost more than half their value in 2022, ending at $64.77. 2023 Racing into AI ChatGPT, released at the end of 2022, set off a rush for AI chips, and Nvidia took almost all of it. AMD answered in December 2023 with the Instinct MI300X, an accelerator with more memory than Nvidia’s H100, which mattered for running large models. Its shares more than doubled in 2023, to $147.41. 2024 The AI race The fight with Nvidia is a different contest from the one with Intel. In AI accelerators, demand has run far ahead of supply, so buyers want a second supplier. AMD’s MI300 chips passed $5 billion in sales in 2024, mostly to Microsoft and Meta. March–June 2025 Buying the rack builder AI customers increasingly buy whole racks, not single chips. In March 2025 AMD completed its purchase of ZT Systems, a builder of data-centre infrastructure for the biggest cloud companies, agreed the year before at about $4.9 billion; it kept the design teams and set out to sell the factories. In June it launched the MI350 accelerators and previewed Helios, its own full-rack system. October 2025 The OpenAI deal AMD signed its biggest AI deal: OpenAI agreed to deploy 6 gigawatts of AMD’s Instinct chips over several generations, starting with 1 gigawatt of the MI450 series in the second half of 2026. In return AMD gave OpenAI a warrant to buy up to 160 million AMD shares for a cent each, released as deployments happen and as AMD’s share price hits targets reportedly up to $600. It’s the Xilinx trick in reverse: this time AMD used its shares to win a customer. Jan–Jun 2026 Intel’s comeback rally Intel had its own surge. Its 18A chips entered high-volume production in Arizona in January, it gave strong forecasts in April, and in June reports of a preliminary chip-making deal with Apple (not confirmed by either company) sent the shares to a record. Intel closed at about $141 on 22 June , nearly four times its price at the start of the year. The physics The memory wall Training a model happens once; inference , running it to answer a question, happens on every request, so over a model’s life it usually costs more. A single message takes this path: The life of one request From the send button to the words on your screen CPU ordinary server processors, such as AMD’s EPYC Accelerator AI chips, such as AMD’s Instinct or Nvidia’s GPUs Message You press send. The app packages the whole conversation so far, not just your last line. Your device API The provider’s front door checks your account and usage limits and accepts the request. CPU Tokenise The text is cut into tokens, word pieces of a few letters each, and each token becomes a number. CPU Route A load balancer sends the request to a server in a data centre that has the model loaded and room to spare. CPU Inference Schedule The server queues the request and batches it with other people’s, so the chips never sit idle. CPU Prefill The whole prompt is processed at once, and what the model worked out is stored in the KV cache . Limited by compute . Accelerator Decode ↻ One token at a time: the chip rereads the model’s weights and the KV cache for every token, then repeats until the answer is done. Limited by memory bandwidth . Accelerator Detokenise and stream Each new token is turned back into text and sent the moment it is made, which is why answers appear word by word. CPU Response The words appear on your screen while decode is still writing the rest. Your device A simplified picture of how large providers typically serve a model; the details differ between companies, and the plumbing steps take milliseconds while prefill and decode take most of the time. The point for AMD: it sells the chips for both halves, the CPUs that handle the plumbing and the accelerators that do the maths. The two phases at the heart of it work very differently: Two phases of every answer A short burst, then a long relay Prefill Decode What it does Reads your whole prompt at once and stores what it worked out in a KV cache Writes the answer one token at a time, looking back at the KV cache on every step What limits it Compute : raw arithmetic Memory bandwidth : moving data What you notice The wait before the first word (time to first token) How fast the words stream (time between tokens) When it dominates Long prompt, short answer Short prompt, long answer: chat, code, agents Within one request the steps are strictly in order: decode needs the cache, and each token needs the one before it. Across many users, a server interleaves different requests' prefill and decode on the same chip (\"continuous batching\") to keep it busy. That raises the provider's total output but doesn't make your own answer arrive faster. Decode is slow because on a GPU the model’s weights sit in high-bandwidth memory beside the processor, not inside it, so for every token the chip streams the whole model in. For Llama 3.1 8B (about 16 GB at 16 bits) on an H200 (4.8 TB/s), that caps one user at about 300 tokens a second ; Nvidia measures about 230 . The speed limit set by memory Most tokens per second one user can get, if each token needs the whole model read once Model size: 8B weights Precision 16-bit 8-bit 4-bit Chip Nvidia H200 AMD MI355X AMD MI455X A simplified upper bound: real systems also read the KV cache, split very large models across several chips, and lose time to overheads, so real speeds are lower. Memory bandwidth: H200 4.8 TB/s, MI355X 8 TB/s (published specs); MI455X 19.6 TB/s (AMD's announced figure). Sources: Nvidia, AMD. August 2026 Buying Taalas Chipmakers have tried three ways around the memory wall. Toronto start-up Taalas took the most radical: build the model into the chip. Its HC1, announced in February 2026, holds all of Llama 3.1 8B on one TSMC 6nm die with 53 billion transistors, and runs at about 17,000 tokens a second per user by Taalas’s count (independent testers measured 15,000–16,000), about 70 times the H200. Three ways around the memory wall Where the model's weights live, and what that costs Approach Idea Catch GPUs (Nvidia, AMD) Weights in HBM next to the chip; batch many users to share each trip to memory Every token still waits on memory: about 230 tokens a second per user on an H200 for an 8B model Groq Keep the weights in fast on-chip memory (SRAM) About 230 MB of SRAM per chip, so a large model is spread over hundreds of chips working in lockstep; a 70B model needed 576 of them Taalas Build the weights into the chip itself as fixed circuitry next to the compute: \"the model is the chip\" One model only, some quality loss from squeezing the weights, and a new model means a new chip In December 2025 Nvidia struck a non-exclusive deal to license Groq's technology and hire its founder and senior staff, reported at about $20 billion (the companies did not confirm the price). Sources: TechCrunch, TechRepublic, Tom's Hardware; Groq chip figures from Groq. The same answer, two chips Both typed in slow motion, at the same slow-down, so you can see the gap Nvidia H200 ~230 tokens/s Taalas HC1 ~17,000 tokens/s Replay Llama 3.1 8B, one user. At real speed this 90-token answer takes about 0.4 seconds on the H200 and about 5 milliseconds on the HC1, too fast to see, so both are slowed 25 times. H200 figure: Nvidia's baseline as cited by Taalas; HC1: Taalas's claim, with independent tests at 15,000–16,000. The catch: it runs one model only, with some quality loss from squeezing the weights, and a new model means a new chip, though Taalas says that takes about two months. The bet is an old one in chips: once a workload stops changing, specialised silicon eats it, as it did with Bitcoin mining and video decoding. Taalas built the HC1 with 24 people for about $30 million. In August 2026 AMD agreed to buy it; AMD’s filing values the deal at about $2.9 billion in cash and stock, including up to about $1.4 billion that depends on conditions being met. 21 September 2026 A trillion dollars In the third quarter AMD began shipping MI455X chips in volume inside full-rack “Helios” systems, naming Anthropic, Meta, Microsoft, OpenAI and Oracle as early customers. On 21 September the shares closed at $615.52, lifting AMD’s value past $1 trillion , the fourth US chip company to get there after Nvidia, Broadcom and Micron. AMD's data-centre sales, per quarter Server CPUs plus AI chips, US$ billions Jul–Sep 2025 (implied by the reported 97% growth) ~$4.3B Apr–Jun 2026 $6.7B Jul–Sep 2026 $8.5B ~64% Data centre's share of AMD's $13.4 billion revenue in July–September 2026. AMD guided about $17 billion of total revenue for the final quarter of 2026, and said AI chip sales are now overtaking server CPU sales. Sources: AMD Q3 2026 results (8-K filed with the SEC), ServeTheHome, Channel Insider. Bars to scale. October 2026 The peak, and Intel’s fall AMD’s record close came on 6 October , at $649.42 . Intel went the other way: on 30 October it fell 28.6% in a day after forecasting weaker fourth-quarter revenue, citing supply shortages and a prolonged PC slowdown. November 2026 You are here AMD completed the Taalas deal on 17 November , putting its technology into the Instinct roadmap alongside AMD’s GPUs, CPUs, ROCm software and Helios racks. Reported ideas around the deal, not AMD announcements, include GPUs handling prefill and Taalas chips decode, and a second-generation chip of around 20 billion weights. Meanwhile chip stocks sold off on doubts about how long AI spending can last, regulation fears and stretched valuations. By 20 November AMD had closed at $424.39 , about 35% below its October peak, and Intel at $51.28 , nearly two-thirds below its June record."},{"id":"s3","h":"What it adds up to","t":"Nvidia is still far ahead, with CUDA, the software most AI developers build on, making it painful to switch. AMD is a strong, fast-growing number two, not yet a challenger for the top spot, and its November slide shows how much of its value now rides on AI spending. The lesson runs through this whole series: in chips, leadership is rented, not owned. A new architecture or a manufacturing slip can topple the leader within a few years, so whoever is on top has to keep betting heavily on the next five. AMD kept betting when it was nearly broke. Intel slowed down when it was on top. 2026 showed how fast it can swing both ways. A GPU is fast at maths and slow at fetching. Taalas's answer is to stop fetching. If you're trying to judge whether AMD's comeback lasts, ask two things. QUESTION 1 Can AMD's software catch up with Nvidia's CUDA, or only its chips? QUESTION 2 What happens to AMD if its AI customers run short of money, or of power for their data centres?"},{"id":"sources","h":"Sources","t":"Each number and where it comes from. Timeline chart: year-end closing share prices 1980–2001 for AMD and Intel from Yahoo Finance’s daily price history ( AMD , Intel ), and 2002–2025 from StatMuse ( AMD , Intel ), plus AMD’s $1.62 low (27 July 2015); 2026 daily closes (AMD $615.52 on 21 Sep, $649.42 on 6 Oct, $424.39 on 20 Nov; Intel $127.39 on 24 Sep, $51.28 on 20 Nov) from StockAnalysis ( AMD , Intel ); Intel’s record close of $140.94 on 22 June 2026 from CompaniesMarketCap . All prices are adjusted for later stock splits, and Intel’s also for dividends (that is how StatMuse gives them; Yahoo’s figures match StatMuse’s exactly for 2002–2025, so the two series join cleanly). Prices before 1980 are not shown. Lisa Su becoming CEO in October 2014 and her career: IEEE Spectrum , CNBC . $1 trillion on 21 September 2026 at a $615.52 close, fourth US chipmaker: TweakTown , Channel Insider , Motley Fool . Filling the timeline’s middle years. 1970s (IPO in 1972, the Am9080 in 1975, the 1976 cross-licence), Meyer’s exit and Read’s arrival (2011), Bulldozer and the APUs, SeaMicro (2012), the PS4 and Xbox One chips (2013), Zen 3 (2020), Frontier (2022) and MI300X (2023): Wikipedia, “Advanced Micro Devices” and the sources it cites. The 1987–1995 licence arbitration and January 1995 settlement: CPU Shack , Electronic Design . The 2009 settlement ($1.25 billion and conduct rules to 2019): AMD , IEEE Spectrum . The 2016 plant sale (about $371 million) and THATIC licence (about $293 million): AMD , AMD 10-K 2016 . ZT Systems completed 31 March 2025: AMD . Year-end share prices in these cards: StatMuse, as above. Intel’s 2026: 18A in volume production, the April forecast and the reported Apple deal behind the June record: Invezz , Nasdaq/Motley Fool ; the 28.6% fall on 30 October and the November selloff: IBTimes , 24/7 Wall St. , Motley Fool . 2006 market shares (Opteron 25.9% of x86 servers in Q2; AMD 25.5% of all x86 by Q4): eWeek , eWeek . ATI acquisition, about $5.4 billion, $2.5 billion term loan: AMD 8-K press release (SEC) , AMD pro forma financials (SEC) ; write-downs: Ars Technica , Los Angeles Times . Austin campus sale and leaseback, about $164 million (2013): AMD . Zen (2017), its 40% target and 52% result, chiplets, Zen 2 on 7nm: Wikipedia , Tom's Hardware . Server share, Mercury Research: about 1–2% in 2018 ( Wccftech ); 17.6% unit share Q4 2022 and 23.1% Q4 2023 ( Tom's Hardware , The Register ); 25.1% Q4 2024 ( Fudzilla ); 28.8% Q4 2025 and 33.2% Q1 2026 ( Heise ); 34.5% Q2 2026 ( Tom's Hardware ); revenue share 33% Q1 2024, 39.4% Q1 2025 ( Wccftech ), 46.2% Q1 2026 ( TechPowerUp ). Xilinx all-stock deal, 1.7234 ratio, about $35 billion at announcement (Oct 2020) and about $49 billion at close (14 Feb 2022): AMD press release , AnandTech , Reuters via Yahoo . AMD passing Intel's market value in 2022: CNBC . MI300 above $5 billion in 2024: CRN , DataCenterDynamics . OpenAI deal, 6 gigawatts, 1 gigawatt of MI450 from the second half of 2026, warrant for up to 160 million shares: AMD newsroom ; one-cent exercise price and share-price targets up to $600: Tom's Hardware . Q2 2026 data-centre revenue $6.7 billion; Q3 2026 revenue $13.4 billion, data centre $8.5 billion (up 97%), MI455X and Helios customers, about $17 billion Q4 guidance: AMD Q3 2026 results (SEC) , ServeTheHome , Channel Insider . Inference phases (prefill, decode, KV cache, continuous batching) and memory bandwidth: H200 4.8 TB/s from Nvidia ; MI355X 8 TB/s and MI455X 19.6 TB/s from AMD . The memory-limit chart is my calculation: bandwidth divided by model size. Groq: Nvidia's non-exclusive licence and hires in December 2025, reported at about $20 billion: TechCrunch , TechRepublic , Tom's Hardware . Taalas HC1 (Llama 3.1 8B, about 17,000 tokens/s claimed, 15,000–16,000 measured, 53 billion transistors, TSMC 6nm, 815 mm², about 200 W, LoRA, quality loss from quantisation, H200 baseline of about 230 tokens/s): CNX Software , Heise , Simon Willison , The Kaitchup . Team of 24, about $30 million spent of more than $200 million raised, two-month turnaround: The Logic , Silicon Republic . AMD's acquisition of Taalas: agreed August 2026 at about $2.9 billion in cash and stock including up to about $1.4 billion of contingent consideration, per AMD's 10-Q (SEC) ; completed 17 November 2026, with the roadmap and Canada plans, per AMD's announcement as reported by TechPowerUp , Engineering.com and DCD ; analysis: Digitimes . The prefill-on-GPU, decode-on-Taalas split and the second-generation chip plans are reported ideas, not AMD announcements. Video explainers used for the inference and memory-wall material (prefill and decode, why decode is memory-bound, and the Taalas story): What Is LLM Inference? How AI Models Generate Responses (IBM Technology) ; AMD's New Chip Does 17,000 Tokens. Nvidia's H200 Does 230. (Evolving AI) . Figures from them were checked against the written sources above. Video profiles used for the history and anecdotes (Fairchild origins, Noyce as an early backer, the second-source story, the ATI decision, the cost-of-ownership pitch, and Lisa Su's remarks): 一口气了解AMD (小Lin说) ; 看蘇姿丰如何扭轉AMD (名人商業解密) ; AMD's CEO Wants to Chip Away at Nvidia's Lead (Bloomberg Originals) . Educational only. Not investment advice. A living document; figures will be updated as AMD reports."}]},{"id":"pi-clubs","href":"explainers/pi-clubs.html","title":"The Insurer Nobody Sees: Twelve Clubs Behind Every Big Ship","short":"The 12 clubs behind every big ship","topic":"Shipping insurance","category":"Market infrastructure","date":"2026-11-19","dek":"Twelve mutual clubs, one four-layer tower, and the Dali claim that reached the top","sec":[{"id":"","h":"","t":"Explainer · Living document · Shipping insurance The Insurer Nobody Sees Whenever a big ship hits something, a bill lands on a company you have almost certainly never heard of. Twelve mutual \"clubs\" insure about 90% of the world's ocean-going tonnage and share one tower of cover. In 2024 a Singapore-flagged container ship pushed that tower to its very last layer for the first time. Layers are not to scale. Structure is the 2024/25 programme (Gard circular); the 90% share is the clubs' own description (West of England); the Dali reserve is as reported by Howden Re and Insurance Business. Sources below. Graphic: Jun Wei Heung. By Jun Wei Heung · Words and graphics November 19, 2026 · 11 min read Copy link Sources This is about one corner of marine insurance: liability cover from \"P&I clubs\", and how the twelve clubs of the International Group pool big claims. Most figures come from the clubs' own circulars, trade press and insurance-industry write-ups, not audited filings, and several are reserves (estimates) rather than final payments. Where sources disagree (the Group has been described as 12 or 13 clubs; the Dali loss as $2.5bn to $2.8bn; the Costa Concordia loss as $1.4bn to $1.6bn) I say so. A few trade pages blocked automated reading, so for those I relied on search excerpts and say \"reportedly\". US-dollar figures are converted at about S$1.289 per US$1 (19 Nov 2026) and are rounded. This explains an industry; it isn't investment or insurance advice. Key findings A ship's hull is insured by ordinary insurers. What it might do to other people (cargo, crew, oil spills, wrecks, bridges) is mostly insured by mutual \"P&I clubs\" . The International Group's twelve clubs cover about 90% of the world's ocean-going tonnage , by their own description. The clubs share large claims through a four-layer tower : the club pays the first $10m , a pool of clubs the next $90m , jointly bought reinsurance the next $2bn , and a $1bn overspill layer sits on top (2024/25 structure). The Dali hit Baltimore's Key Bridge on 26 March 2024. The claim was first reserved at about $1.5bn , now more than $2.8bn : the largest single marine insurance loss on record, and reportedly the first to reach the overspill layer. The buffer held because the clubs hold a record $6.8bn in free reserves. Whether the next renewal gets dearer is the open question, and ten of the twelve clubs have Singapore offices ."},{"id":"s1","h":"The insurer nobody sees","t":"Start with why you have never heard of it. A container ship can carry thousands of boxes of phones, shoes and spare parts. Its owner insures the steel (the \"hull and machinery\") with ordinary marine insurers. That's the part everyone knows. But there is a second, bigger risk: what the ship might do to everyone else. Damage cargo. Injure a crew member. Spill oil. Sink in a channel and block it. Hit a bridge. Ordinary underwriters have long been reluctant to insure open-ended risks like that. So shipowners did what neighbours do: they pooled their own money. The result is the P&I club , short for \"protection and indemnity\". It is a mutual: the people it insures are also its members, and it reports to them, not to shareholders, per Wikipedia's overview . The first one is usually dated to 1855. The morning after A claims desk at a club, the day a container ship hits a bridge. Illustration by Jun Wei Heung with generic figures, not real people. The ~$1.5bn first reserve is as reported by Howden Re ; the 12-club count is from the International Group . The scene is imagined, not a record of any club's office. Today twelve principal clubs form the International Group of P&I Clubs , from Britannia and Gard to Skuld, the Swedish Club and Japan's own association. They \"collectively insure the liabilities of around 90% of the world's ocean trading tonnage\", according to one member's description (that page still says thirteen clubs; the Group itself now lists twelve). Nobody advertises it. There is no logo on a hull. You meet it only when something goes wrong. An analogy: the street's roof fund Picture a street where every house chips in to one pot. When a storm rips a hole in one roof, the pot pays for the repair. There are no shareholders taking a cut. If the pot runs dry after a bad storm, the neighbours top it up. That top-up is what clubs call a \"call\"."},{"id":"s2","h":"The tower of cover","t":"One club cannot carry a disaster alone. So the twelve share. Think of a tower with four floors. A claim fills it from the bottom, and each floor belongs to someone different. Scroll through it. Layer sizes below are the 2024/25 structure; they are reset every year. Floor 1 · The club The club that insures the ship pays the first slice itself: $10m (about S$13m). In the Dali's case, the entering club, Britannia, was reported as responsible for up to $10m. $10m club retention Floor 2 · The pool Above that, the twelve clubs share a \"pool\": $90m above $10m , split into two layers ($40m above $10m and $50m above $50m). A big claim stops being one club's problem. $90m pooled by the clubs Floor 3 · The reinsurers Above $100m, the clubs jointly buy reinsurance: $2bn above $100m , called the General Excess of Loss (GXL) cover. AXA XL leads it, and roughly 80 reinsurers reportedly share it. The Dali's first reserve of about $1.5bn sat inside this floor. $2bn GXL reinsurance Floor 4 · The overspill On top sits a $1bn collective overspill cover, a last backstop. If even that ran out, member clubs could levy an \"overspill call\" on shipowners, capped at 2.5% of each entered ship's convention limit. $1bn last backstop March 2024 · The Dali The claim was reserved at more than $2.8bn (about S$3.6bn). That filled the first three floors and entered the fourth, described as a layer that had never previously been called on. Roughly $300m of overspill cover remained. $2.8bn+ reserved One tower, four floors A big claim fills it from the bottom Schematic, not to scale. Layers from the Gard circular (2024/25). Dali figures from Insurance Business ; reported programme size for the loss year was about $3.1bn ( The Insurer ). Twelve competitors share one tower, because no single balance sheet can hold the worst day. An analogy: buckets that spill into bigger buckets A tap drips into a small bucket. When it fills, it spills into a bigger one, then a huge tank. Almost every claim ends in the first bucket or two. The last tank is for the rare flood. The Dali was the first time water reached it."},{"id":"s3","h":"Why it's a bottleneck","t":"The tower is clever. It is also a single point. Three things make it hard to route around. Scale. About 90% of the world's trading tonnage sits in one club system. A shipowner who wants cover that charterers, ports and banks accept does not have an abundance of alternatives. Paper. International conventions on bunker oil spills, oil-tanker pollution and wreck removal require ships to hold certificates proving insurance. The International Group's clubs issue \"blue cards\", evidence of that insurance, to the state that signs the certificate. The International Maritime Organization has encouraged states to accept blue cards from the Group's members, per Skuld's circular . (Port-state guidance notes the blue card itself is not the certificate; a state still signs that.) The club is the first link in that chain. Open-ended risk. Ordinary insurers shied away from unlimited liabilities in the nineteenth century, which is why clubs exist. Nothing has fundamentally changed. Someone has to be willing to stand behind a $2.8bn claim, and the market for that is thin. The twelve Principal clubs of the International Group, alphabetical American Club Britannia Gard Japan P&I Club London P&I Club NorthStandard Shipowners' Club Skuld Steamship Mutual Swedish Club UK P&I Club West of England Names shortened from the formal entities listed on the International Group's club page . Some trade write-ups still say 13 clubs; the Group lists 12."},{"id":"s4","h":"The stress test","t":"At about 01:30 local time on 26 March 2024, the container ship Dali lost power outbound from Baltimore and struck a pier of the Francis Scott Key Bridge, per the manager's first update . The ship was Singapore-flagged and managed by Synergy Marine (the page lists a Singapore address); the owner, Grace Ocean, is reported as Singapore-based. It was entered with Britannia, one of the twelve. The Dali under the wreckage of the Key Bridge, photographed in March 2024 (date code in the file name). Most of the loss came from replacing the bridge, not the ship. Photo: David Adams / U.S. Army Corps of Engineers, Baltimore District, public domain , via Wikimedia Commons. The bill unfolded in stages. In April 2024 the owner and manager asked a US court to limit their liability under an 1851 law, reportedly to about $43.7m (about S$56m). That did not hold in practice. In April 2026, Maryland announced a $2.25bn settlement (about S$2.9bn) with the owner and operator, per Fox Baltimore . The state's own estimate for a new bridge is $4.3bn to $5.2bn , opening around late 2030, per Carrier Management . Its claims against the shipbuilder continue. Howden Re puts the total insured loss at more than $2.8bn , up $1.3bn from the first reserve of $1.5bn, with roughly $2.5bn of it the bridge. That tops the roughly $1.6bn loss from the 2012 Costa Concordia grounding, making it the largest single marine insurance loss on record. Other reports put Costa at $1.4bn, so treat the gap as roughly 1.75x to 2x. Biggest losses against the tower Insured loss, US$ billions; the bar's full width is the ~$3.1bn programme Costa Concordia (2012) ~$1.6bn Dali, first reserve (Mar 2024) ~$1.5bn Dali, now reserved $2.8bn+ Howden Re (Costa \"circa $1.6bn\"; Dali \"exceeding $2.8bn\", initial $1.5bn). Some reports give Costa as $1.4bn. The ~$3.1bn programme size is from The Insurer ; Howden rounds it to $3bn. The Dali is a reserve (an estimate), not a final payment. The previous record holder: the Costa Concordia , wrecked off Italy in 2012, being rolled upright in September 2013. Wreck removal was a major part of that loss; one report puts removal costs at $917m ( Insurance Day ). Photo: Rvongher , CC BY-SA 3.0 , via Wikimedia Commons. Then came a wobble. Insurance Business reports that a reinsurer initially declined to pay US$180m of the claim, and the clubs temporarily funded the shortfall before the reinsurer later agreed to pay. The same report says the claim has \"entered the collective overspill layer, a protection mechanism that has never previously been called upon\", with about $300m left. The International Group has also said all clubs approved an interim solution to fund a shortfall so the Dali's club could meet a settlement with Maryland."},{"id":"s5","h":"Who pays next","t":"Mutual means the bill returns to the members. So what happens now? The tower was rebuilt for 2026/27: the overspill cover now sits above $2.35bn instead of $2.1bn, per Reinsurance News , so total programme limits reportedly rose to $3.35bn (about S$4.3bn) at the February renewal. The reinsurance bill, by ship type Change in the allocated reinsurance rate for 2026/27, percent (bar length is the size of the change) Fully cellular container ships +15% Dry cargo ships −5% $6.8bn Combined free reserves of the clubs, an all-time high, which meant no shipowner levy was needed. Reported average combined ratio: 102.5% in 2025/26, from 104.9%. Insurance Business , citing Gallagher. A combined ratio over 100% means claims and costs exceeded premiums; investment returns (about 8% in 2025/26) filled the gap. The same article gives two different totals for the 2025/26 underwriting loss across reports, so I show only the ratio. Container ships, the ship type of the Dali, are being charged more at the reinsurance level. Lockton forecasts average general increases of 5% at the next renewal; Gallagher expects 2.5% to 5%. Howden Re says marine liability rates will \"almost certainly\" rise. These are forecasts, not outcomes. The reserve cushion is why the clubs did not have to ask their shipowners for emergency money."},{"id":"s6","h":"The Singapore angle","t":"Singapore is the Dali's flag, its manager's home and the world's biggest bunkering port. It is also a club town. The Group's chief executive said in April 2024 that ten of the twelve clubs have offices in Singapore , announced with a deal between the Group and the Singapore Maritime Foundation to promote marine-liability training for local talent, per Hellenic Shipping News . The Maritime and Port Authority counts more than 30 marine insurers and about 60 licensed brokers in the city, per MPA . So when a Singapore ship breaks a bridge, the claims desk, the lawyers and often the club itself are a short MRT ride from the harbour. For a business student that is the point: the plumbing of world trade is a service industry, and a lot of it sits here."},{"id":"s7","h":"How we got here","t":"1855 Perhaps the first protection association, the Shipowners' Mutual Protection Society, is formed in Britain. 1874 Liability for cargo carried is added to club cover. 1967 After the Torrey Canyon oil disaster, oil-spill liability becomes a central part of P&I cover. 2012 Costa Concordia runs aground. Its insured loss, about $1.6bn, becomes the record. ~$1.6bn 26 Mar 2024 The Dali strikes the Key Bridge. First reserve: about $1.5bn. $1.5bn 17 Apr 2024 The Group and the Singapore Maritime Foundation sign an agreement on training. Feb 2026 Programme limits reportedly rise to $3.35bn at renewal. Apr 2026 Maryland reaches a $2.25bn settlement. Reserve revised to over $2.8bn, ahead of Costa. $2.8bn+ Sep 2026 Reports say the claim has entered the overspill layer for the first time, with about $300m left. Nov 2026 You are here. So the bottleneck isn't a mine or a fab. It is a promise: that if a ship does its worst, someone good for billions will pay. Next time you hear about a disaster at sea, ask two things. QUESTION 1 Who else could carry a liability that no single insurer will price? QUESTION 2 What does the next renewal charge for a top floor that has now been used?"},{"id":"sources","h":"Sources","t":"Each number and where it comes from. 12 principal clubs: International Group of P&I Clubs, Group Clubs . Autonomous-ship pooling, Baltimore interim funding: igpandi.org . \"Around 90% of the world's ocean trading tonnage\"; the page's \"thirteen clubs\" wording: West of England, About us . 2024/25 structure: club $10m, pool $90m above $10m (lower $40m above $10m, upper $50m above $50m), GXL $2,000m above $100m, overspill $1,000m, call limit 2.5% of convention limit: Gard member circular 15/2023 . 2026/27 changes (overspill above $2.35bn from $2.1bn; AXA XL leading; recent pool-claims trend): Reinsurance News . Programme about $3.1bn, AXA XL leading, ~80 reinsurers, Britannia first $10m (search excerpts of The Insurer and trade coverage in April 2024; the pages themselves blocked automated reading). Treat as reported. Dali loss over $2.8bn (+$1.3bn from $1.5bn), ~$2.5bn bridge, Costa ~$1.6bn, \"almost certainly\" rising rates: Howden Re, 30 Apr 2026 . First use of overspill, ~$300m left, $180m reinsurer dispute, limits to $3.35bn, $6.8bn reserves: Insurance Business, 8 Sep 2026 . Combined ratio 104.9% to 102.5%, +15% container and −5% dry-cargo reinsurance rates, Lockton 5% and Gallagher 2.5–5% forecasts: Insurance Business . Maryland $2.25bn settlement: Fox Baltimore . Bridge replacement $4.3–5.2bn, ACE $350m, shipbuilder claims: Carrier Management, 13 Apr 2026 . Reported ~$43.7m limitation figure and Singapore-based owner: search excerpts of S&P Global, 3 Apr 2024 and Container News (both blocked automated reading). Unverified. Collision time, flag, manager address: Synergy Marine, Dali update . Costa wreck removal $917m: Insurance Day (headline as seen in search). Mutual structure, 1855 and 1874 dates, Torrey Canyon: Wikipedia: Protection and indemnity insurance . Blue cards and the conventions: Skuld circular ; port-state guidance: Paris MoU guidelines (as quoted in search results). Ten of twelve clubs in Singapore, 17 Apr 2024 agreement: Hellenic Shipping News . Marine insurers and brokers: MPA . Currency: about S$1.289 per US$1, open exchange-rate data , 19 Nov 2026. Figures are rounded. Educational only. Not investment advice. This is a living document and will be updated as the Dali claim settles and the next renewal is priced."}]},{"id":"oil-window","href":"explainers/oil-window.html","title":"The Price Behind the Price: Who Sets the Number Oil Is Sold Against","short":"The price behind the price","topic":"Oil price benchmarks","category":"Market infrastructure","date":"2026-11-20","dek":"Dated Brent, Platts Dubai, the 16:30 window, and ADNOC's switch","sec":[{"id":"","h":"","t":"Explainer · Living document · Oil price benchmarks The Price Behind the Price When one country sells oil to another, the contract almost never names a price in dollars. It points to a number that a data company works out once a day from a handful of afternoon trades. Most of the world's traded oil hangs off these numbers. One of them is named after Dubai but barely contains any Dubai oil anymore, and this month Abu Dhabi tied all its oil to it. The 78% share is Energy Intelligence's estimate as published by ICE (other estimates are lower, about two-thirds). The 25,000-barrel lot and the 16:30 timestamps are from S&P Global Platts' methodology pages. Sources below. Graphic: Jun Wei Heung. By Jun Wei Heung · Words and graphics November 20, 2026 · 12 min read Copy link Sources This story is about the companies that publish the oil prices every contract is measured against, mainly S&P Global Platts. For how it all works I rely on Platts' own how-we-do-it pages, regulators' documents, and research from the Oxford Institute for Energy Studies. Some of that research is from 2012–13, so I flag when a number is old. For a few S&P Global pages I could read only the headline and summary, not the full article, and I flag those too. Some companies and people mentioned were investigated or fined, but none of that accused Platts of wrongdoing, and I say what was and wasn't found. Dollar amounts are shown in Singapore dollars at about S$1.30 to the US dollar (the rate in November 2026), rounded. This is an explainer, not investment advice. The short version Somewhere between two-thirds and 78% of the oil the world ships across borders is priced off one family of reference numbers called Brent (the exact share depends on who is counting). The main Brent number, Dated Brent , is set every day at 4:30pm London time . Its Gulf cousin, Dubai , is set at 4:30pm Singapore time . The company that sets both is S&P Global Platts, a data firm. Brent is now a blend, not a single oil . The North Sea oil fields behind it are drying up: the oil feeding the number fell from 850,000 to under 700,000 barrels a day between late 2020 and early 2023. So in 2023 Platts started counting a Texas oil, WTI Midland , as part of \"Brent\" too. The Dubai number is worse off: actual Dubai oil fell from 400,000 barrels a day in the early 1990s to about 60,000 by 2012. It keeps going by counting several other Gulf oils and letting traders deal in small 25,000-barrel lots . One 2012 study found that on half of all days , not a single one of those lots changed hands. Because so much money rides on so few trades, watchdogs have looked in: EU raids in 2013 (the oil part was reportedly dropped in 2015) and a US$95.7 million (about S$124 million) US penalty on the trading firm Vitol in 2020 over two other Platts numbers. Neither case accused Platts itself. On 1 November 2026 , Abu Dhabi's oil company ADNOC switched all its oil to be priced off the Dubai number, pouring even more of the world's oil onto that one afternoon price."},{"id":"s1","h":"The number nobody sees","t":"Nobody just buys \"oil\". They buy a specific type of oil, loading at a specific port, in a specific month. And here is the surprising part: the contract usually doesn't say how much it costs. Instead it says something like \"whatever the Brent price is on the day it loads, plus two dollars a barrel\". So someone still has to work out what \"the Brent price\" actually was that day, in a way both the buyer and seller trust. That someone is a price reporting agency : a data and news company, not an oil company. It doesn't pump oil, ship it, or refine it. Each afternoon it opens a short trading period called a window , watches the offers to buy and sell and the deals that actually happen, decides which of them count, and then publishes one number for the day. The biggest of these firms is S&P Global Platts, which one 2013 news report called \"the world's leading oil pricing agency\" . You've probably never heard of it, because it sells to industry professionals, and because the oil price you see flash across the TV news is a different thing (a futures price, basically a bet on where oil is heading, not the price in a real cargo deal). Singapore, 16:29 One minute before the window closes. Illustration by Jun Wei Heung with generic figures, not real people. The 25,000-barrel lot, the 20-partial cargo of 500,000 barrels and the 16:30 Singapore timestamp are from S&P Global Platts' Dubai methodology pages . The scene is imagined, not a record of any office. Here is the whole point of this story. An enormous amount of money hangs on a number that comes from very few trades. ICE, the company that runs the Brent futures market, says around 78% of the oil shipped between countries is priced off Brent one way or another (it credits a firm called Energy Intelligence for that figure). Other estimates are lower: ICE elsewhere says \"nearly two-thirds\" , and S&P Global says more than two-thirds. They disagree because they count the indirect cases (like Dubai, which is itself loosely tied to Brent) differently. Whichever figure you pick, it's a huge share of the world's oil. How much oil is priced off Brent? Share of globally exported crude, depending on the estimate \"Nearly two-thirds\" of the spot export market (ICE) ≈ 65% \"Around 78%\", direct and indirect (Energy Intelligence, via ICE) 78% \"Nearly two-thirds\" is drawn at about 65% for illustration; the source gives no exact figure. Estimates use different definitions and years. Sources: ICE , ICE (Energy Intelligence) . An analogy: the closing-bell mango stall Imagine a city whose grocers agree that every mango, in every lorry, is priced off whatever one small stall sells a few crates for when the closing bell rings. Most days it works. But one buyer, or one stubborn seller, at the bell can move the price of the whole city's fruit. That is the worry about thin benchmarks."},{"id":"s2","h":"\"Brent\" is a recipe, not a single oil","t":"Start with the word \"Brent\". It began as the name of one real oil field in the North Sea. By the US government's energy statisticians , that field produced the most it ever would in 1982, about 504,000 barrels a day, and it's now being shut down for good. You can't keep a world-famous price running off one field that's dying, so over the years Platts kept folding in oil from other fields. Scroll through how the recipe changed. 1982 · One field The Brent field hits its all-time high of 504,000 barrels a day . Ships load the oil at the Sullom Voe terminal in Shetland, and traders start buying and selling it in advance. That trade is the great-grandparent of today's Brent price. 504k b/d, the peak 2002–2007 · Add more oil Platts adds Forties and Oseberg in 2002, which lifts the benchmark's volume from under 400,000 to around 1.2 million b/d . Ekofisk follows in 2007. By 2006, loadings of the four grades topped 1.4 million b/d . 1.4m b/d in 2006 2012–2016 · The fall North Sea output keeps sliding: under 1 million b/d by 2012, then 1.03 million in 2015 and 948,000 in 2016, as older fields run down. 948k b/d in 2016 2018 · Troll Norway's Troll grade joins from 2018. By the EIA's count it would have added about 29% to 2016's volume, taking it to roughly 1.2 million . +29% from Troll 2020–2023 · Still shrinking The five North Sea oils together loaded about 850,000 b/d in December 2020 and under 700,000 by early 2023 (Refinitiv data, via Reuters). Platts and ICE had warned there might soon be too little oil to fill even one shipload a day. <700k b/d, early 2023 2023 · Texas joins Platts decides to add WTI Midland , a US grade. On 2 May 2023 buyers and sellers could first bid for it for June delivery, making it the sixth stream and the first addition since 2018. US cargoes to Europe had already topped 800,000 b/d after Russia's invasion of Ukraine. 6 streams, one price The oil behind the benchmark Thousand barrels a day. Definitions change between rows. BFOE = Brent, Forties, Oseberg, Ekofisk. Rows are not like-for-like. Sources: EIA , Platts–ICE paper , Reuters , Platts . The label is still \"Brent\". Less and less of the oil is. The Sullom Voe oil terminal in Shetland, seen across the moors. The Brent forward contract that today's benchmark grew from was built around cargoes loaded here, according to the Platts–ICE white paper . This photo shows the terminal in general, not a particular cargo. Photo: Colin Smith , CC BY-SA 2.0 , via Wikimedia Commons. An analogy: the jam jar that keeps its label A jam maker's strawberries run short, so the recipe adds raspberries, then blueberries, then fruit from another country. The label still says \"Strawberry\". Buyers accept it as long as the taste stays the same, and the maker has rules, like quality adjustments, that keep it so. That is how Dated Brent has survived its own oil running out. So how do six different oils produce one price? Reuters explains the rule: Brent takes the price of the cheapest oil in the mix on that day, after a fair adjustment for shipping costs and quality differences. Cheapest oil of the day wins. The point of adding more oils is that the more there is, the harder it is for any single seller to nudge the price their way. Not everyone liked mixing in American oil. Jorge Montepeque, whom Reuters calls the man who originally designed Dated Brent, said it looked like \"an ocean too far\" . Wait, doesn't adding a new oil change the price? This is the thing that trips most people up. Brent is not an average of the oils in it. If it were, dropping in a cheaper oil would drag the whole number down. It works differently: Cheapest-wins, not a blend. Each day the number follows whichever cargo is offered most cheaply. A newly added oil only moves the price if it turns out to be the cheapest that day. If it's dearer, it just sits in the pool unused and changes nothing. Every oil is put on the same yardstick first. Oils differ in weight and sulfur, and a lighter, cleaner oil is worth more. Platts adjusts each one for quality and for the cost of shipping it, so a barrel of Texas WTI Midland is compared to North Sea oil like-for-like. Adding Texas oil doesn't pull the number toward \"the Texas price\"; it competes only after being normalised. More oil means harder to push, not cheaper. The reason to add streams is to widen the pool so no single seller can corner the window and shove the number around. Adding oil makes the price steadier and harder to rig, not automatically higher or lower. In short: a new oil can only pull Brent down to a lower floor if it is genuinely the cheapest; it can never push Brent up just by being added. That is why the label can keep saying \"Brent\" while the ingredients change underneath. Which raises the obvious next question: how much of each oil is in Brent? The honest answer is that there is no fixed percentage. Brent is not a set recipe like \"40% Forties, 20% Oseberg\". The six streams are simply eligible to set the price, and the mix that actually matters changes day to day with whichever cargoes are cheapest and available. What you can measure is how much of each oil is physically loading, and even that shifts constantly as North Sea fields decline and US cargoes come and go. The six oils eligible for \"Brent\" There is no fixed share. These are the pooled loading volumes over time, which is what actually changes. Four North Sea grades (Brent, Forties, Oseberg, Ekofisk), 2006 peak > 1.4m b/d Same four grades, 2016 948,000 With Troll added, 2016 (≈ +29%) ≈ 1.2m Five North Sea grades, December 2020 ≈ 850,000 Five North Sea grades, early 2023 < 700,000 WTI Midland (US) flows to Europe, 2022, added 2023 > 800,000 6 oils eligible to set one price: Brent, Forties, Oseberg, Ekofisk, Troll and WTI Midland. No fixed split; the cheapest on the day sets the number. Bars are rough and not like-for-like: the rows mix different grade sets and years, scaled against the 2006 peak for illustration. These are loading volumes, not a recipe. Sources: US EIA (2006, 2016, Troll ≈ +29%); Reuters/Refinitiv (850,000 b/d Dec 2020, under 700,000 early 2023, US flows over 800,000 b/d)."},{"id":"s3","h":"The Dubai price, with hardly any Dubai in it","t":"If Brent is a recipe missing some of its ingredients, Dubai is a recipe with the main ingredient almost entirely gone. \"Dubai\" is the reference number used for Gulf oil sold to Asia. In a 2012 paper for the Oxford Institute for Energy Studies, the researcher Bassam Fattouh tracked what happened to the actual oil coming out of Dubai. Dubai crude, the oil that names the benchmark Production of Dubai crude, thousand barrels a day, as given in the 2012 paper Peak, 1990–95 400,000 2004 under 120,000 2009 about 90,000 Latest figure in the paper 60,000 < 4 cargoes of Dubai crude a month at that last figure, by the paper's own arithmetic. And the paper found that since late 2008, no Dubai partial was traded in the window on 50% of days . Fattouh, \"Oil Price Benchmarks in International Trade\", Oxford Energy Forum, February 2012. These numbers are 14 years old; I couldn't find current Dubai output in a source I could verify. Bars drawn to scale from the figures given (\"under 120,000\" drawn at 120,000). So how does a price survive without the oil it's named after? Two patches. First, Platts lets other Gulf oils count as \"Dubai\" too. It started allowing Oman oil in the early 2000s, and today the Dubai number can be based on Dubai, Oman, Upper Zakum, Al-Shaheen, and (with an adjustment for quality) Murban. Second, in 2004 Platts let traders deal in small slices . A full shipload is 500,000 barrels (475,000 in the 2012 paper), which is more than most traders want to buy or sell at once. So the window lets them trade it in 25,000-barrel chunks instead. Collect twenty chunks from the same partner and you've bought a full shipload that must actually be delivered; anything less is just settled in cash. This made it far easier to take part in the window. But it also meant a single tiny trade could end up being the last, and therefore the official, price of the day. That was exactly Fattouh's worry: so few firms did the trading that one of them, buying as little as a single 25,000-barrel chunk , could sway the price of millions of barrels elsewhere. He noted the counter-argument too: plenty of others watch the window and would push back fast. A 2013 article from the side of a rival marketplace, the Dubai Mercantile Exchange, put it more harshly, saying the Dubai setup \"can have as few as two or three participants and regularly does not trade at all\" . That's a competitor talking down a rival, so take it with a pinch of salt. And a lot of oil is priced off this one number. Saudi Aramco, for instance, said in July 2025 that it set the August price of its Arab Light oil to Asia at US$2.20 a barrel (about S$2.90) above the average of the Oman and Dubai numbers . The figure it adds its $2.20 to is the one this little afternoon window spits out."},{"id":"s4","h":"How the daily price actually gets made","t":"So what exactly is this \"window\"? Fattouh's description is still the clearest. Think of it as a short, supervised trading session, like a stock market's final half hour, but with two twists. First, everyone can see who is making each offer ; the firms aren't anonymous. Second, a person at Platts, not an automatic rulebook, decides which trades count toward the final number. The Dubai price is built from actual completed deals first; if there were none, from the best offers to buy and sell; and if even those are missing, from related side-bets traded around it. From a bid to a barrel price Simplified. Following Fattouh's 2012 description and Platts' methodology pages. Traders offer to buy and sell In 25,000-barrel chunks for Dubai. Everyone can see who is making each offer. Platts picks what counts Real deals first, then offers, then nearby side-bets if nothing actually trades. One number at 4:30pm Brent at 4:30pm London. Dubai at 4:30pm Singapore. Everyone's contracts follow E.g. Aramco's oil to Asia: the Oman/Dubai average, plus an extra $2.20. Sources: Fattouh (2012) ; Platts Dated Brent and Dubai pages; Arab News on Aramco's August 2025 price. Platts says it has no financial stake in whether oil is high or low; its job is simply to record what the market is willing to do. The key step is that filter: a human editor, following published rules, turns a messy half hour of trading into one clean number. Platts publishes those rules and any changes to them, and its 2026 Dubai notes show it's still tweaking them. There's a Singapore angle here, and it's more than just the clock. The Dubai window runs on Singapore time. A 2013 paper noted that Gulf oil producers had long leaned on prices \"established by predominantly Singapore-based traders and assessed by the price reporting agency Platts\" . Singapore's own fuels, the petrol, diesel, jet fuel and fuel oil traded here, are usually priced off a separate daily average called the Mean of Platts Singapore (MOPS), with each cargo sold a little above or below it."},{"id":"s5","h":"Who checks the referee?","t":"When a small group of traders can set a number that moves billions of dollars, watchdogs pay attention. Three moments show what they looked into, and what they did and didn't conclude. The window under scrutiny What each authority did, and what it did not say When What happened Oct 2012 Prompted by the world's leading economies in 2011, the global club of market regulators (IOSCO) published a set of good-practice rules for firms like Platts that publish oil prices. Advice, not a licence they have to hold. A follow-up review came in 2014. May 2013 EU competition investigators turned up at BP, Shell and Statoil , and Platts said the EU also looked over its London office, chasing a suspicion that companies had fed distorted prices into the daily window. Statoil linked it to Platts' end-of-day pricing and said it might go back to 2002. The EU stressed a visit doesn't mean anyone is guilty. Bloomberg reported in Dec 2015 that the oil part was dropped; a separate ethanol thread carried on. Dec 2020 The US derivatives regulator (the CFTC) ordered the trading firm Vitol to pay US$95.7 million (about S$124 million) over misconduct that included trying to rig two Platts fuel-oil prices in 2014 and 2015. The total mixes fines and returned profits, and part of the case was about bribery abroad. These were US fuel-oil prices, not Brent or Dubai. None of these actions charged Platts itself. They show why the window gets watched: a trader who wants a better price for an existing position has a reason to move a small market at the close. The built-in defence is the one from Part 2: pour more oil into the number so no single trade can shove it around. The Dubai number has always had a thinner, more awkward history on that front, which is why Abu Dhabi's attempt to build an alternative, and then its U-turn, is the next part of the story."},{"id":"s6","h":"A busier window","t":"Back in March 2021, ICE set up a futures market in Abu Dhabi for ADNOC's main oil, Murban, trying to make Murban a big-name price in its own right. This year ADNOC went the opposite way. On 31 July 2026 it announced that from 1 November 2026 it would price all four of its oils (Murban, Das, Umm Lulu and Upper Zakum) off the Dubai number instead of its own Murban market. In plain terms, it gave up on its homegrown price and plugged back into the Dubai window, publishing how much above or below Dubai each oil would sell the month before it ships. ADNOC said the switch matches its prices better to the month the oil actually loads and shows its \"commitment to pricing transparency\". Reuters tied the timing to this year's war involving Iran, which snarled Gulf oil shipments through the Strait of Hormuz and left traders holding Abu Dhabi oil with heavy losses on their bets. One trader called the move sudden. ICE said its Murban futures market would keep running for months that still had active contracts. The Dubai window itself had a frantic spring. An S&P Global headline says trading hit a record high in March during the war, with its summary citing 1,920 small-lot trades and 82 full shiploads (I could read only the headline and summary, not the full article). Set that against Fattouh's 2012 finding of days when nothing traded at all. Either the window is far more active now, or March was just an unusual month. One busy month can't tell you which. Here's my own read, and it's only that. Murban and Upper Zakum are already two of the oils that can count toward the Dubai number. From November, they're also priced off it. So the oil that sets the number and the oil that follows the number are more and more the same oil. That can be healthy (more trading in the window) or risky (a loop that mostly checks itself, with little outside it to keep it honest). When the producer joins the window, the window decides its own price too."},{"id":"s7","h":"How we got here","t":"1982 The Brent field peaks at 504,000 barrels a day. 504k b/d 1987–88 Platts starts publishing the Dated Brent price in 1987. In 1988 major OPEC members stop setting their prices by decree and start pricing Asia-bound oil off Dubai instead. 2002 Forties and Oseberg join Dated Brent. Oman crude is allowed into Dubai around the same time. 2004 Platts brings in 25,000-barrel \"partials\" for Dubai. 2007 Ekofisk joins the Brent basket. Oct 2012 IOSCO publishes principles for oil price reporting agencies. May 2013 EU inspectors visit BP, Shell and Statoil and review Platts' London premises. The oil probe is reportedly dropped in 2015. 2018 Troll joins Dated Brent. Dec 2020 CFTC orders Vitol to pay US$95.7 million over a scheme that included two Platts benchmarks. Mar 2021 ICE launches Murban futures in Abu Dhabi. May 2023 WTI Midland becomes the sixth stream in Dated Brent. 6 streams Mar 2026 S&P Global reports record Platts Dubai trading. 31 Jul 2026 ADNOC announces it will price all its crude off Platts Dubai. 1 Nov 2026 The change takes effect. You are here. Next time an oil price jumps or a contract is \"linked to Brent\", ask two things. QUESTION 1 How many barrels actually changed hands to set that number? QUESTION 2 Who was on the other side of the bid, and who checks the one who decides?"},{"id":"sources","h":"Sources","t":"Each number and where it comes from. Share of exported crude priced off Brent (around 78%, Energy Intelligence): ICE . \"Nearly two-thirds of the spot export market\", 600,000-barrel cargoes: ICE, \"Evolution of Brent\" . \"More than two-thirds\" of traded crude: S&P Global's Dated Brent page (read through a search summary; the page blocks my fetcher). Timestamps (Dated Brent 16:30 London; Platts Dubai 16:30 Singapore), Dubai basket (Dubai, Oman, Upper Zakum, Al-Shaheen, Murban with premium), 25,000-barrel partials, 20 partials = 500,000 barrels, Platts' statement of no financial interest: S&P Global Platts, Dubai explained (via search summary; the page blocks my fetcher). Brent field peak (504,000 b/d, 1982), BFOE loadings (over 1.4m b/d 2006, under 1m 2012, 1.03m 2015, 948,000 2016), Troll (+29%, about 1.2m, from 2018): US EIA, Today in Energy . The 2021 Platts–ICE paper gives a different, rougher picture of the Brent field's 1980s output (around 1 million b/d), so I use the EIA's figure. Forties and Oseberg (2002) lifting volume from under 400,000 to about 1.2m b/d, Ekofisk (2007), Sullom Voe, the 600,000-barrel cargo concern: S&P Global Platts and ICE, \"Brent Benchmark Complex: Evolving necessity\" (July 2021) . WTI Midland: Platts, 2 May 2023 (first bids for June delivery; six streams; first addition since 2018); Platts, 8 June 2022 (decision); Reuters explainer via Kitco, 25 April 2023 (five grades under 700,000 b/d vs 850,000 in Dec 2020, Refinitiv data; flows to Europe over 800,000 b/d; cheapest-grade rule; the Montepeque quote). Dubai crude output, partials, trading concentration, the structure of the window, 50% of days with no partial: Bassam Fattouh, \"Oil Price Benchmarks in International Trade\", Oxford Energy Forum, February 2012 . \"Few as two or three participants\" and \"predominantly Singapore-based traders\": Owain Johnson, \"Towards a Middle East Trading Ecosystem\", Oxford Energy Forum, November 2013 , an article arguing for the rival DME Oman contract. Aramco's August 2025 Arab Light price to Asia (US$2.20 over the Oman/Dubai average): Arab News, 6 July 2025 . ADNOC's move to Platts Dubai (announced 31 July 2026, effective 1 November 2026, grades, reasons, Murban futures at ICE Futures Abu Dhabi): Reuters via Khaleej Times, 3 August 2026 and Zawya . Murban futures start (late March 2021): S&P Global (headline only). Record Platts Dubai trading in March 2026 (1,920 partials, 82 cargoes): S&P Global , as summarised in search results; I could not open the full text, so please treat the two counts as unverified. IOSCO principles: IOSCO final report (Oct 2012) , with FSB notice . EU inspections, May 2013: RTÉ (Reuters) , Al Jazeera . Probe dropped: Bloomberg, 7 Dec 2015 (read through a search summary). Vitol: CFTC release 8326-20, December 2020 . MOPS: S&P Global subscriber note ; Wikipedia (secondary). Currency: about S$1.30 per US dollar, a rounded figure inside the S$1.28–1.30 range shown by mid-market sites in November 2026. Figures are rounded. Educational only. Not investment advice. This is a living document and will be updated as figures are checked and ADNOC's change settles in."}]},{"id":"cls","href":"explainers/cls.html","title":"The Bank Behind the Currency Market: How CLS Keeps FX Trades From Going Wrong","short":"The bank behind the currency market","topic":"FX settlement","category":"Market infrastructure","date":"2026-11-20","dek":"Herstatt risk, payment-versus-payment, and the $1.4tn a day with no safeguard","sec":[{"id":"","h":"","t":"Explainer · Living document · Currency settlement The Bank Behind the Currency Market Swap one currency for another and two payments must cross two countries on two different clocks. If one arrives and the other doesn't, you have lost the full amount. One bank you have almost certainly never heard of, CLS, exists to stop that. It settles 18 currencies, the Singapore dollar among them. Yet only about a third of the market's daily settlement goes through that kind of lock. Shares are from the BIS survey of April 2025 (the intragroup share is reported as \"more than 35%\", drawn here as 36% so the bar sums to 100). Source below. Graphic: Jun Wei Heung. By Jun Wei Heung · Words and graphics November 20, 2026 · 11 min read Copy link Sources This is about one corner of finance: how foreign-exchange (FX) trades are settled, and the single institution, CLS, built to make that safe. Most figures come from the Bank for International Settlements (BIS) and from CLS's own pages, which are the source with an interest in the story. A few historical details and records come from Wikipedia and are marked as such. The BIS figures are for one month (April 2025) and its authors warn the 2025 method is not comparable with the 2019 and 2022 surveys. CLS's operating window is from a 2015 Reserve Bank of Australia paper plus a CLS description, so check it against current CLS rules. Conversions to Singapore dollars use about S$1.29 per US$1 (20 Nov 2026) and are rounded. This explains an industry; it isn't investment or financial advice. Key findings A currency trade has two payments in two countries . If one side pays and the other doesn't, the loser is out the full amount . That is \"settlement risk\", and it has a name: Herstatt risk , after a German bank that closed on 26 June 1974. More than US$14 trillion (about S$18tn) of FX obligations came due on an average day in April 2025. Only 36% (about $5.2tn) settled \"payment versus payment\", the lock that CLS provides. About 10%, or $1.4tn , had no protection at all. CLS , a bank with no household customers, settles 18 currencies using accounts at 18 central banks. It says it settles about US$8tn a day and cuts funding needs by more than 96% . CLS can't cover everything: pairs it doesn't handle are only 12% locked, and the Indonesian rupiah, Malaysian ringgit and Thai baht, among others, sit outside it. Singapore's dollar is inside ."},{"id":"s1","h":"The bank you can't bank with","t":"Start with why you have never heard of it. Whenever a company swaps Singapore dollars for US dollars, or a fund hedges a euro position, the trade is agreed in seconds on a screen. That is the easy part. The hard part comes after: two payments, in two currencies, through two countries' payment systems that open and close at different hours. The agreeing is famous. The moving of the money is plumbing. The plumbing has a name: CLS Bank International , a New York institution regulated by the US Federal Reserve, according to CLS . Ordinary people and firms can't open an account there. Its direct customers are about 75 big banks (\"settlement members\"); another roughly 38,000 participants use it through them. CLS itself says it settles about US$8tn a day (about S$10tn). For scale, the BIS counted US$9.6tn a day of FX trading in April 2025 ( BIS release ). The two numbers measure different things (settled value is not the same as trading turnover), so don't divide one by the other. The afternoon Herstatt closed A Frankfurt dealing desk, 26 June 1974, as the bank's doors shut. Illustration by Jun Wei Heung with generic figures, not real people. The closing time and sequence (marks paid in Frankfurt, then the New York correspondent suspending dollar payments) follow the BIS Quarterly Review, June 2026 . The scene is imagined, not a record of any bank's office."},{"id":"s2","h":"The afternoon Herstatt closed","t":"Here is the story the whole system is built on. On 26 June 1974 , German regulators shut Bankhaus Herstatt at 3.30pm Frankfurt time. Earlier that day, other banks had paid Herstatt Deutsche marks in Frankfurt for US dollars they expected in New York. But New York was hours behind. Herstatt's New York correspondent suspended its outgoing dollar payments, and the banks that had already paid their marks got nothing back. That account is in the BIS Quarterly Review, June 2026 . The lesson isn't about one weak bank. It is about time zones . Any trade where you pay first and receive later leaves you exposed for the gap in between, and the exposure is the whole trade, not a small fee. The BIS describes it as the full value for the period between delivering one currency and receiving the other. Banks call it Herstatt risk, and for decades it hung over the FX market. A 1996 report from the central-bank community (the Allsopp report, per Wikipedia ) called time on it, and the banks eventually built their own fix. An analogy: the locked swap box Imagine swapping a pile of cash for a pile of foreign notes with a stranger who is in another time zone. Hand yours over first and you can only hope. Or both of you drop your piles into one locked box, and it only opens once both are inside. That's the idea of \"payment versus payment\" (PvP): the two halves of a trade happen together or not at all."},{"id":"s3","h":"The fix: lock the swap","t":"CLS was the banks' answer. Wikipedia dates the Allsopp report to 1996, the company to 1997, CLS Bank to 1999, and the first live settlement to 9 September 2002 with seven currencies. Today CLS holds an account at the central bank of each currency it handles, so both halves of a trade can move at once, inside CLS's books. A 2015 Reserve Bank of Australia paper describes a daily window of 07:00 to 12:00 Central European Time for most currencies, shorter (07:00 to 10:00 CET) for the Asia-Pacific ones. In Singapore's winter clock that's roughly 2pm to 7pm (my arithmetic, so check it against CLS's current rules). That's the first trick: the lock . The second is netting : instead of sending every payment, CLS adds up what each bank owes and is owed in each currency and moves only the difference. CLS says this cuts the funding banks need by more than 96% on average. That figure is CLS's own claim. An analogy: the dinner tab that cancels out Three friends each owe the next one $10 after a dinner. Pay it all out and $30 moves around the table. Add it up first and you see everyone is exactly even, so nobody needs to pay. Real FX netting is messier, because the amounts and currencies differ, but the principle is the same. The numbers in this scene are made up for illustration. The 18 Currencies CLS settles, each with the central bank holding CLS's account. Singapore's is highlighted. US dollar Federal Reserve Bank of New York Euro European Central Bank Pound sterling Bank of England Japanese yen Bank of Japan Swiss franc Swiss National Bank Canadian dollar Bank of Canada Australian dollar Reserve Bank of Australia New Zealand dollar Reserve Bank of New Zealand Singapore dollar Monetary Authority of Singapore Hong Kong dollar Hong Kong Monetary Authority Korean won Bank of Korea Swedish krona Sveriges Riksbank Norwegian krone Norges Bank Danish krone Danmarks Nationalbank South African rand South African Reserve Bank Mexican peso Banco de México Israeli shekel Bank of Israel Hungarian forint Magyar Nemzeti Bank From CLS's currency list . Note what's missing: the Chinese yuan, Indian rupee, Indonesian rupiah, Malaysian ringgit and Thai baht are not among the 18."},{"id":"s4","h":"The $14tn day","t":"So how much of the market actually gets the lock? The BIS surveyed it for April 2025 and published the results in June 2026. It found gross FX obligations of more than $14tn a day (about S$18tn). Scroll through where they went. Percentages are the BIS's; the squares are one per percentage point. The lock · PvP 36% , about $5.2tn (S$6.7tn), settled payment-versus-payment, where one leg can't happen without the other. CLS is the biggest such system; non-CLS ones exist too. 36% PvP The cancel-out · netting 15% , more than $2tn , was netted before settling: offsetting trades cancelled out. That left just $337bn of it to settle by other means. 15% netted first Same company · in-house More than 35% were trades between two arms of the same bank, like its London desk and its New York branch. The money never really leaves the firm, so there's no stranger on the other side to stiff you. >35% same company A sliver · timing controls Only 3% settled over ordinary bank accounts with timing controls, such as paying only after confirming the other leg is on its way. That's a safeguard, but not a lock. 3% timing controls The exposed slice · no safeguard 10% , about $1.4tn (S$1.8tn) a day, had no protection at all : one side pays and just hopes the other follows. The Herstatt gap, still wide open. The BIS notes the other 90% used methods that remove or cut the risk. 10% no safeguard One day, 100 squares How FX obligations were settled, April 2025 Source: BIS Quarterly Review, June 2026 , \"FX settlement risk in April 2025\". The BIS's intragroup share is \"more than 35%\"; drawn as 36 squares so the grid sums to 100. This is one month in one survey, not a trend. Even in the most settled market on earth, one trade in ten still has the Herstatt gap wide open."},{"id":"s5","h":"Why it's a bottleneck","t":"A lock only helps on the doors it fits. Three things stop the lock from reaching every trade. The currency has to be one of the 18. Trades in currencies CLS can handle came to $12.2tn a day, more than four-fifths of the market, yet only 40% of them actually used the lock. For pairs CLS doesn't cover, almost nothing was locked: just 12% went through other lock-style systems. Those other systems are CCIL in India, CHATS in Hong Kong and Buna in the UAE, according to the BIS. Both sides have to be set up for it. Of the unprotected 10%, about three-quarters could not have used the lock even if they'd wanted to. The most common reason, the BIS says, was that one side of the trade simply wasn't connected to any locking system (57% of cases). And the trade has to be the right kind. Trades that settle the same day can't use the lock yet, and that explains 37% of the stuck trades; another 36% were in currencies the lock doesn't cover. (A trade can hit more than one of these, so the reasons add up to more than 100%.) The last quarter, $347bn a day , could have been locked and wasn't: the firms just didn't use it. Why trades stayed unprotected Of the unprotected trades that couldn't use the lock (about three-quarters of the 10%), the share blaming each reason. A trade can hit more than one, so they add to more than 100%. The other side wasn't connected to any lock 57% Wrong kind of trade (e.g. same-day) 37% Currency the lock doesn't cover 36% Covers the roughly three-quarters of unprotected trades that couldn't have used the lock. Source: BIS Quarterly Review, June 2026 , \"Reasons for gross bilateral settlement\". Who gets the lock, and who doesn't Share of trades that used the lock, by who was on each side, April 2025 Two big banks trading with each other 72% A bank trading with a fund or smaller firm 44% A bank trading with an ordinary company 32% 0.01% of the money due actually failed to arrive in the survey month: $121m between banks and $1.4bn elsewhere. The safeguards mostly work. The worry is the rare trade that slips through. Source: BIS Quarterly Review, June 2026 . The last row's \"ordinary company\" means businesses such as corporates; the first row leaves out trades inside one banking group. And there is the bigger worry. CLS isn't one lock among many: it's the single hub the market built, and regulators treat it that way. Wikipedia reports that in July 2012 a US regulator formally labelled CLS \"systemically important\", official code for too central to be allowed to fail. When the lock is one institution, \"what happens if the lock itself breaks?\" is a fair question. I found no record of a serious outage in these sources, but that's silence in what I read, not proof it can't happen."},{"id":"s6","h":"The Singapore angle","t":"Singapore is a major FX trading centre, and the Singapore dollar is one of CLS's 18. Wikipedia dates its addition to September 2003 , in the second wave after the original seven, and the Monetary Authority of Singapore (MAS) is the central bank holding CLS's SGD account. So an SGD/USD trade between two members can settle with the lock. The neighbours are a different story. The rupiah, ringgit and baht aren't CLS currencies. According to the BIS they are settled PvP through CHATS in Hong Kong, a system that has existed since 2000, or with less protection. For a Singapore treasury, the practical lesson is to know which of your currency pairs can be locked, and which depend on trust and timing. Asia-Pacific currencies' earlier pay-in window (2015 figures, see above) also means a late Asian afternoon is when the clock matters most."},{"id":"s7","h":"How we got here","t":"26 Jun 1974 Bankhaus Herstatt is closed at 3.30pm Frankfurt time, after counterparties have paid marks but before dollars arrive in New York. Herstatt 1996 The Allsopp report on FX settlement risk is published (per Wikipedia). 1997 to 1999 CLS Services is formed in the UK (1 July 1997); CLS Bank International is established as a New York \"Edge Act\" bank (1 Nov 1999). 9 Sep 2002 CLS Bank goes live with seven currencies: AUD, CAD, EUR, JPY, CHF, GBP, USD. 7 Sep 2003 Four more are added, including the Singapore dollar. 2004 to 2015 HKD, KRW, NZD and ZAR (Dec 2004), ILS and MXN (May 2008) and HUF (Nov 2015) complete the 18. Jul 2012 The US FSOC designates CLS systemically important (per Wikipedia). 15 Dec 2021 Record single-day settled value of $15.4tn (per Wikipedia). Apr 2025 The BIS survey month: $9.6tn a day of FX trading, more than $14tn of settlement obligations, 36% settled PvP. 36% Jun 2026 The BIS publishes the settlement-risk results. Nov 2026 You are here. So the bottleneck isn't a mine or a fab. It is a lock: the one institution that ties 18 central banks' clocks together for a few hours each day, so that nobody gets Herstatted. Next time you hear about a currency trade, ask two things. QUESTION 1 Why do two-thirds of the market still settle without the lock, and who bears the cost when a leg fails? QUESTION 2 What happens to currencies outside the 18 if shorter settlement cycles and same-day trading squeeze the time to fix errors?"},{"id":"sources","h":"Sources","t":"Each number and where it comes from. $14tn+ gross obligations; PvP $5.2tn (36%); netting >$2tn (15%), leaving $337bn; intragroup >35%; timing controls 3%; gross bilateral $1.4tn (10%); CLS-eligible pairs $12.2tn, 40% PvP; non-CLS pairs 12% PvP; CCIL, CHATS and Buna; counterparty PvP shares 72/44/32%; reasons 57/37/36%; $347bn PvP-eligible but gross; fails 0.01% ($121m and $1.4bn); Herstatt 26 June 1974, 3.30pm; non-comparability warning: BIS Quarterly Review, June 2026 . US$9.6tn a day of FX trading in April 2025: BIS media release, 30 Sep 2025 . Founded 2002; 18 currencies; 75+ settlement members; ~38,000 participants via them; about $8.0tn+ settled a day; funding reduction of over 96%; regulation by the Federal Reserve: CLS, About us . The 18 currencies and their central banks: CLS, eligible currencies . Allsopp report (1996), CLS Services (1997), CLS Bank (1999), go-live 9 Sep 2002, currency dates (SGD Sep 2003), FSOC designation (Jul 2012), $15.4tn record (15 Dec 2021): Wikipedia: CLS Group (secondary source; treat as indicative). Pay-in windows (07:00 to 12:00 CET; Asia-Pacific 07:00 to 10:00 CET), July 2015: Reserve Bank of Australia, CLS and RITS session times . CLS's own description of a 07:00 to 12:00 CET cycle: CLS FX ecosystem white paper, Nov 2023 (as seen in search excerpts). Currency: about S$1.29 per US$1, open exchange-rate data , 20 Nov 2026. Figures are rounded. Educational only. Not investment advice. This is a living document and will be updated as new BIS and CLS data appear."}]},{"id":"iodine","href":"explainers/iodine.html","title":"The Crystal Behind Your Scan and Your Screen: Who Controls the World's Iodine","short":"The crystal behind your scan and your screen","topic":"Iodine","category":"Commodities","date":"2026-11-21","dek":"A hidden element, three source areas, and a price that rose 3.5 times","sec":[{"id":"","h":"","t":"Explainer · Living document · Iodine The Crystal Behind Your Scan and Your Screen Iodine is in the dye that makes a CT scan readable and in the films inside LCD screens. You have almost certainly never thought about where it comes from. About two-thirds of it comes from one Chilean desert, and a single company says it sells roughly 37% of the world's supply. Since 2017 the price has more than tripled. Figures are USGS estimates for 2025. The US figure is withheld by the USGS, and China and Uzbekistan are not reported, so \"about two-thirds\" is the USGS's own statement for world production excluding the US. Source below. Graphic: Jun Wei Heung. By Jun Wei Heung · Words and graphics November 21, 2026 · 11 min read Copy link Sources This is about one small, obscure commodity. Production, reserves and price figures come from the US Geological Survey (USGS), which is the most neutral source I found. Market shares, the split of demand and customer concentration come from the 2024 annual report (Form 20-F) of SQM, the Chilean company that is the largest seller. SQM has an interest in the story, and these are its own estimates, so treat them as indicative. A few older figures come from a 2010 note by the research firm Roskill. The USGS's world production total leaves out the United States (output withheld) and China and Uzbekistan (not reported), so shares of \"the world\" are approximate. Conversions to Singapore dollars use about S$1.29 per US$1 (20 Nov 2026) and are rounded. This explains an industry; it isn't investment advice, or medical advice. Key findings Excluding the US, Chile produced about two-thirds of the world's iodine in 2025: 23,000 tonnes against 9,000 from Japan. Every other country the USGS reports is under 1,000 tonnes. SQM says five Chilean companies made about 60% of global iodine sales in 2024, SQM alone about 37% . Eight Japanese producers were about 23% , US producers nearly 5% . These are SQM's estimates. The biggest use is the dye for X-ray scans (about 37% of demand, SQM's estimate). LCD and LED screens take about 13% . The average import price in the US rose from US$19.55 a kilogram in 2017 to about US$68 in 2025 , roughly 3.5 times (USGS). That is about S$88 a kilogram."},{"id":"s1","h":"A crystal nobody thinks about","t":"Start with why you have never heard of it. Iodine is a grey-black crystal that most people know only as brown antiseptic or as an ingredient on a salt packet. Yet it is quietly written into two very different supply chains. When a hospital runs a contrast-enhanced CT scan, the dye contains iodine. When a factory makes the polarising film that sits inside an LCD or LED screen, iodine is one of the raw materials. That's a medical product and a consumer-electronics part, joined by one element that almost nobody has on their radar. The element is also oddly hard to get. According to the USGS , seawater holds about 0.06 parts per million of it, and the oceans are estimated to hold some 90 billion tonnes . But the world doesn't get its iodine from the sea. It gets almost all of it from three places: Chilean desert mines, Japanese gas and oil fields and the brine wells of north-western Oklahoma. The room where iodine does its job A CT suite: dye goes in through the drip, and the vessels show up on screen. Illustration by Jun Wei Heung with generic figures, not real people. The demand shares on the board (about 37% and 13% of iodine demand in 2024) are SQM's estimates from its 2024 Form 20-F . The room itself is imagined and not meant to depict any real hospital. Iodine crystals: a small lab sample, not industrial output. Photo: Dnn87 , CC BY 3.0 , via Wikimedia Commons."},{"id":"s2","h":"Three places it comes from","t":"The USGS lists reported 2025 output for seven countries, and the first two are the whole story. Chile made about 23,000 tonnes and Japan about 9,000. Everything else the USGS could report was below 1,000 tonnes each. The US is third overall but its number is withheld, to avoid revealing company data. The USGS says that, excluding the US, Chile made about two-thirds of the world's iodine. Where iodine is produced Estimated mine production, 2025, tonnes of iodine Chile 23,000 Japan 9,000 Turkmenistan 800 Iran 700 Azerbaijan 210 Indonesia 50 Russia 8 Source: USGS Mineral Commodity Summaries 2026, iodine . The USGS total of about 34,000 tonnes excludes the US (withheld), and China and Uzbekistan also produce but are not reported. Estimates. The three sources work in different ways. In Chile , iodine comes from a mineral called caliche ore, which SQM's filing describes as \"unique\". In Japan , it is recovered from brine that comes up with natural gas. INPEX , one Japanese producer, says seven companies make iodine there, about 80% of it in Chiba prefecture, which has \"one of the world's largest brine deposits\". The Oklahoma iodine, which the USGS says three companies made in 2025, also comes from brine wells. The USGS describes iodine production as a by-product business: of gas, nitrates and oil. Humberstone, an abandoned saltpetre (nitrate) works in Chile's Atacama desert. This is a historic site, not a modern iodine plant, but it shows the landscape and the nitrate industry that Chile's iodine is tied to. Photo: Benjamin Goetzinger , CC BY-SA 4.0 , via Wikimedia Commons. Now the twist. Chile produces most of the iodine, but it isn't where most of the reserves are. The USGS puts Japan's iodine reserves at 4.9 million tonnes against Chile's 750,000 , out of a world total of more than 6.3 million. By my arithmetic, at 2025 rates that's roughly 540 years of Japanese output but only about 33 years of Chilean output. Reserves are an economic estimate, not a forecast, and Chile's figure was revised this year. But it shows that today's leader isn't necessarily the biggest holder. Who holds the reserves Estimated iodine reserves, thousand tonnes Japan 4,900 Chile 750 United States 250 Azerbaijan 170 Russia 120 Turkmenistan 70 Iran 40 Source: USGS Mineral Commodity Summaries 2026 . The USGS gives a world total of more than 6.3 million tonnes. The \"years of output\" comparison in the text divides reserves by 2025 production and is my arithmetic, not a USGS figure."},{"id":"s3","h":"Who sells it","t":"The ore is one thing and the sellers another. SQM's 2024 filing gives the best view I found of who sold how much, and it is the company's own estimate. Scroll through; one square is about one percentage point of world sales in 2024. The leader · SQM SQM estimates its sales were about 37% of global iodine sales by volume in 2024. It sold more than 14.5 thousand tonnes , a record, and earned US$968 million (about S$1.25bn) from iodine and its derivatives. 37% SQM The rest of Chile Four other Chilean producers made about 23% : Cosayach, ACF Minera, Algorta Norte and Atacama Minerals. Add SQM and, by its estimate, five Chilean companies made about 60% of the world's sales. 23% four others Japan Eight Japanese producers made about 23% , including recycled iodine, SQM estimates. Japan's iodine comes from brines that rise with natural gas. 23% Japan The United States US producers made nearly 5% . The USGS says three companies pump it from brine wells in Oklahoma, and one opened an eighth plant in 2025. ~5% US Everyone else What's left is about 12% . That is my subtraction, not a figure in the filing. SQM also says about 16% of the supply comes from recycling. That overlaps with these groups, so the squares are a guide, not an exact split. ~12% remainder One year, 100 squares World iodine sales by producer group, 2024 (SQM's estimates) Source: SQM Form 20-F for 2024 , \"Iodine: Competition\". The 12% remainder is my arithmetic. Recycling overlaps with the groups, so don't add it on top. Three-fifths of the world's iodine is sold by five companies, all in one Chilean desert."},{"id":"s4","h":"Where it goes","t":"What is all this iodine for? The USGS lists the leading uses, in descending order of quantity, as X-ray contrast media, LCDs, pharmaceuticals, iodophors, animal feed and fluorochemicals. SQM puts numbers on them. X-ray contrast media took about 37% of demand in 2024. The company says iodine's \"high atomic number and density\" make it suited to the job: it helps vessels and organs stand out on a scan. An analogy: a highlighter for X-rays An ordinary X-ray sees through most of the body in similar shades of grey, a bit like a page where every line is printed in the same pale ink. Iodine is dense, so it blocks X-rays strongly. Put it in the blood and the vessels show up, like a highlighter drawn over one line on the page. The scene is a simple picture of the idea, not a medical diagram. LCD and LED screens are the other big use. Roskill, a research firm, wrote in 2010 that iodine in optical polarising film (a layer inside an LCD) already made up about 12% of total consumption, and that demand for it had grown by 22% a year since 2000. That note is old. SQM's 2024 estimate is about 13% . The two sources measure differently, so don't read it as a trend, but the screen share looks broadly similar. The rest is a long tail of other uses. What the world's iodine is used for Estimated share of iodine demand, 2024, percent (SQM's estimates) X-ray contrast media 37% Pharmaceuticals 13% LCD and LED screens 13% Animal nutrition 7% Other applications 7% Iodophors and povidone-iodine 6% Fluoride derivatives 6% Biocides 5% Nylon 3% Human nutrition 3% Source: SQM Form 20-F for 2024 . SQM says the pharmaceutical figure is what it believes. The shares add to 100. The USGS ranks the uses in a similar broad order but gives no percentages. One more thing about demand: the USGS notes that \"no comparable substitutes exist\" for iodine in many of its main uses, including animal feed, catalytic, nutritional, pharmaceutical and photographic ones. Bromine and chlorine can stand in for biocide, colorant and ink uses, but they are \"usually considered less desirable\". When a buyer can't swap the input, the price is the only valve."},{"id":"s5","h":"The price","t":"Which brings us to the price. The USGS tracks the average value of iodine imported into the US (including insurance and freight). In 2017 it was US$19.55 a kilogram . By 2025 it was an estimated US$68 , about 3.5 times as much and roughly S$88. Note that this is a US import value, not a global spot price, and that it rises in every one of the eight year-to-year steps shown. The price of iodine, US imports Average import unit value, US$ per kilogram 2017 $19.55 2018 $22.46 2019 $26.38 2020 $31.57 2021 $32.72 2022 $45.81 2023 $61.55 2024 $61.84 2025 (estimate) $68 Sources: USGS Mineral Commodity Summaries 2026 (2021 to 2025) and 2022 (2017 to 2020). Cost, insurance and freight value of crude iodine imports into the US. The 2021 value was an estimate of 32 in the 2022 edition; the later figure is shown. Nominal dollars, not adjusted for inflation. SQM's own numbers tell a similar story from the seller's side. It says its average iodine price was about US$67 a kilogram in 2024 and about US$68 in 2023, and that its volumes rose from 12.7 thousand tonnes in 2022 to 13.1 in 2023 and 14.5 in 2024. Iodine revenue went from US$754 million to US$892 million to US$968 million. The company also estimates the market grew about 7 to 8% in 2024, and expected prices to stay \"relatively stable, due to limited market supply\" in 2025. An analogy: one tap, a longer queue Imagine a barrel that fills from one tap at a fixed pace, while the queue of people with cups gets a little longer each year. Nobody can make the water run faster by wanting it more. The tap can only grow when someone builds a new one, which takes time. In the meantime, the price of a cup is what keeps the queue in order. Supply can grow: the USGS says one US producer opened an eighth plant in 2025 and a leading Chilean producer planned to add 4,000 tonnes a year of capacity. But it grows plant by plant."},{"id":"s6","h":"Why it's a bottleneck","t":"A bottleneck needs a few things: few sources, few substitutes, and a market where a few buyers meet a few sellers. Iodine has all three, though none of them is extreme. Few sources. The USGS says most of the world's iodine comes from just \"three areas\": Chile's nitrate mines, Japan's gas and oil fields, and Oklahoma's brine wells. Of US imports in 2021 to 2024, 88% came from Chile and 11% from Japan. A supply hiccup in one desert could quickly turn into a price spike. I haven't found a record of a major disruption in the sources I read, so that is a risk, not a history. Few substitutes. The USGS says there are none comparable in many main uses (see Part 4). Few, big buyers. SQM says its ten largest customers took 77% of its iodine sales in 2024, and two customers each bought at least 10%, together about 33%. It sold to 131 customers in about 33 countries. Its sales are made on spot orders or under supply agreements with minimum and maximum annual commitments, with prices adjusted periodically. When a few buyers meet a few sellers, the price is set in negotiation, not at a corner shop. There is one softener: recycling . SQM estimates about 16% of the world's iodine supply now comes from recycled waste, mainly from China and Japan. It is a growing source. But it is a topping, not a replacement for the mines. Where SQM sells its iodine Share of SQM's iodine revenue by region, 2024, percent Asia and others 43% Europe 38% North America 16% Central and South America (excl. Chile) 2% Source: SQM Form 20-F for 2024 , geographical breakdown of iodine revenues. Rounded by SQM (the shares add to 99). Chile itself was 0%. The Singapore angle. I found no Singapore-specific iodine data, so I won't invent any. What the filing does say is that Asia and others is SQM's biggest iodine region at 43% , ahead of Europe. It doesn't break that down by country. For a Singapore business or AI student, the lesson is about method: a key input to a familiar product can be a commodity you never see on a quote, and a good supply-chain map should go down to that level."},{"id":"s7","h":"How we got here","t":"Before 1959 Seaweed is a major source of iodine. The USGS says it remains a large resource, but is less economical than iodine from gas, nitrates and oil. 2000 to 2009 Roskill estimates iodine demand for polarising film grew 22% a year from 2000, reaching about 12% of total consumption in 2009. 12% 2010 Roskill says four producers (SQM, Cosayach, Ise Chemicals and Godo Shigen) hold about two-thirds of installed capacity. 2017 US import price: US$19.55 a kilogram. $19.55 2021 to 2023 US import price rises from US$32.72 to US$45.81 to US$61.55. 2024 SQM sells a record 14.5 thousand tonnes. Five Chilean companies hold about 60% of world sales, by its estimate. 60% 2025 US import price an estimated US$68. A US producer opens its eighth plant; a leading Chilean producer plans a 4,000-tonne capacity increase. Feb 2026 The USGS publishes its 2026 summary: Chile 23,000 tonnes, Japan 9,000. Nov 2026 You are here. So the bottleneck here isn't a chip or a mine you have heard of. It is a crystal in a hospital dye and a screen film, dug mostly from one desert and sold by a handful of firms. Next time you see an element on the periodic table, ask two things. QUESTION 1 If supply only grows plant by plant, who absorbs the cost when demand for scans and screens rises faster? QUESTION 2 Could Japan's large reserves and a growing recycling trade loosen Chile's hold, and how fast?"},{"id":"sources","h":"Sources","t":"Each number and where it comes from. Production by country (2025e), reserves, \"about two-thirds\" Chile statement, three source areas, US import sources 88% Chile and 11% Japan (2021 to 2024), 2021 to 2025 US import prices, leading uses in order, \"no comparable substitutes\", seawater 0.06 ppm and 90 billion tonnes, seaweed history, US eighth plant and Chilean +4,000 tonnes: USGS, Mineral Commodity Summaries 2026: Iodine (published February 2026). US import prices for 2017 to 2020: USGS, Mineral Commodity Summaries 2022: Iodine . Market shares (SQM 37%, five Chilean 60%, eight Japanese 23%, US nearly 5%, recycling 16%), demand split by use, volumes and revenues 2022 to 2024, prices of about US$67 and US$68, customer concentration (ten largest 77%; 131 customers in about 33 countries), regional revenue shares, \"limited market supply\" quote, caliche ore: SQM, Form 20-F for 2024 (SEC) . A company filing by the largest seller: its estimates, not independent data. Polarising film at 12% of consumption, 22% a year growth since 2000, four producers at two-thirds of capacity (2010 data): Roskill via PR Newswire, 13 Jul 2010 , and AZoM, 14 Jul 2010 . Seven Japanese producers, about 80% in Chiba, brine deposits: INPEX, Naruto gas project page . (SQM counts eight Japanese producers; the two counts differ and I have not reconciled them.) Photos: Dnn87, CC BY 3.0 ; Benjamin Goetzinger, CC BY-SA 4.0 , both via Wikimedia Commons. Currency: about S$1.29 per US$1, open exchange-rate data , 20 Nov 2026. Figures are rounded. Educational only. Not investment or medical advice. This is a living document and will be updated as new USGS and company data appear."}]},{"id":"bond-market","href":"explainers/bond-market.html","title":"One Seesaw, Two Fears: How the Fed Uses the Bond Market","short":"One seesaw, two fears","topic":"Central banking","category":"Market infrastructure","date":"2026-11-21","dek":"The price-yield seesaw, QE and QT, and the 2020–2023 rate cycle","sec":[{"id":"","h":"","t":"Explainer · United States · Central banking One Seesaw, Two Fears: How the Fed Uses the Bond Market The US Federal Reserve aims for inflation of about 2% a year. When it fears a slump, it makes money cheap by pushing bond prices up and yields down. When prices run hot, it does the reverse. Between 2020 and 2023 it did both, in the space of three years. Here's how one seesaw explains it all. By Jun Wei Heung · Words and graphics November 21, 2026 · 10 min read Copy link Sources A plain-English model of how the Federal Reserve uses the bond market, built around the 2020–2023 cycle. It simplifies a lot: the Fed sets a short-term interest rate and buys or runs down bonds, while the yields on longer bonds are set by investors, who also react to inflation, growth and government borrowing. Figures come from the Fed, the US Treasury and the Bureau of Labor Statistics (see Sources). Educational only, not a forecast and not investment advice. The short version A bond's price and its yield move in opposite directions . Push the price up and the yield falls; let the price drop and the yield climbs. The Fed aims for about 2% inflation . It never wants prices to fall; it wants them rising slowly and steadily. Afraid of a slump (2020), it cut its rate to near zero and bought so many bonds that its holdings went from about $4.2 trillion to nearly $9 trillion. Bond prices rose, yields fell, and borrowing got cheap. Afraid of inflation (2022–23), after prices rose 9.1% in a year, it raised its rate to 5.25–5.5% and let its bond holdings shrink. Bond prices fell, yields rose, and borrowing got expensive."},{"id":"s1","h":"The price-yield seesaw","t":"Start with the one rule that makes the rest work. A bond pays a fixed stream of cash. If you pay more for that fixed stream, the return you earn on your money, the yield , is lower. If the bond gets cheaper, the same fixed cash is a bigger return, so the yield is higher. Price and yield sit on opposite ends of a seesaw. The seesaw Two ways to tip it, and what each does to borrowing Price ↑ Yield ↓ Borrowing gets cheaper. Money flows into the economy. vs Price ↓ Yield ↑ Borrowing gets dearer. Spending and lending cool. Whoever moves bond prices, the Fed buying, investors rushing in, or investors selling out, moves yields the opposite way. Mortgages and company loans are priced off those yields, which is how the seesaw reaches the rest of the economy. The Fed on the seesaw Push one end down, the other end goes up. Illustration by Jun Wei Heung. A generic figure, not a real person. So how does the Fed push on the seesaw? It has two tools. The main one is its policy rate , the interest rate banks charge each other overnight. The second is buying bonds, or letting the bonds it owns run off. The rate works on bond prices in a way that surprises people, so it's worth one worked example. Why a rate rise makes old bonds cheaper A 10-year, $1,000 bond paying $30 a year, when new bonds start paying 5% Your old bond $30 a year 3% on $1,000 New bonds now pay $50 a year for the same $1,000 What a buyer will now pay for yours about $846 at that price, your $30 plus the $1,000 at the end works out to 5% a year Nobody pays full price for a bond paying 3% when new ones pay 5%. So the old bond's price falls until its yield matches the market: here to about $846. My arithmetic, for a bond with ten years left; longer bonds fall further. That's why bond prices tend to drop when rates rise, and rise when rates fall."},{"id":"s2","h":"Afraid of a slump (2020)","t":"In early March 2020, Covid hit. Factories closed, people stayed home, and a deep recession looked certain. The Fed's fear was a slump, and in the worst case prices falling outright, which makes debts harder to repay and makes people delay spending. Its answer was to make money as cheap as it could. 3 and 15 March 2020 The Fed cuts its rate twice in two weeks, from 1.5–1.75% to 0–0.25% , close to zero. The dash for cash Panicked investors sell almost everything, even US government bonds, to raise cash. Bond prices fall: the 10-year yield jumps from 0.54% to 1.18% between 9 and 18 March. The market itself is pushing the seesaw the wrong way. 23 March: quantitative easing The Fed promises to buy bonds in whatever amounts are needed. It pays with newly created money, so its holdings swell from about $4.2 trillion to $7 trillion by June. The seesaw Price ↑ · yield ↓ · the 10-year hits a record low of 0.52% on 4 August With the Fed buying, bond prices rose and yields fell. Cheap money then had two effects worth separating. Investors cheer With bonds paying almost nothing, investors look for returns elsewhere. Stocks rise. The lending catch Banks are flush with cash to lend, and mortgages get cheap. But nervous households and firms hang back; the bold ones grab cheap loans. Why \"quantitative easing\"? When the Fed's rate is already near zero, it can't cut much further. So it adds money another way: it creates new money (reserves held at the Fed) and uses it to buy bonds. That lifts bond prices, holds yields down, and leaves banks with cash to lend. It's \"quantitative\" because the Fed targets a quantity of purchases rather than a price."},{"id":"s3","h":"The turn (2021–22)","t":"Cheap money worked, and then some. Spending came back faster than factories and shipping could. Governments had handed out support, and the war in Ukraine pushed up energy and food. US consumer prices went from rising 0.1% a year in May 2020 to 9.1% in June 2022, the fastest since 1981. Now the Fed's bigger fear flipped. The cycle in one chart The Fed's rate and the 10-year government bond yield, monthly, 2019–2024 Fed policy rate (top of target range) 10-year Treasury yield (monthly average) Inflation, % a year Sources: Federal Reserve rate decisions; US Treasury daily yield curve (10-year, averaged by month); Bureau of Labor Statistics consumer price index (12-month change). Monthly averages smooth out daily spikes such as the March 2020 dash for cash. The Fed stopped buying bonds in March 2022 and started raising its rate the same month. Bond investors didn't wait. Expecting higher rates and worried that inflation would eat into fixed payments, they sold, and bond prices fell before most of the hikes had even happened. Look at the chart: the 10-year yield (gold) starts rising in early 2022, alongside the Fed's rate (black)."},{"id":"s4","h":"Afraid of inflation (2022–23)","t":"The Fed's goal now was to slow price rises back towards 2%, not to make prices fall. That's sometimes called disinflation . To get there it pushed the seesaw the other way, with both tools. March 2022 to July 2023 Eleven rate rises take the Fed's rate from near zero to 5.25–5.5% , its fastest increase since the early 1980s. From June 2022: quantitative tightening The Fed lets the bonds it owns mature without buying new ones, shrinking its holdings from about $9 trillion . It mostly doesn't sell; it stops replacing. The seesaw Price ↓ · yield ↑ · the 10-year peaks at 4.98% on 19 October 2023 People borrow and spend less Mortgages and company loans get expensive. Spending and hiring slow, and price rises ease: inflation is back to 3.0% by June 2023. That's the point. From here the story can go two ways, and the same policy looks either like a triumph or a mistake. Soft landing Inflation cools without a deep recession or a jump in unemployment. Investors relax, long-term yields drift down, and stocks recover . Hard landing Higher rates bite too hard. Firms cut jobs, borrowers default, and stocks fall . In 2022, stocks and bonds fell together. The Fed's two fears Same central bank, two fears. When it's afraid of a slump, it hands out cheap money to wake the economy up. When it's afraid of runaway prices, it holds up a stop sign to cool things down. Either way it's aiming for the same thing: prices rising about 2% a year."},{"id":"s5","h":"Side by side","t":"Strip away the events and the two playbooks are mirror images. One seesaw, two fears Afraid of a slump (2020) Afraid of inflation (2022–23) Inflation at the time 0.1% (May 2020) 9.1% (June 2022) The Fed's rate Cut to 0–0.25% Raised to 5.25–5.5% The Fed and bonds Buys them (QE): holdings to about $9 trillion Lets them mature without replacing (QT) Bond prices Up Down 10-year yield Down, to 0.52% Up, to 4.98% Borrowing Cheap; money flows Expensive; spending cools Hoped-for ending Recovery; stocks rise Soft landing; stocks recover Feared ending Inflation overshoots (it did) Recession; stocks fall Both columns aim at the same 2% target, from opposite sides. Sources: Federal Reserve, US Treasury, Bureau of Labor Statistics. Price up, yield down. Price down, yield up. Everything the Fed does to steer inflation is a choice about which way to tip that one seesaw. Words you'll see in the headlines Yield The yearly return a bond pays, as a percentage of what you paid for it. Hike / cut The Fed raising or lowering its policy rate. Hawkish / dovish Leaning towards higher rates to fight inflation (hawkish), or towards lower rates to support jobs and growth (dovish). Liquidity How easily money moves: how quickly you can turn something into cash, or how easy it is to borrow. QE / QT Quantitative easing: the Fed creating money to buy bonds. Quantitative tightening: the Fed letting its bonds mature without replacing them. Soft landing Cooling inflation without causing a recession. So next time you read that the Fed \"hiked\" or \"turned dovish\", ask two things. QUESTION 1 Is the Fed more afraid of a slump, or of inflation? QUESTION 2 Is this heading for a soft landing, or a hard one?"},{"id":"sources","h":"Sources","t":"Each number and where it comes from. The Fed's 2% inflation goal and why it doesn't want falling prices: Federal Reserve, \"Why does the Federal Reserve aim for inflation of 2 percent?\" Rate cuts on 3 and 15 March 2020 to 0–0.25%: Federal Reserve, 15 March 2020 ; purchases \"in the amounts needed\": Federal Reserve, 23 March 2020 . Rate rises from March 2022 to 5.25–5.5% in July 2023: Federal Reserve, 26 July 2023 ; quantitative tightening from June 2022 by not reinvesting maturing bonds: Federal Reserve, plans for reducing the balance sheet (4 May 2022) . The Fed's holdings, about $4.2 trillion before Covid, about $7 trillion by June 2020 and close to $9 trillion at the 2022 peak: Federal Reserve, balance sheet trends . 10-year yields, including 0.54% on 9 March 2020, 1.18% on 18 March 2020, the 0.52% low on 4 August 2020 and the 4.98% high on 19 October 2023, and the monthly averages in the chart: US Treasury, daily yield curve rates . Inflation of 0.1% (May 2020), 9.1% (June 2022, the largest since November 1981) and 3.0% (June 2023): Bureau of Labor Statistics, June 2022 CPI release ; June 2023 CPI release ; May 2020 CPI release . The worked example ($1,000 bond, $30 a year, ten years left, priced to yield 5%) is my own arithmetic. Educational only. Not investment, legal or tax advice. A simplified model of a complex system; real outcomes depend on far more than one seesaw."}]},{"id":"govt-bonds","href":"explainers/govt-bonds.html","title":"Lending to Governments: US Treasuries vs Singapore's Bonds","short":"Lending to governments","topic":"Government debt","category":"Market infrastructure","date":"2026-11-22","dek":"T-Bills, bonds and SSBs, and why the US and Singapore borrow for opposite reasons","sec":[{"id":"","h":"","t":"Explainer · United States & Singapore · Government debt Lending to Governments A bond is a loan you make to a government. The United States borrows to spend, and now pays more interest on its debt than it spends on defence. Singapore borrows even more, relative to its economy, yet almost none of it funds spending at all. Two countries, two opposite reasons to issue debt. By Jun Wei Heung · Words and graphics November 22, 2026 · 9 min read Copy link Notes This is a plain-English comparison of how two governments borrow. The figures are drawn from US sources (the Congressional Budget Office and the US Treasury) and Singapore's bond framework (MAS and the Singapore Savings Bond rules), as summarised in my notes below. Bond rules, rates and tax treatment change; check the official pages before acting on anything here. This explains an instrument; it isn't investment, legal or tax advice. Key findings A bond is a loan to a borrower , usually a government or company. You hand over cash now and get interest, then your money back at the end. In the US, interest on the debt hit about $963B in the first ten months of fiscal 2026, more than the $763B spent on defence , on roughly $40.1T of public debt. Singapore's government debt is about 172% of GDP , higher than the US, yet about 99% of it is issued to build markets and reserves , not to fund day-to-day spending. Both offer short T-Bills and longer bonds ; Singapore adds the Singapore Savings Bond , a saver-friendly bond you can redeem any month with no penalty."},{"id":"s1","h":"What a bond is","t":"A bond is a fixed-income instrument: a loan an investor makes to a borrower, usually a corporation or a government. You hand over your money, the borrower pays you interest for the use of it, and at the end, the maturity date, you get your original amount, the principal, back. Governments are the biggest borrowers of all, and the two below borrow for strikingly different reasons."},{"id":"s2","h":"USA: bills versus bonds","t":"The US Treasury sells debt across many maturities. The two ends of the range behave very differently: short Treasury Bills and long Treasury Bonds . T-Bills vs T-Bonds The short end and the long end of US government debt Feature Treasury Bills (T-Bills) Treasury Bonds (T-Bonds) Maturity Short-term: 4 to 52 weeks (max 1 year) Long-term: 20 or 30 years (min 20) How you get paid No coupons. You buy at a discount below face value and get the full face value at maturity; the gap is your profit. A fixed coupon paid to you every 6 months until maturity. Price swings if you sell early Almost none; they mature too fast to move much. Very high. If interest rates rise, the market value drops sharply. Main risk Reinvestment risk: when a bill matures, you reinvest the cash, and if rates have fallen you earn less. Inflation risk: a 30-year fixed rate can be eroded by decades of rising prices. Best used for Short-term parking: an emergency fund, a down payment next year, or cash waiting for a dip. Locking in income: a predictable payout every 6 months for 20 to 30 years. Tax Exempt from state and local tax; taxable federally. Exempt from state and local tax; taxable federally. Where to buy TreasuryDirect.gov (US residents) or a brokerage (foreigners). TreasuryDirect.gov (US residents) or a brokerage (foreigners). Source: US Treasury / TreasuryDirect. The trade-off is simple: bills are safe but must be rolled over; bonds lock in a rate but swing in value and can be eaten by inflation."},{"id":"s3","h":"USA: the interest problem","t":"Borrowing is not free, and for the US the bill has grown enormous. In the first ten months of fiscal 2026, the government spent about $963 billion on interest, more than the $763 billion it spent on defence. Interest is now the second-largest single spending category, growing faster than any other. More on interest than on defence US federal outlays, first ten months of fiscal 2026, US$ billions Social Security $1,370B Interest on the debt $963B Medicare $952B Defence (military) $763B Medicaid $598B Veterans' benefits $360B +14% How fast interest on the debt grew over the year, faster than Social Security, Medicare, Medicaid or defence. That's about $131 million an hour. Source: CBO Monthly Budget Review, as summarised in \"US Interest on the Debt Exceeds Defense Spending\" . Bars are to scale against Social Security. That interest sits on about $40.1 trillion of public debt. Just under half is held by other US investors, roughly a fifth by US government accounts, and about a ninth by the Federal Reserve. The last fifth, around $8.5 trillion , is held abroad, and the largest foreign holders are Japan, the United Kingdom and China . Because the debt keeps growing and the government still runs a deficit, the interest bill compounds, which is why it is projected to top $1 trillion in 2027 . Who holds the $40.1 trillion Click the foreign holders slice to break it down by country ← All holders Source: US Treasury (Debt to the Penny; TIC) and Federal Reserve H.4.1, April–August 2026, as summarised in \"US Interest on the Debt Exceeds Defense Spending\" . The foreign view splits the $8.5tn foreign total; \"other foreign\" is every holder outside the top three. Why rising rates bite twice When the Fed raises rates to fight inflation, the government must offer higher coupons to sell new debt and to roll over old debt. So the same rate rise that cools the economy also makes the government's own borrowing more expensive, feeding the interest bill."},{"id":"s4","h":"Singapore: three choices","t":"Singapore offers three main government instruments to savers today: short T-Bills , long SGS Bonds , and the Singapore Savings Bond (SSB) , a product built specifically for ordinary individuals. T-Bills vs SGS Bonds vs SSB Singapore's three government instruments for savers Feature T-Bills SGS Bonds SSB What it is Short-term debt sold at a discount Long-term bonds paying fixed interest Step-up bonds designed for individual savers Tenure 6 months or 1 year 2 to 50 years Up to 10 years Minimum S$1,000 S$1,000 S$500 Maximum No limit (subject to auction) No limit S$200,000 per individual How interest is paid At maturity (discount to face value) Fixed coupon every 6 months Step-up interest every 6 months; the rate rises the longer you hold Sell early? Yes, on the secondary market (price may move) Yes, on the secondary market (price may move) Yes, redeem any month, no penalty, at full face value CPF / SRS? Yes Yes SRS only (not CPF) Non-residents? Yes Yes Yes, with a CDP and local bank account Source: MAS and the Singapore Savings Bond rules. The SSB is the unusual one: a government bond you can pull out of any month at face value, with a rate that climbs the longer you stay."},{"id":"s5","h":"Singapore: borrowing it doesn't need","t":"Here is the twist. Singapore's government debt is about 172% of GDP , well above the US and far above the 100% mark often treated as a warning line. On its face that looks alarming. It isn't, because the debt is raised for an entirely different purpose. What Singapore's borrowing is for Of government debt at about 172% of GDP ~99% Issued to develop the bond market (a benchmark risk-free yield curve), provide safe assets, and for reinvestment. Not for funding government operations. ~1% Funds large-scale, long-term infrastructure projects. Source: Singapore's government borrowing framework. Unlike most countries, Singapore does not borrow to cover a spending gap. Singapore runs controlled deficits with a reputation for fiscal discipline, and in surplus years the government collects more than it spends. Those surpluses go into the reserves and are reinvested through GIC, Temasek and MAS , rather than being spent. So where the US borrows because it spends more than it earns, Singapore borrows almost entirely to build financial plumbing and hold safe assets, while its actual budget stays tight."},{"id":"s6","h":"Two opposite playbooks","t":"Strip it down and the contrast is the whole story. Same instrument, opposite reasons United States Singapore Why it borrows To cover a spending gap (deficit) To build markets and reserves, not to spend Debt-to-GDP High and rising About 172%, mostly by design Interest burden ~$963B, above defence, and growing 14% a year Low; surpluses are reinvested, not consumed Saver-only product None like it SSB: redeem any month, step-up rate The worry Compounding interest on a $40.1T debt Little, so long as the reserves keep earning A debt ratio on its own tells you almost nothing. What matters is why the money was borrowed and what it's doing. The US borrows because it spends. Singapore spends carefully and borrows anyway, to build a market and a reserve. So next time you read a scary debt-to-GDP number, ask two things. QUESTION 1 Is this debt funding spending, or building something that earns? QUESTION 2 Does the interest bill compound, or do the assets behind it earn their keep?"},{"id":"sources","h":"Sources","t":"Where the figures come from. US T-Bill vs T-Bond features, tax treatment and where to buy: US Treasury / TreasuryDirect . US interest of $963B vs $763B defence in the first ten months of fiscal 2026, +14% growth, $131m an hour, and the $40.1T debt with Japan, the UK and China as the largest foreign holders: CBO Monthly Budget Review and US Treasury, as summarised in \"US Interest on the Debt Exceeds Defense Spending\" (r/dataisbeautiful) . The over-$1 trillion 2027 figure is a projection. Singapore T-Bills, SGS Bonds and the Singapore Savings Bond, including minimums, tenures, redemption and CPF/SRS eligibility: Monetary Authority of Singapore and the Singapore Savings Bond rules. Singapore's ~172% debt-to-GDP, the ~99% / 1% split, controlled deficits and reserves reinvested via GIC, Temasek and MAS: Singapore's government borrowing framework. Educational only. Not investment, legal or tax advice. Bond terms, rates and tax rules change; confirm with the official sources before acting."}]},{"id":"stablecoins","href":"explainers/stablecoins.html","title":"Keeping a Coin Worth a Dollar: How Stablecoins Hold (and Lose) Their Peg","short":"Keeping a coin worth $1","topic":"Crypto","category":"Crypto","date":"2026-11-23","dek":"Fiat-backed, crypto-backed and algorithmic coins, the Terra collapse, and Bitcoin, Ether and STRC","sec":[{"id":"","h":"","t":"Explainer · Crypto · Stablecoins Keeping a Coin Worth a Dollar A stablecoin promises one thing: that it will always be worth a dollar. Some keep that promise with cash in a bank, some with crypto locked in a contract, and some with nothing but code and confidence. The last kind once wiped out about $40 billion in three days. Here's what keeps a dollar a dollar, and what breaks it. By Jun Wei Heung · Words and graphics November 23, 2026 · 11 min read Copy link Notes This explains how stablecoins try to stay worth a dollar and why some fail, plus a few related crypto instruments. Mechanisms are described in plain terms; specific products (USDT, USDC, BOLD, TerraUSD, STRC) change their terms, reserves and rates over time, so check each issuer's current disclosures before relying on anything here. Where I describe risks, I am setting out how a design can fail, not predicting that a named product will. This is educational, not investment advice. Key findings A stablecoin is a crypto token that tries to track a reference value , almost always one US dollar. How it holds that peg is the whole story. Fiat-backed coins (USDT, USDC) hold cash and reserves 1:1 and lean on trust in the issuer and audits. Crypto-backed coins (BOLD) lock more crypto than they issue. Algorithmic coins use code alone. Algorithmic designs are the most capital-efficient and the most fragile. TerraUSD and its sister token LUNA collapsed together in a \"death spiral\" in May 2022, erasing roughly $40 billion . Even sound-looking designs can break: a crypto-backed coin can become underbacked if its collateral crashes faster than the system can sell it."},{"id":"s1","h":"What a stablecoin is","t":"Cryptocurrencies run on a blockchain : a shared ledger no single company controls. Most crypto prices swing wildly, which makes them hard to use as money. A stablecoin is the fix: a token designed to track a steady reference value, almost always one US dollar. Type \"1 USDC\" and it should be worth a dollar today, tomorrow, and next year. The promise is easy to make and hard to keep. A stablecoin's price is still set by trading: if enough holders sell and too few buy, the price can slip below a dollar , because the market clears at whatever price a seller will accept. What stops that slip, or fails to, is the coin's design. There are three main designs."},{"id":"s2","h":"Three ways to hold a dollar","t":"Every stablecoin answers one question: what actually stands behind the dollar it promises? Three designs, three kinds of backing What each type relies on to stay at $1 Type Fiat-backed Crypto-backed Algorithmic Backed by Cash & reserves (incl. Treasuries) Crypto locked in a contract, worth more than the coins issued Nothing but code and another token You trust The issuer, its banks and audits Public on-chain collateral and automated rules The mechanism and other buyers Strength Simple, widely accepted Transparent; no bank to trust Capital-efficient (little real collateral) Weakness You must trust reserves exist Collateral can crash faster than it sells Fragile; can spiral to zero Example USDT, USDC BOLD (backed by ETH) TerraUSD (UST) The trade-off runs left to right: more capital-efficient, less safe. Fiat-backed needs a dollar in a bank for every token; algorithmic needs almost no real collateral, which is exactly why it can fail."},{"id":"s3","h":"Fiat-backed: trust the issuer","t":"The simplest design. For every token in circulation, the issuer claims to hold one dollar of cash or safe reserves, often including US Treasuries. USDT (Tether) and USDC (Circle) work this way. Hand a token back and you should get a dollar. Because you can't see the bank account, this design runs on trust , propped up three ways: issuers publish periodic audits or attestations to show the reserves exist; they keep policies to restrict or delay redemptions if too many people cash out at once; and the market itself arbitrages small gaps. Still, the price is set by trading, so a fiat-backed coin can briefly slip under a dollar when sellers pile up faster than buyers, which is exactly what a bank run looks like."},{"id":"s4","h":"Crypto-backed: over-collateralise","t":"Here there's no bank to trust. Instead you lock more crypto than the stablecoin you mint, and everyone can see the collateral on-chain. BOLD is a crypto-backed stablecoin that targets $1 and is backed by ETH . You lock ETH in a smart contract, borrow freshly created BOLD against it, and if your collateral falls too far, the system liquidates it to cover the debt. A worked example Lock about $2,000 of ETH and you can borrow roughly $1,000 of BOLD. Spend that BOLD as dollars, then later repay the $1,000 of BOLD plus interest to unlock your ETH. You've borrowed against your crypto without selling it. The extra collateral, the \"over\" in over-collateralised, is the safety buffer. That buffer is the whole defence, and it can fail. The danger isn't an ETH price drop by itself; it's a drop so fast that the system can't sell the collateral in time . How a crypto-backed coin becomes underbacked One loan, a fast crash, and a shortfall Step 1 · borrow Someone locks $2,000 of ETH and borrows 1,000 BOLD . Step 2 · crash ETH suddenly crashes; the collateral is now worth only $700 . Step 3 · can't sell Network congestion or too few buyers stop the system selling it in time. Step 4 · shortfall Selling now raises only $700 against a $1,000 debt. Multiply across many loans and BOLD can become underbacked . Simplified numbers. The design depends on liquidations keeping up with prices. If confidence then goes, holders may sell BOLD below $1, worsening the loss. An ETH drop alone isn't collapse; a drop plus failing liquidations is. Source: Liquity documentation."},{"id":"s5","h":"Algorithmic: the Terra spiral","t":"The most capital-efficient design holds almost no real collateral. It keeps the peg with code and a second token. TerraUSD (UST) was the famous example: each UST could always be swapped for $1 worth of LUNA , a companion token whose value rested on how useful the Terra network was. In calm markets, that swap kept UST at a dollar. Under stress, it became a trap. The death spiral May 2022: how UST and LUNA pulled each other down Illustration by Jun Wei Heung. A simplified loop of the mechanism, not a tick-by-tick record. The loop feeds itself: each step makes the next worse. UST slips below $1. Selling pressure pushes it under the peg. Holders swap UST for LUNA. Each gets $1 worth of newly made LUNA and sells it, pushing LUNA's price down. Terra prints more LUNA. To give the next person $1 of LUNA at a lower price, it must mint even more, driving the price down again. Confidence breaks. Terra tried to save one falling token by printing another. Buyers fled, and both UST and LUNA collapsed together. Terra tried to save a falling token by printing another one. The market lost faith in both."},{"id":"s6","h":"Bitcoin, Ether and a Bitcoin-backed stock","t":"Stablecoins live on top of two networks worth knowing. Bitcoin was the first widely successful cryptocurrency: scarce digital money with no central operator, which is why people call it \"digital gold.\" Its coin is BTC . Ethereum is a platform where developers build things on top: stablecoins, lending, NFTs and other apps. Its coin is Ether (ETH) , the same ETH that backs BOLD above. Bitcoin's rise has also spawned instruments built around holding it. One is STRC , a perpetual preferred share issued by Strategy (formerly MicroStrategy), which uses the proceeds to buy and hold Bitcoin. STRC, in plain terms A bond-like share that funds a Bitcoin pile Feature What it means What it is A perpetual preferred share from Strategy; proceeds buy Bitcoin, which the company holds in reserve. Perpetual No maturity date; it doesn't \"come due\" like a bond. Dividend About 11.5% a year (roughly 1% a month). Buy one $100 unit and you'd receive about $1 each month. Price target Aims to trade near $100. If it slips, the dividend can be raised to attract buyers. As described for 2026; terms can change. \"Perpetual preferred\" means a share that pays a set dividend forever, with no repayment date. Why critics are wary of STRC The attraction is obvious: a high, bond-like yield. The worry is where the yield comes from. Critics argue the structure leans on new investors' money and on Bitcoin continuing to rise to cover the dividend, needing at least a couple of percent of Bitcoin appreciation a year just to break even on the payout. Paying dividends largely out of fresh capital is the hallmark of a Ponzi-like structure , and sceptics call the model unsustainable on those grounds. Whether that criticism proves right depends on Bitcoin's price and Strategy's own disclosures, which you should read directly. The thread through all of it is the same question a stablecoin asks: what actually stands behind the promise? Cash you must trust, crypto you can see, code you have to believe in, or a rising asset that has to keep rising. So next time something promises a steady value or a fat yield, ask two things. QUESTION 1 What stands behind the dollar, and can you see it? QUESTION 2 Is the yield paid from earnings, or from the next buyer?"},{"id":"sources","h":"Sources","t":"Where the figures and claims come from. Stablecoin types, fiat-backing 1:1, audits and redemption controls, and that exchange price is set by trading: general stablecoin descriptions from the issuers ( Tether , Circle ). BOLD mechanics (ETH collateral, borrowing, liquidation, the under-collateralisation scenario): Liquity documentation . The TerraUSD / LUNA design and the May 2022 collapse, including the roughly $40 billion in value lost: contemporary reporting on the Terra collapse. Bitcoin as the first widely successful cryptocurrency and \"digital gold\"; Ethereum as a platform for stablecoins, lending and NFTs: general network descriptions. STRC as a perpetual preferred share from Strategy funding Bitcoin purchases, with a stated ~11.5% annual dividend and a ~$100 target: Strategy's disclosures. The Ponzi-like criticism is a characterisation by sceptics, not a statement of fact about the company. Educational only. Not investment, legal or tax advice. Crypto products are volatile and can lose all their value; confirm every figure with the issuer before acting."}]},{"id":"chip-share","href":"explainers/chip-share.html","title":"Three Companies, One Industry: AMD vs Intel vs Nvidia","short":"AMD vs Intel vs Nvidia","topic":"Semiconductors","category":"Chips & hardware","date":"2026-11-24","dek":"Market share across data centre, client, gaming and embedded, and how the lead changed hands","sec":[{"id":"","h":"","t":"Explainer · United States · Semiconductors Three Companies, One Industry Intel once owned the processor outright. Today the fight between AMD, Intel and Nvidia splits across separate markets, server and PC chips, AI accelerators, gaming graphics, and embedded, and in each of them the leader is a different company. Here is who holds what, and how the lead changed hands. By Jun Wei Heung · Words and graphics November 24, 2026 · 10 min read Copy link Notes A market-share snapshot of three US chip companies, segment by segment, as of the third quarter of 2026, with historical context. Current figures come from Mercury Research (x86 CPUs), Jon Peddie Research (graphics) and industry estimates (AI accelerators, FPGAs) as summarised in my notes; shares move every quarter and different trackers count differently (units vs revenue, discrete vs all graphics), so treat them as approximate. This explains a market; it isn't investment advice. Key findings There is no single \"chip market.\" AMD, Intel and Nvidia lead in different places : Intel still ships the most PC CPUs, AMD leads cloud server CPUs, owns consoles and leads FPGAs, and Nvidia dominates AI and gaming graphics. In x86 server CPUs , AMD hit a record 35.5% of units in Q3 2026 (up from 27.8% a year earlier), and 46.8% of mainstream cloud/enterprise servers, a level it last approached in the Opteron days of 2006. In AI accelerators , Nvidia holds roughly 75–81% of revenue; AMD about 5–7%, with custom hyperscaler chips (Google TPU, Broadcom-built) around 12–15%. In discrete gaming GPUs , Nvidia holds about 90% of shipments. But AMD powers essentially all current-generation consoles and most PC handhelds."},{"id":"s1","h":"How we got here","t":"For most of the x86 era, there was one giant and one gadfly. Intel, founded in 1968, set the pace; AMD lived in its shadow, with one brief exception. In 2006 AMD's Opteron server chips reached about a quarter of the x86 server market, its high-water mark. Then a weak chip design (Bulldozer) and years of losses pushed its server share toward the low single digits, and by the mid-2010s Intel controlled roughly 90% or more of server CPUs. The comeback came with the Zen architecture and the EPYC server line from 2017. AMD broke back through 10% server share in 2021 and kept climbing. Meanwhile Nvidia, long the gaming-graphics leader, turned its GPUs into the default engine of the AI boom, and AMD bought its way into embedded chips by acquiring Xilinx in 2022. The result is today's split market. The lead changes hands AMD's share of x86 server CPUs, the key turning points 1968 Intel founded; it will dominate x86 for decades. 2006 AMD's Opteron peaks near 26% of the x86 server market. 2017 After the Bulldozer era, AMD is down to 0.8% of x86 server chips at the end of 2017; Intel has the rest. 2017 Zen and EPYC launch; the climb back begins. 2021 AMD breaks back above 10% server share (10.7% at year end). 2026 AMD hits a record 35.5% of server units; Intel's overall x86 share drops below 70% for the first time since 1995. Turning points only; the timelapse below plays the full series. Figures from Mercury Research and contemporary reporting; see notes. Watch the share change AMD vs Intel, share of x86 server CPUs shipped. Press play. ▶ Play Data centre Server CPUs AI accelerators Client PCs Desktop CPUs Laptop CPUs Gaming Discrete Integrated Consoles 2006 Every point is a reported quarterly figure, with no gaps filled in; a faint dashed line marks a stretch with no published data. CPU shares are Mercury Research unit shares of x86 chips (excluding IoT), so Intel's share is the rest; Apple and Arm chips aren't counted. Graphics cards are Jon Peddie Research shares of desktop add-in boards; \"Integrated graphics\" is its count of every PC graphics chip, including those built into processors. AI figures mix revenue and shipment estimates from different firms, so read that race as rough. Sources below each point and in the notes."},{"id":"s3","h":"Data centre: two very different races","t":"The data centre is really two markets. In server CPUs , AMD's EPYC has stormed back: 35.5% of all x86 server units in Q3 2026, up from 27.8% a year earlier, and 46.8% once you narrow to mainstream cloud and enterprise two-socket servers, almost half. Intel's Xeon still holds the majority overall, but the gap is the narrowest in decades. In AI accelerators , the picture flips entirely. This is a $200 billion-plus market, and Nvidia owns roughly 75–81% of the revenue . AMD's Instinct line holds about 5–7% (and may climb toward 7–10% as its MI450 and Helios racks ship), while custom hyperscaler chips, Google's TPU and Broadcom-built accelerators, take around 12–15%. Intel is a rounding error here. That share is still largely won in training , where raw computing power decides. The bill that grows with every user is inference , answering their questions, which over a model’s life usually costs more than training it. There, the limit is often not arithmetic but how fast a chip can pull the model out of memory. That opens the door to chips built around memory rather than compute, and is why AMD bought the inference-chip start-up Taalas. The memory wall, explained in the AMD story ."},{"id":"s4","h":"Client PCs: Intel still ships the most","t":"In the PCs people actually buy, Intel remains ahead by units. In Q3 2026 AMD held 29.9% of x86 desktop CPUs (down from 34.9% the prior quarter, as GPU supply bottlenecks hit gaming-PC builds) against Intel's 70.1%, and a record 29.2% of laptop processors against Intel's 70.8%. Combine client and server and AMD sits at 30.1% of all x86 units, up from 25.6% a year earlier, its highest ever, while Intel's share dipped below 70% for the first time since 1995."},{"id":"s5","h":"Gaming: it depends how you count","t":"Gaming graphics is the segment where the headline number misleads most. In discrete desktop graphics cards , Nvidia holds about 90% of shipments, AMD about 8%, and Intel about 2%. But count integrated graphics too , the graphics built into Ryzen and Intel processors, and it reshuffles: in the second quarter of 2026, Intel had about 56% , Nvidia about 23% and AMD about 21%. Intel leads simply because most PCs, especially cheap laptops, have no separate graphics card at all. Its share has slipped from 71% at the end of 2022 as Ryzen chips with built-in Radeon graphics sell more. And there's a market Nvidia isn't in at all. AMD's semi-custom chips power essentially 100% of current-generation consoles , the PlayStation 5 and PS5 Pro and the Xbox Series X and S, with only Nintendo's Switch using a different design. AMD also powers most Windows and SteamOS handhelds, the Steam Deck, ROG Ally and Legion Go. Nvidia's near-monopoly on discrete cards sits next to AMD's near-monopoly on consoles."},{"id":"s6","h":"Embedded: the quiet lead","t":"The least-watched segment is one AMD bought its way into. With Xilinx , AMD holds more than half of the FPGA market, the reconfigurable chips used in telecoms, aerospace, industrial and defence gear, according to Yole Group. That lead is old: back in 2010, Xilinx already had about 52% to Altera's 39%, by the Linley Group's count. Altera, bought by Intel in 2015, became independent again in 2025 when Silver Lake took a majority stake; Microchip and Lattice fill out the rest. It's a small, high-margin market, about $11–12 billion a year, that rarely makes headlines. There is no \"chip champion.\" There are several separate races, and three companies that each win different ones. So next time someone says a company \"leads the chip market,\" ask two things. QUESTION 1 Which segment do they actually lead? QUESTION 2 Are they counting units or revenue, and which slice?"},{"id":"sources","h":"Sources","t":"Where the figures come from. Q3 2026 shares (AMD 35.5% of server units, 29.9% desktop, record 29.2% laptop, 30.1% of client and server combined) and Intel's x86 share below 70% for the first time since 1995: Mercury Research via HotHardware , Hardware Busters , TechRadar and TechPowerUp . Other outlets quote 33.9% overall, a wider count that includes IoT and semi-custom chips. Timelapse, server/desktop/laptop unit shares every quarter (Mercury Research, excluding IoT): Q3 2016–Q4 2024 from the running tables in Tom's Hardware's Q4 2024 report and Q4 2023 report ; Q1–Q4 2025 and Q1 2026 from Tom's Hardware ( Q3 2025 , Q4 2025 , Q1 2026 ), with earlier 2025 quarters read from their year-ago comparisons; Q2–Q3 2026 from TechRadar, HotHardware and Hardware Busters (above). Server share in 2005–2006 (7.4% in Q1 2005; 22.1% and 25.9% in Q1 and Q2 2006): Mercury Research via InfoWorld and eWeek . Mercury's separate server, desktop and laptop figures for 2007–2016 were not published in the press, so that stretch has no points. EPYC's recovery past 10% in 2021: The Next Platform . AI accelerators: Nvidia about 80.6% of cloud and data-centre AI processor revenue in 2020 (Omdia, via TechRadar ); about 80% in 2024 (analysts, via Motley Fool ); today's ~75–81%, AMD ~5–7% and custom chips ~12–15%: CNBC and industry estimates. Desktop graphics cards, by quarter (Jon Peddie Research; where a firm's share wasn't given it is the remainder, and the note says so). Q3 2018–Q3 2019 from PCGamesN ; Q4 2019 and Q3 2020 from Wccftech ; Q1 2020 from JPR via CGW ; Q2 2020 from PC Gamer ; Q3 2021 from JPR ; Q1–Q2 2022 from Tom's Hardware ; Q1–Q2 2023 from Tom's Hardware ; Q1–Q2 2024 from Tom's Hardware ; Q3 2024 from Wccftech ; Q1–Q3 2025 from Yahoo Finance , Tom's Hardware and TechSpot ; Q1 2026 from Igor's Lab . Fourth-quarter figures: 2017 from Digital Trends and TechRadar ; 2020 from Tom's Hardware ; 2021 from Jon Peddie Research ; 2022 from Tom's Hardware ; 2023 from Tom's Hardware ; 2024 from Tom's Hardware ; 2025 from PC Gamer ; mid-2026 (~90%, AMD ~8%, Intel ~2%) from Tom's Hardware . Integrated graphics (all PC graphics, including graphics built into processors; Jon Peddie Research): Q4 2019 from Tom's Hardware ; Q4 2020 from Tom's Hardware ; Q3 2020 (Intel 62%) from Wccftech ; Q4 2021 from Tom's Hardware ; Q3 2022 (Intel 72%) from Notebookcheck ; Q4 2022 from Tom's Hardware ; Q2 2025 (Intel 67%, down from 76% a year earlier; Nvidia about a quarter of all PC GPUs) from PCWorld , with Nvidia's \"roughly one in four\" share corroborated by VG Times (AMD the remainder); Q3 2025 (Intel 61%, Nvidia 24%, AMD 15%) from pcpartsgeek, citing JPR ; Q2 2026 (Intel 56%, Nvidia 23%, AMD 21%) from Jon Peddie Research . FPGAs: Xilinx 52% and Altera 39% in 2010 ( Linley Group via Design & Reuse ); Xilinx/AMD \"more than 50%\" today ( Yole Group ); market of about $11–12 billion and Altera's 2025 sale to Silver Lake: MarketsandMarkets . console graphics by generation: the original Xbox (2001) used an Nvidia GPU and the PS2 Sony's own Graphics Synthesizer; the Xbox 360 (2005–06) used an ATI GPU and the PS3 an Nvidia one; the PS4, Xbox One, PS5 and Xbox Series X/S all use AMD semi-custom chips (ATI was acquired by AMD in 2006). AMD also powers most PC handhelds. Console and handheld chip suppliers: company specifications and announcements, and industry coverage. Educational only. Not investment advice. Market-share figures move every quarter and vary by tracker and by whether units or revenue are counted; confirm against the latest Mercury Research and Jon Peddie reports."}]},{"id":"derivatives","href":"explainers/derivatives.html","title":"Financial Weapons of Mass Destruction? How Derivatives Work","short":"How derivatives work","topic":"Derivatives","category":"Market infrastructure","date":"2026-11-23","dek":"Futures, options and swaps, hedging versus speculation, and why Buffett warned about them","sec":[{"id":"","h":"","t":"Explainer · Market infrastructure · Derivatives Financial Weapons of Mass Destruction? A derivative is a contract whose value comes from something else: a price, a rate, an index. A farmer uses one to lock in next season's wheat price; an airline uses one to cap its fuel bill. The same contracts also let a trader bet huge sums with little money down. In 2002 Warren Buffett called them \"financial weapons of mass destruction.\" Here is what they are, why they're useful, and how the same tools helped blow up LTCM, AIG and the 2008 crisis. By Jun Wei Heung · Words and graphics November 23, 2026 · 14 min read Copy link Sources This explains what derivatives are and how they're used, in plain terms, with simplified numbers to show mechanisms rather than exact market prices. The historical episodes (LTCM, AIG, 2008 and the later blow-ups) are summarised; the full stories are more involved than any one cause. Where I describe risks, I am setting out how a tool can fail when misused, not making a claim that any particular product or firm will. This is educational, not investment advice. Key findings A derivative is a contract that settles at a future date , and its value is derived from an underlying thing: a commodity price, an interest rate, a stock, an index. Contracts traded privately between banks had a face value of about $846 trillion in mid-2025, but were actually worth about $22 trillion : the face value is mostly a reference figure, not money at stake. Three building blocks cover most of the market: futures (fix a price now for a trade later), options (the right, not the duty, to trade at a set price), and swaps (exchange one stream of payments for another). The same contract can reduce risk or add it. A user with real exposure hedges ; a trader with none speculates , often with heavy leverage , controlling a large position for a small outlay. Buffett's 2002 \"weapons of mass destruction\" warning was about that leverage plus interconnection : when big players owe each other on derivatives, one failure can cascade. LTCM (1998), AIG (2008) and the wider 2008 crisis all showed it, and smaller blow-ups since (negative oil prices in 2020, Archegos in 2021, UK pension funds in 2022) show it still can. After 2008 the world's leaders pushed standard derivatives through clearing houses that stand between buyer and seller and demand collateral every day. That untangled the web of bilateral debts, but it also concentrated the risk in a few central hubs."},{"id":"s1","h":"What a derivative is","t":"Buy a share of a company and you own something directly. A derivative is one step removed: it's a contract whose value is derived from the price of something else, the \"underlying.\" The underlying can be almost anything with a price that moves: wheat, oil, a currency, an interest rate, a stock, a market index. You don't have to own the underlying to trade a derivative on it. The common thread is time . A derivative is an agreement made today about a transaction that settles later , at a price or on terms fixed now. That lets two parties move a future risk between them: one of them wants certainty about a future price, and the other is willing to take the other side. Everything else is a variation on that idea. The market built on that idea is enormous, and its headline number is easy to misread. Contracts traded privately between banks and big firms, known as over-the-counter (OTC) derivatives, had a notional value of about $846 trillion at the end of June 2025, several times the world's annual output. But notional is just the reference amount payments are calculated on: an interest-rate swap on $100 million doesn't put $100 million at risk, only the difference between two interest rates on it. What the contracts were actually worth, if every one were settled at market prices, was about $21.8 trillion . After netting out what the same firms owe each other, the real exposure was about $3 trillion . The big number and the real one OTC derivatives outstanding, end of June 2025, drawn to scale by area Notional is the amount payments are calculated on; gross market value is what it would cost to replace every contract at market prices; credit exposure is what's left after legally enforceable netting between the same firms. Exchange-traded futures and options are counted separately. Source: Bank for International Settlements, OTC derivatives statistics at end-June 2025."},{"id":"s2","h":"The three building blocks","t":"Most derivatives are built from three basic kinds of contract. Learn these and the rest are combinations. Futures, options and swaps What each contract commits you to Contract Futures Options Swaps What it is An agreement to buy or sell something at a set price on a set future date The right, not the obligation , to buy or sell at a set price before a deadline An agreement to exchange one stream of payments for another over time Do you have to act? Yes. Both sides are committed No. The holder chooses whether to use it; they pay a fee (the \"premium\") up front Yes, for the life of the deal Everyday example A farmer locks in a wheat price for the autumn harvest now You pay for the right to buy a stock at $100; if it jumps to $140 you exercise, if it falls you walk away Two firms swap a floating interest rate for a fixed one so each gets the payments it prefers Why use it Certainty: you know the price in advance Insurance: cap your downside, keep the upside, for the cost of the premium Fit: convert a payment you have into the kind you want Futures bind both sides; an option binds only the seller, giving the buyer a choice for an up-front premium; a swap trades two ongoing cash-flow streams. Nearly every exotic-sounding derivative is some mix of these three. Why an option is like insurance Profit at expiry on one option to trade a stock at $100, for a $5 premium Call (right to buy) Put (right to sell) You buy it You sell it Stock price at expiry: $120 Simplified: one share, a $100 strike, a $5 premium, no fees or interest. The buyer of an option can lose at most the premium; the seller pockets the premium but takes the open-ended side. Every option has both, which is why selling options for income can end badly."},{"id":"s3","h":"Hedging vs speculation","t":"The same contract can make the financial system safer or riskier . What changes is who's using it and why. Two people can hold the identical futures contract for opposite reasons. A hedger already has a real-world exposure and uses a derivative to cancel it out. An airline knows it must buy jet fuel for years to come, so it buys contracts that lock in the price; if fuel spikes, the gain on the contract offsets the higher bill. A speculator has no such exposure. They take the position purely to profit from a price move, often using leverage : a small deposit controls a large contract, so gains and losses are both magnified. One contract, two motives The split that decides whether a derivative dampens or amplifies risk The contract A futures contract on oil at a fixed price for delivery in six months. Used by a hedger An airline buys it to lock its fuel cost. It has a real exposure; the contract removes uncertainty. Risk goes down. Used by a speculator A trader with no fuel to buy takes the same position with borrowed size , betting on the price. The contract adds risk, and leverage magnifies it. Hedging and speculating aren't different products; they're different reasons for holding the same product. Markets need both: speculators provide the willing counterparties that let hedgers offload risk. Trouble starts when the speculative, leveraged side grows large and interconnected. How the airline's hedge works Fuel for next year, 100 million gallons, locked in with futures at $2.50 a gallon Jet fuel price next year: $3.50 a gallon Illustrative numbers. Whatever happens to the price, the bill plus the futures result adds up to the locked-in $250 million. The airline gives up the benefit if fuel gets cheaper, in exchange for certainty if it gets dearer. In practice hedges are partial and the fuel and the contract don't track each other perfectly. What leverage does to a small move A $100,000 oil position, held with a small deposit (the margin) Pay in full 20% down 10% down 5% down Oil price moves: −6% Simplified: futures exchanges set the deposit, called initial margin, and collect or pay out gains and losses every day. When losses eat into it, the broker makes a margin call for more cash, and if you can't pay, the position is closed at a loss. A derivative doesn't create or destroy risk by itself. It moves risk from someone who wants to shed it to someone willing to carry it."},{"id":"s4","h":"Buffett's warning","t":"In Berkshire Hathaway's 2002 annual letter, Warren Buffett set out at length why he distrusted the fast-growing derivatives market. His line became the most quoted verdict on the subject. \"We view them as time bombs , both for the parties that deal in them and the economic system… In our view… derivatives are financial weapons of mass destruction , carrying dangers that, while now latent, are potentially lethal.\" Warren Buffett, Berkshire Hathaway annual letter, 2002 His worry wasn't the contracts themselves; it was three things that come with a large, lightly watched derivatives market. Leverage lets positions grow far beyond the cash behind them. Interconnection means big institutions owe each other on these contracts, so one firm's failure becomes everyone's problem. And opacity : positions valued by models rather than market prices can hide how large and how fragile they really are, until they aren't. Within a decade, each of those played out. Buffett used them too The warning came from a user, not a bystander. A few years after the letter, Berkshire Hathaway sold long-dated put options on stock indices, collecting about $4.9 billion in premiums up front, and explained in later letters that it was comfortable because the contracts were few, mispriced in its favour, and needed little collateral. That fits his own test: he wasn't against derivatives, but against leverage, interconnection and positions nobody can see."},{"id":"s5","h":"When derivatives blew up","t":"Three big episodes show the failure modes Buffett named. In each, derivatives did exactly what they were designed to do until conditions turned, and the leverage and interconnection that made them powerful made the fallout spread. Leverage LTCM, 1998 Long-Term Capital Management, a hedge fund run by Nobel laureates, used huge leverage on bets that small price gaps would close, with derivatives of more than $1 trillion in notional value on under $5 billion of capital. When Russia defaulted and markets moved the wrong way, the magnified losses threatened its many trading partners. What happened: the New York Fed organised a roughly $3.6 billion rescue by 14 of its lenders to unwind the positions in an orderly way and prevent a chain reaction. Interconnection AIG, 2008 The insurer AIG had sold vast amounts of credit default swaps , contracts promising to pay out if certain mortgage bonds defaulted. About $79 billion of them insured complex bundles of mortgage debt. It collected premiums as if the payouts would never come, and set aside little against them. What happened: when housing collapsed, the claims did come. AIG couldn't cover them, and because so many banks relied on its guarantees, the US committed up to $182 billion of support. Opacity The 2008 crisis Mortgage risk was sliced, repackaged and insured through layers of derivatives few could value. On paper the risk looked spread out and safe; in reality it was concentrated and uncollateralised. What happened: when the underlying mortgages failed, the hidden losses surfaced all at once across interconnected firms, turning a housing downturn into a global financial crisis. Since 2008 The reforms after 2008 (Part 6) made the biggest banks safer, but smaller blow-ups have kept arriving, each through the same three doors. Leverage Oil below zero, 2020 A futures contract can require physical delivery . As COVID emptied the roads, storage at the delivery hub in Cushing, Oklahoma filled up, and traders holding May oil contracts with nowhere to put the oil had to pay others to take them. What happened: on 20 April 2020 the expiring US oil contract settled at minus $37.63 a barrel, the first negative price in its history. Opacity Archegos, 2021 A family office built giant stock bets through total return swaps with several banks. Because the banks held the shares, no one outside, and no single bank, saw how big the combined position was. What happened: when its stocks fell, the banks raced to sell. Losses topped $10 billion , about $5 billion of them at Credit Suisse, which was taken over two years later. Interconnection UK pensions, 2022 British pension funds used interest-rate swaps and borrowing in \"liability-driven\" strategies. When a surprise budget sent bond yields soaring, they faced sudden collateral calls and had to dump government bonds, pushing yields higher still. What happened: the Bank of England stepped in to buy long-dated gilts, about £19 billion over two weeks, after some funds came within hours of collapse. The pattern, not the products Note what's common across all of these: the derivative worked as intended in calm times and masked a build-up of risk. The problem each time was a large, leveraged, interconnected position whose true size and fragility were hard to see from outside, exactly the \"latent, potentially lethal\" danger Buffett described. Used to hedge a real exposure, these same contracts are routine and useful. The danger lives in scale, leverage and opacity, not in the idea of a derivative."},{"id":"s6","h":"After 2008: the clearing house","t":"The AIG rescue showed the core problem: when big firms trade derivatives directly with each other, everyone is exposed to everyone, and no one can see the whole web. So in September 2009 the G20 leaders agreed that standard OTC derivatives should be centrally cleared , traded on platforms where possible, and reported to regulators, with tougher capital rules for the rest. The US wrote it into the Dodd-Frank Act in 2010; Europe followed. A clearing house , or central counterparty, steps into the middle of every trade: it becomes the buyer to every seller and the seller to every buyer. Each member posts collateral up front and settles gains and losses with it every day, so losses can't quietly pile up the way they did at AIG. If a member fails, its collateral and a shared default fund absorb the loss before anyone else is hit. From a tangle to a hub Who owes whom, before and after central clearing Trading directly Through a clearing house Make one bank fail Schematic. Central clearing doesn't remove risk; it gathers it in one place, behind layers of collateral and capital. That makes the clearing houses themselves \"too important to fail,\" which is why regulators now treat them as critical infrastructure."},{"id":"s7","h":"Open interest","t":"One number tells you how big and how active a derivatives market has become: open interest . It's the total count of contracts that are currently open , meaning entered into and not yet closed out, settled or expired. Every open contract has a buyer on one side and a seller on the other, so it measures the stack of live obligations between market participants. Open interest isn't the same as volume. Volume counts how many contracts changed hands in a day, including ones opened and closed the same day; open interest counts how many remain outstanding at the end of it. A single contract can trade many times (adding to volume) without changing open interest, because it's just passing between hands. Open interest only moves when a new contract is created or an existing one is closed . Try it. Make some trades Watch which number moves Two traders open a new contract A holder sells to someone new Both sides close a contract End the day Each line is one open contract between a buyer (left) and a seller (right). Open interest vs volume Two numbers people often confuse Measure What it counts Volume How many contracts traded during the day, even if opened and closed within it. A measure of activity. Open interest How many contracts remain open at the close. A measure of outstanding obligations still on the books. Rises when Open interest: a buyer and seller create a brand-new contract. Volume: any trade at all occurs. Falls when Open interest: an existing contract is closed, settled or expires . Volume resets to zero each day. Why it matters: rising open interest means fresh money and new obligations are flowing into a market, so more risk is being carried between participants. That's the leverage-and-interconnection build-up Buffett was pointing at, made into a single trackable figure. For scale: exchanges worldwide traded about 119 billion futures and options contracts in 2025, while open interest at the year's end was about 1.5 billion . Source: FIA. So when you read that open interest in a contract is \"at a record,\" it means more live bets than ever are stacked up in that market, each one an obligation someone eventually has to honour. Whether that's healthy hedging or a fragile pile of speculation is the same question this whole piece turns on. Next time you meet a derivative, ask two things. QUESTION 1 Is this contract hedging a real risk, or creating a new one? QUESTION 2 How much leverage, interconnection and hidden size sits behind it?"},{"id":"sources","h":"Sources","t":"Where the figures and claims come from. Definitions of futures, options and swaps, hedging, margin and open interest: CME Group education and the CFTC glossary . OTC derivatives at end-June 2025: notional of about $846 trillion, gross market value of $21.8 trillion, gross credit exposure of about $3.0 trillion: Bank for International Settlements and ISDA . Exchange-traded volume of 119.3 billion contracts in 2025 and year-end open interest of about 1.5 billion: FIA . The \"financial weapons of mass destruction\" and \"time bombs\" quotes: Berkshire Hathaway's 2002 annual letter ; Berkshire's own index put options and roughly $4.9 billion of premiums: Berkshire Hathaway's 2008 annual letter . Long-Term Capital Management's derivatives of more than $1 trillion notional and the roughly $3.6 billion recapitalisation by 14 institutions, coordinated by the New York Fed: Federal Reserve History . AIG's credit default swaps, including about $79 billion on mortgage-linked CDOs, the role of derivatives in the 2008 crisis, and the up-to-$182 billion of government support: Financial Crisis Inquiry Commission report . The May 2020 WTI contract settling at −$37.63 on 20 April 2020: CFTC interim staff report . Archegos's total return swaps and bank losses above $10 billion, including Credit Suisse's: Fortune and the Bank of England PRA final notice to Credit Suisse . The September 2022 LDI crisis and the Bank of England's temporary gilt purchases: Bank of England Quarterly Bulletin and BIS Quarterly Review . The G20's 2009 commitment to central clearing and reporting of standard OTC derivatives: G20 Pittsburgh leaders' statement . Educational only. Not investment, legal or tax advice. Derivatives are complex and can lose more than the money put up; the historical episodes here are simplified summaries. Confirm any figure before relying on it."}]},{"id":"japan-bubble","href":"explainers/japan-bubble.html","title":"The Biggest Bubble in History: Japan's Lost Decades","short":"Japan's lost decades","topic":"Asset bubbles","category":"Economic history","date":"2026-11-26","dek":"The post-war miracle, the Plaza Accord, the land-and-share feedback loop, the burst, zombie loans and QE","sec":[{"id":"","h":"","t":"Explainer · Economic history · Japan The Biggest Bubble in History Picture 1989. Japan has rebuilt from the war, grown its economy ninefold in a generation, and given the world Mario. Thirty-two of the fifty largest companies on earth are Japanese, and the land under Tokyo's Imperial Palace is said to be worth more than all of California. Then the bubble bursts. Here is how Japan rose so fast, how a loop between land and shares inflated the biggest asset bubble in history, and why the fall turned into two lost decades. By Jun Wei Heung · Words and graphics November 26, 2026 · 14 min read Copy link Notes This is an economic-history explainer, condensed from a longer story. Figures are rounded and the causes are simplified: a bubble this large had many drivers, and economists still debate how much each one mattered and how well the later policy responses worked. Company names (Sony, Toyota and others) appear only as examples of the era, not as the subject. Nothing here is investment advice. Key findings Post-war Japan grew at roughly 10% a year from 1950 to 1973, rebuilding from near-total destruction into the world's second-largest economy . Human capital, exports and a fixed, cheap yen did much of the work. The 1985 Plaza Accord doubled the price of Japanese exports by strengthening the yen. To avoid a slump, the Bank of Japan cut rates to record lows, and the cheap money flooded into land and shares . A feedback loop inflated the bubble: rising land lifted share prices, which fattened banks' cross-held shares and capital, which let them lend still more against land and shares. Over half the profits of firms like Toyota and Nissan came from speculation , not their actual business. When the Bank of Japan finally raised rates, the Nikkei fell about 60% by 1992 and property followed. Zombie loans kept failing firms alive, a bank crisis hit in 1997, and growth averaged about 0.8% a year across the lost decades. Japan's response, including the first quantitative easing , became the playbook the US used in 2008."},{"id":"s1","h":"From ashes to miracle","t":"In 1945 Japan surrendered in ruins: about a quarter of its structures destroyed, most of its ships gone, its colonies seized, and the country under an American-led occupation headed by General MacArthur. The occupation rewrote the rules to make Japan peaceful and democratic: full demilitarisation, the emperor reduced to a figurehead, a new constitution renouncing war, and a new government. It also went after economic power. Believing the big industrial groups, the zaibatsu , had backed the war, the occupation passed an anti-monopoly act and forced their shares to be sold off. Workers won the right to unionise, and union membership jumped from about 3.2% in 1945 to 53% in 1948 . Under land reform, roughly 38% of farmland was bought by the government and resold cheaply to the people who farmed it. These reforms removed the old non-democratic powers, but they didn't fix runaway inflation and weak output, so food was still short. The Cold War changed America's aim: make Japan a capitalist success and a bulwark against communism. The 1949 Dodge Plan cut subsidies, balanced the budget and slowed the printing presses to kill inflation, and fixed the exchange rate at 360 yen to the dollar to push exports. Then war in Korea turned Japan into a supply base for US and UN forces, and demand for its manufactured goods surged. With few natural resources, Japan grew by investing, industrialising and exporting. In 1960 Prime Minister Hayato Ikeda set a goal to double national income in ten years , and hit it in seven. From 1950 to 1973 the economy grew around 10% a year , the post-war miracle. Education mattered: by 1960 Japan's secondary-school enrolment already beat the 1965 average of other industrial economies, so workers could move from low- to high-productivity work."},{"id":"s2","h":"The oil shock and the Plaza Accord","t":"The miracle ended in 1973 . Japan imported 99% of its energy , so when the oil crisis hit there was panic over blackouts and shortages. Soaring oil prices raised production costs and pushed inflation to 24.9% in 1974 . Japan eventually diversified its energy suppliers and built up reserves, but investors had seen its vulnerability and lowered their growth expectations. Having largely caught up to the West on technology, Japan's growth stepped down from about 10% to 5% after 1973. Even so, it was now the world's second-largest economy. Into the 1980s, Japanese firms, Sony, Honda, Nintendo, Canon and more, became global giants, and a property boom took hold. Land ownership carried deep meaning in a country not long out of feudalism and short on buildable, mountainous terrain. From 1956 to 1986 land prices rose about 5,000% , falling in only one year. Banks lent freely against land, trusting the myth that it only ever rose. American anxiety grew as Japanese products spread; in Detroit, autoworkers smashed Japanese cars in protest. In 1985 , US Treasury Secretary James Baker gathered the leading economies at New York's Plaza Hotel . They agreed to push the dollar down against other currencies, including the yen. Why the Plaza Accord mattered A stronger yen made every Japanese export nearly twice as expensive abroad, which threatened a recession at home. To prevent it, in 1986 the Bank of Japan held a low interest-rate policy , cutting the rate it charged commercial banks to a record 2.5% by February 1987 . Borrowing was cheap, and the new money didn't go into factories. It went into land and the stock market . Cheap money plus light regulation is how the asset bubble began."},{"id":"s3","h":"Cheap money and zaitech","t":"Through the post-war years the Bank of Japan used window guidance : it told banks directly how much to lend, and to which industries. In the mid-1980s it relaxed that guidance and urged banks to lend more. They did, reaching for higher-risk borrowers . At the same time, companies could pad profits by trading securities and stocks, often without paying capital-gains tax, a practice known as zaitech (\"financial engineering\"). Brokerages offered tokkin funds with guaranteed returns above interest rates, run through special speculative accounts. In 1985 about ¥9 trillion sat in these funds; four years later it was as much as ¥40 trillion (around $300 billion). The numbers ran away. From 1985 to 1989 real-estate prices roughly quadrupled ; from 1985 to 1987 asset prices doubled . As shares climbed, companies' speculative profits soared, and for big names like Toyota and Nissan, over half of profits now came from speculation rather than their actual business. Few noticed that ordinary operating profits had actually been falling."},{"id":"s4","h":"The loop that fed itself","t":"The property boom and the stock boom then fused into a single, self-reinforcing machine. Investors began valuing companies on their land holdings , treating owned property as \"hidden assets\" that made the shares look cheap. The more that happened, the more the whole thing spun. The land-and-share feedback loop Each step made the next one bigger A simplified loop. Banks held shares in each other and in the firms they lent to (\"cross-shareholding\"), so rising share prices inflated bank capital, which let them lend more, which pushed prices higher again. The circular process was the bubble's fatal flaw. This is the heart of the story: nothing in the loop needed new real wealth to keep going. Higher land lifted shares, higher shares lifted bank capital, more capital meant more lending, and more lending lifted land. Belief did the rest."},{"id":"s5","h":"The peak","t":"Even the global stock crash of October 1987 didn't break the spell; Japan recovered best, convincing many that the government would always hold prices up and shares simply could not fall. In April 1988 , the abolition of tax-exempt postal savings freed about ¥300 trillion (around $2.2 trillion) for new investment. By that year Japan's economy had grown over nine times its 1955 size, while the US had grown about 2.67 times. The war's humiliation felt answered, and economists worldwide praised Japan's performance. > California Land under Tokyo's Imperial Palace was estimated to be worth more than all the real estate in the state of California. Plans were floated for an underground city in Tokyo. $8.7B Spent by Japanese collectors on world-famous paintings by Van Gogh, Renoir and Monet, in one of the art market's hottest-ever buying sprees. ¥400M Price of a membership at Tokyo's exclusive Koganei Country Club (about $2.7 million). Total golf-membership value in Japan was put near $200 billion. Yet for young people, land prices had risen so far that an average lifetime's earnings could no longer buy even a small apartment in central Tokyo. Some families took out 100-year, multi-generational mortgages . Japanese companies, flush with a huge trade surplus, spent lavishly in America: in 1989 Sony bought Columbia Pictures and Mitsubishi took a majority stake in the Rockefeller Center , feeding American fears of a takeover. In December 1989 the Nikkei 225 peaked at 38,915 , and Nomura Securities predicted it would reach 80,000 by 1995 . But Japanese stocks had already risen three times faster than corporate earnings, even counting unsustainable speculative profits. Things were about to turn. The Nikkei 225, 1985–1998 The rise to 38,915, and the long way down Approximate year-end index levels; the 1989 point is the 29 December all-time peak of 38,915. Hover or tap a point to see what happened that year. The crash was gradual, not a single day. Source: Nikkei 225 historical levels."},{"id":"s6","h":"The burst","t":"Late in 1989 Yasushi Mieno became governor of the Bank of Japan, set on pricking the bubble. On Christmas Day he ordered a rate rise, and the Nikkei peaked days later. Unlike 1929, Japan's crash didn't happen overnight; the bubble deflated gradually . The Bank of Japan raised rates five times, to 6% by August 1990 . Tight money, fading confidence and existing over-borrowing ended both the stock and land bubbles. By 1992 the Nikkei was down about 60% from its peak, and within a few years Tokyo property had fallen about as far. The bubble had to pop, but people lost fortunes, stopped spending, and tipped Japan into recession. The government fought back with aggressive stimulus, more public-works spending and lower rates from the central bank, and recovery seemed to come. Then, in 1997 , a second shock arrived: a banking crisis."},{"id":"s7","h":"Zombie loans and lost decades","t":"After the bubble popped, many companies could no longer repay their bank loans. Rather than force the issue, banks gave big borrowers a break, cutting interest or forgiving part of the debt. These loans that were unlikely ever to be repaid became known as zombie loans , worth hundreds of billions of dollars. How a zombie loan kept a dead firm walking The quiet choice that prolonged the slump After the burst An unprofitable company can't repay its loans. In a normal market, it would shut down. The bank blinks Instead of forcing repayment, the bank cuts the rate or forgives part of the loan to avoid booking the loss. The zombie walks The firm survives but creates no growth, while the bad loan sits on the bank's books as a zombie loan , weakening the bank. Multiply across hundreds of billions of dollars of such loans and whole banks were put in jeopardy. Keeping dead firms alive also blocked healthier companies from taking their place. With zombie loans eating into their assets, some banks were in real danger, and in 1997 Hokkaido Takushoku became the first big bank to fail. The government had to step in to rescue others. Clearing all the zombie loans off bank balance sheets took six years to a decade , a painful process, but the crisis finally passed. Across the two lost decades, Japan's economy grew about 0.8% a year in real terms. Japan also fell behind the US in adopting information technology and investing in the digital industry, going from one of the fastest-growing countries to one of the slowest. Its rigid labour market made it hard to hire or fire; the term madogiwazoku (\"window-side tribe\") described employees kept on but sidelined, sitting by the window with no real work. With firms offering lifetime employment , young workers found fewer openings and less chance to innovate, creating a \"lost generation.\" Some withdrew entirely, becoming shut-ins who wouldn't leave home or join the workforce. Meanwhile the four Asian tigers, Hong Kong, Singapore, Taiwan and South Korea, had copied Japan's early playbook and were now strong competitors."},{"id":"s8","h":"Deflation, QE and the lessons","t":"By 1998 people and firms were spending so little that prices began to fall , and Japan entered a deflationary spiral . The usual cure, print money and cut rates to make cash cheap, had already been running for years; rates were near 0% by 1998 , yet spending stayed weak and prices kept dropping. Japan was in a liquidity trap , where normal monetary policy had stopped working. So the Bank of Japan tried new tools. With forward guidance , uncommon for central banks then, it promised to keep rates at 0% until the economy improved. And Japan became the first country to use quantitative easing . What quantitative easing does In QE, the central bank buys bonds and other assets from banks, paying cash. The banks then have fresh cash to lend out into the economy, which is meant to lift spending and prices. In Japan, QE's effect on deflation and growth was seen as modest , and its usefulness is still debated. The lessons travelled. When the US recession hit in 2007–08 , policymakers studied Japan and moved fast: a $700 billion bank bailout , rates cut to zero, and, when that wasn't enough, forward guidance and quantitative easing . Without Japan's painful example, the crisis might have been far worse. A bubble is hard to spot until it bursts, and Japan's shows how devastating one can be. Today Japan faces a different, supply-side problem: a rapidly ageing and shrinking population that strains its economy, and an open question of whether a country can keep growing as its population falls."},{"id":"s9","h":"The yen carry trade","t":"Those decades of near-zero rates left a lasting side effect: the yen became the cheapest money in the world to borrow . That gave rise to the yen carry trade , a bet that runs on the gap between Japan's rock-bottom rates and higher rates abroad, and it now links Japan's quiet monetary policy to markets everywhere. The move is simple. Borrow yen almost for free , convert it to dollars, and buy something that pays more, usually US Treasury bonds or other dollar assets. Pocket the difference in interest, and any gain if the yen falls too. For years it looked like free money. The carry trade, and its unwind Cheap yen in, dollar assets out, until the trade reverses Step 1 · borrow cheap Investors borrow yen at Japan's near-zero rate. Step 2 · buy dollars They sell the yen for dollars and buy higher-yielding US bonds, earning the rate gap. Step 3 · the yen weakens All that yen-selling floods the market with yen , pushing its value down. A weak yen then makes Japan's imported energy and food more expensive, imported inflation for a country that buys most of both from abroad. Step 4 · the unwind When Japan raises rates (or the gap narrows), the trade reverses: investors sell overseas assets to buy yen back and repay their loans. The rush sends the yen up, crashes the Nikkei , and wipes out savings caught in the trade. The trade is self-feeding on the way up and self-feeding on the way down, like the bubble's loop in miniature. In August 2024 , a tiny Bank of Japan hike (to about 0.25%) helped trigger an unwind that sent the Nikkei down roughly 20% over a few days , including about 12% on 5 August , its largest one-day point fall ever, before markets recovered. Source: reporting on the 2024 carry-trade unwind. It's the same lesson in a new form. Cheap money that looks free builds up a hidden pile of leverage, and the reckoning comes fast when the rate that made it cheap finally moves. Japan spent the lost decades at near-zero rates to escape deflation; the carry trade is the bill that arrangement can present, not just to Japanese investors but to markets far beyond. A bubble needs no new wealth to keep inflating, only belief. The hard part isn't the pop. It's everything that comes after. So when a market looks unstoppable, ask two things. QUESTION 1 Is the price rise feeding on itself, or on real earnings? QUESTION 2 Once it bursts, what makes the recovery so slow?"},{"id":"sources","h":"Sources","t":"Where the figures and claims come from. This explainer is based on the video \"Japan's Lost Decades\" (YouTube), which supplied the narrative and figures below. The sections that follow note where specific numbers originate. Post-war occupation reforms (demilitarisation, the new constitution, the anti-monopoly act and zaibatsu dissolution, labour unions and land reform): US State Department, Office of the Historian, \"Occupation and Reconstruction of Japan, 1945–52\" . The 3.2%→53% union-membership rise and the ~38% land-reform figure are as given in the source video. The 1949 Dodge Line and the fixed 360-yen rate; the Income Doubling Plan that Hayato Ikeda achieved in about seven years; and ~10% average growth 1950–1973: the Japanese economic miracle . Korean War demand and secondary-school enrolment are as given in the source video. The 1973 oil crisis and Japan's energy vulnerability, and the 1974 contraction and inflation spike: JBIC , and T. Ito, \"Great Inflation and Central Bank Independence in Japan\" (NBER) , which puts 1974 CPI inflation near 30%; the video's figure of 24.9% is within the range reported by different price measures. The 99% energy-import and ~10%→5% growth figures are as given in the source video. The 1985 Plaza Accord and the stronger yen: J. Frankel, \"The Plaza Accord, 30 Years Later\" (NBER) . Land prices rising ~5,000% from 1956 to 1986 and the Bank of Japan's cut to a 2.5% discount rate by February 1987 are as given in the source video. Zaitech (\"financial engineering\") and companies such as Toyota drawing large shares of profit from financial dealings in the mid-1980s: Euromoney, \"Japan: an embarrassment of riches\" . Window guidance, the tokkin fund totals (¥9 trillion to ~¥40 trillion), and the asset- and real-estate-price multiples are as given in the source video. The asset bubble as a credit-fuelled over-valuation of land and shares driven by loose policy and aggressive lending, and the cross-shareholding dynamic: International Monetary Fund, \"The Japanese Asset Price Bubble\" . The 1987 crash recovery, the April 1988 postal-savings change, the nine-fold growth since 1955, the Imperial Palace land, the art spree, golf memberships, 100-year mortgages, and the Sony–Columbia and Mitsubishi–Rockefeller Center purchases: as given in the source video; the Imperial-Palace-vs-California comparison is a widely repeated illustration of the era (see e.g. HistoryRise ). The Nikkei 225's all-time high of 38,915.87 on 29 December 1989: Nippon.com and The Wall Street Journal . Nomura's 80,000 prediction, the ~60% fall by 1992, the gradual deflation under Governor Yasushi Mieno, and rates reaching 6% by August 1990 are as given in the source video. Zombie loans and the banks' forbearance that kept insolvent firms alive: R. Caballero, T. Hoshi and A. Kashyap, \"Zombie Lending and Depressed Restructuring in Japan\" (NBER) . The 1997 failure of Hokkaido Takushoku Bank ; the Bank of Japan's response to the crisis: Bank for International Settlements, \"The financial crisis in Japan during the 1990s\" (BIS Papers No 6) . The ~0.8% average growth, madogiwazoku, lifetime employment, shut-ins and the four Asian tigers are as given in the source video. The liquidity trap and the Bank of Japan's zero-interest-rate policy (from 1999) and quantitative easing (from March 2001), with debated results: Federal Reserve Bank of San Francisco, \"Quantitative Easing by the Bank of Japan\" , and the Bank of Japan's own survey of the policy's effects . The deflationary spiral, the framing of Japan as the first user of QE, and the US borrowing the playbook in 2007–08 (the $700 billion bailout, zero rates, forward guidance and QE) are as given in the source video. The yen carry trade (borrowing cheap yen to buy higher-yielding dollar assets) and its August 2024 unwind, including the Bank of Japan's hike to about 0.25% and the Nikkei's fall of roughly 20% over a few days (about 12% on 5 August, a record one-day point drop): Foreign Policy and contemporary reporting on the 2024 carry-trade unwind. Educational only. Figures are rounded and the history is simplified; confirm specifics before relying on them. Nothing here is investment advice."}]},{"id":"seigniorage","href":"explainers/seigniorage.html","title":"The Lord's Cut: How Seigniorage Lets Money-Makers Profit","short":"The profit in making money","topic":"Seigniorage","category":"Market infrastructure","date":"2026-11-23","dek":"The profit from issuing money, from coin debasement to central banks, bank deposits, stablecoins and CBDCs","sec":[{"id":"","h":"","t":"Explainer · Market infrastructure · Seigniorage The Lord's Cut Make a dollar for a few cents, then spend it at face value, and you keep the difference. That gap is seigniorage , the oldest profit in finance. Medieval lords took it by minting coins, and took more by quietly shaving the silver out of them. Today a government, a bank and a stablecoin issuer each take their own version of it. Here is how the same trick has run from the mint to the blockchain. By Jun Wei Heung · Words and graphics November 23, 2026 · 13 min read Copy link Sources This explains a single idea, seigniorage, and traces it across very different kinds of money. Historical silver-content figures are approximate and vary by source and mint. Modern figures (printing costs, central-bank remittances, stablecoin profits) change year to year; the numbers here show the mechanism, not a current quote. Company names appear as examples. Nothing here is investment advice. Key findings Seigniorage is the profit an issuer makes from putting money into circulation: the face value of the money minus the cost of creating it. The word comes from the Old French for a feudal lord's right to mint coins. Early mints charged a fee to coin your metal. Rulers took a bigger, hidden cut by debasement , lowering the precious-metal content while keeping the face value. The Roman denarius fell from about 98% silver to roughly 2% over three centuries. Small coins now run the opposite way : a US penny costs more to make than it's worth, a loss called reverse seigniorage. Paper and digital money make the gap enormous: a $100 note costs about nine cents to print, so almost the whole $100 is seigniorage. A modern central bank collects it as interest on the bonds it buys with new money and hands the profit to the treasury. Because about half of all US dollar notes are held abroad, much of that profit comes from foreigners. The same logic now runs through bank deposits , stablecoins (take a dollar, hold Treasuries, keep the yield) and proposed central-bank digital currencies . In every case the issuer profits from money it created cheaply, and over-issuing it causes inflation , which is the natural limit. Zimbabwe found that limit in 2008, when prices rose by about 80 billion percent in a single month."},{"id":"s1","h":"What seigniorage is","t":"Here is the whole idea in one line. If you can create money for less than it's worth and spend it at full value, you pocket the difference. That difference is seigniorage . A $100 bill costs about nine cents to print, so on paper its issuer is $99.91 ahead the moment the note goes out into the economy. (Part 4 shows how a modern central bank actually collects that gap.) The name is medieval. It comes from the Old French seigneuriage , the right of the seigneur , the lord, to mint money, and it has meant \"the profit from issuing money\" ever since. The thing being issued has changed many times, from silver coins to paper notes to numbers in a database, but the profit has the same shape every time: face value minus cost to create . The industry that always does well Sector outlooks, with one exception Illustration by Jun Wei Heung, after a widely shared economics cartoon. Generic figure, not a real person. Every other sector's outlook slides; the money-printing line runs off the top, because its product costs almost nothing to make and sells at full face value. That's the joke, and the whole of seigniorage."},{"id":"s2","h":"The mint and the shave","t":"For most of history money was the metal. A silver coin was worth roughly the silver in it. So where was the profit? Two places. First, an honest fee. If you brought raw silver to the ruler's mint to be struck into coins, the mint kept a small cut for the service. That fee was the original seigniorage, modest and openly charged. Second, the shave. A ruler who controlled the mint could debase the coinage: issue a coin that still said \"one denarius\" but contained less silver than before, melt in cheap base metal, and spend the extra coins at full value. It's seigniorage by stealth, and rulers short of money reached for it again and again. Rome did it for centuries. The incredible shrinking denarius Silver content of Rome's main coin, Augustus to the late third century Approximate fineness; exact figures vary by source and mint. Each emperor who lowered the silver content spent the saved metal as new coins, taking the difference as seigniorage. Debase too far and people notice: prices rose and the good old coins vanished from circulation, hoarded away. Sources: numismatic histories of the denarius. Good money, bad money Two coins with the same face value, but only one keeps its silver When a debased coin and a full-silver one both count as \"one denarius,\" people spend the bad one and keep the good one. The pure coins disappear into hoards and only the watered-down ones circulate. That tendency, later summed up as \"bad money drives out good,\" is Gresham's law , named after Thomas Gresham, an adviser to Elizabeth I. It's the recurring cost of pushing seigniorage too hard."},{"id":"s3","h":"When it runs in reverse","t":"Seigniorage isn't always a profit. When the cost of making a coin meets or beats its face value, the issuer earns little, nothing, or even takes a loss. That's negative, or reverse, seigniorage , and it shows up in two very different places: at the honest top of the market, and at the humble bottom of it. At the top sit bullion coins , the opposite of a debased one. A coin like the American Gold Eagle carries a $50 face value but contains a full ounce of gold worth thousands of dollars. The face value is almost symbolic; the coin is worth its metal. Governments don't issue these to pocket a gap, because there isn't one. The mint sells them at the gold price plus a small premium that just covers striking and handling, so the buyer pays for the metal and the making. The whole point is that the metal is real, which is also why there's no seigniorage profit to take. At the bottom sit the smallest everyday coins , where the metal and manufacturing now cost more than the coin is worth . Here the issuer loses money on every piece it strikes. It's the mirror image of Roman debasement: instead of the coin being worth less than its stamp, the coin costs more than its stamp. Face value against the cost of making it Which side is heavier decides who wins Costs are for fiscal 2024 (coins) and the 2025 print order (the $100 note), and include making and distributing. The scales tip by direction, not exact weight: a $100 note is worth over a thousand times its cost. Sources: US Mint via the Richmond Fed; Federal Reserve. Two ways seigniorage turns negative When the metal, or the making, is worth as much as the coin Coin Face value What it's really worth / costs Seigniorage American Gold Eagle (1 oz) $50 Roughly the gold price, several thousand dollars; sold at metal value plus a mint premium None by design: the buyer pays for the metal US penny 1¢ About 3.7¢ to make and distribute (2024) Negative: a loss on every coin US nickel 5¢ About 13.8¢ to make and distribute (2024) Negative: a loss of nearly 9¢ each Figures approximate and from fiscal 2024 for the US coins. In 2024 the US Mint lost about $85 million on pennies alone, and the last circulating penny was struck on 12 November 2025. Treasury expects to save about $56 million a year. Sources: US Mint 2024 Annual Report (via the Federal Reserve) and bullion-coin pricing. Why the humble coin lost its profit A penny that cost a fraction of a cent to make a century ago now costs several cents, because metal and manufacturing got more expensive while the face value stayed frozen at one cent. The same pressure explains the whole arc of this story: as coins became loss-makers, seigniorage migrated to where production is almost free, first to paper notes , then to digital money . The profit didn't disappear; it moved to a cheaper medium."},{"id":"s4","h":"Paper money","t":"Paper breaks the link between a coin and its metal, and that's exactly what makes the profit explode. A banknote is worth its face value by law and habit, not by the paper. So the cost of creating it falls to almost nothing while the face value stays whole. In the United States, the Treasury's Bureau of Engraving and Printing makes the notes and the Federal Reserve issues them. Printing a $100 note costs only around nine cents ; the other $99.91 is seigniorage. But a central bank doesn't simply spend new money. It buys assets with it, mostly government bonds, and those assets pay interest. That interest, minus the Fed's running costs, is the modern form of seigniorage. So there are two ways to count it. The accounting way is the one in Part 1: face value minus printing cost, nearly $100 per $100 note. The economic way is the interest earned on what the note bought: hold a $100 note in your wallet for a year at 4% interest rates and the Fed collects about $4 on the bond behind it, every year the note stays out. Over many years the two meet, but the second is how the money actually arrives. Cash is, in effect, an interest-free loan from everyone who holds it to the central bank. And many holders aren't American. Federal Reserve staff estimate that about 45% of all US banknotes , and around two-thirds of $100 notes , are held abroad, as savings and for trade in countries where the local currency is less trusted. That's roughly $950 billion of interest-free lending from the rest of the world, a quiet part of what is sometimes called the dollar's \"exorbitant privilege.\" Where the profit goes The Federal Reserve isn't allowed to keep its profit. After expenses, it remits the surplus to the US Treasury , so the seigniorage ends up funding the government. In 2021 that came to about $109 billion , and in 2022 about $76 billion . Then the payments stopped. The reason is the other side of the Fed's balance sheet. Since 2008 it has bought far more bonds than there is cash to back them, paying for the rest with bank reserves , which the Fed pays interest on. When rates were near zero that cost nothing. When rates jumped in 2022, the interest the Fed paid on reserves overtook what its older, low-yielding bonds earned. Try it below. When the money-maker runs at a loss The Fed's yearly result at different interest rates, roughly 2023's balance sheet Interest rate the Fed pays on reserves: 0.1% Illustrative model, rounded: about $7.7 trillion of assets yielding about 2.1%, $2.3 trillion of cash that costs the Fed no interest, and about $5.3 trillion of reserves and similar deposits that do, plus about $9 billion of running costs. In 2023, with rates around 5.3%, the Fed actually lost about $114 billion. Losses are recorded as a deferred asset , an IOU against future profits, which reached about $243 billion by the end of 2025; payments to the Treasury resume only once it's paid down. Sources: Federal Reserve; New York Fed. Notice what never loses money: the cash. Notes cost the Fed no interest, so the bonds behind them always earn. The losses come from the reserves, which are borrowed money with a price. That's the line between seigniorage and ordinary banking, drawn in the Fed's own accounts."},{"id":"s5","h":"Bank money","t":"Most money today isn't cash at all. It's the balance in your bank account, created when banks make loans. Commercial banks don't print notes, so their version of the profit is different, but it rhymes with seigniorage. When a bank lends, it credits the borrower's account with new deposit money, backed by the loan it now holds as an asset. The bank's profit is the interest it charges on that lending , over what it pays to fund itself. It is earning a return on money that largely came into being with a keystroke. Economists debate whether to call bank profit true seigniorage, but the family resemblance is clear: create the medium cheaply, earn on it at full value."},{"id":"s6","h":"Stablecoins","t":"The newest issuers look surprisingly like the oldest. A stablecoin such as USDT promises to be worth one dollar (see our explainer on stablecoins ). Here is how the issuer profits from making it. How a stablecoin issuer earns seigniorage Take a real dollar, hold a Treasury, keep the interest Step 1 · mint You send the issuer $1 . It creates 1 token and gives it to you. Creating the token costs almost nothing. Step 2 · invest The issuer takes your dollar and buys a US Treasury bill that pays interest. Step 3 · keep the yield You hold a token worth $1; the issuer holds a bond earning yield and keeps the interest . Multiply by billions of tokens and the profit is large. This is seigniorage in all but name: the token circulates at face value, costs almost nothing to issue, and the backing it was created against earns a return for the issuer. In 2024 the USDT issuer Tether reported roughly $13 billion in profit, against well over $100 billion of US Treasuries. Source: Tether attestations and reporting. The catch, as with any issuer, is the promise to redeem. The dollar you handed over is now tied up in a bond; if too many holders ask for their dollars back at once and the issuer can't sell the backing fast enough, the peg can break. That risk is the price of the profit. Notice who doesn't get the interest: you. In the US that is now the law. The GENIUS Act , signed in July 2025, bars stablecoin issuers from paying interest or yield to holders, so the issuer keeps the whole return on the backing, just as a central bank pays nothing on cash. The fight has moved to the middlemen. Circle, which issues USDC, shares much of its interest income with partners that distribute the coin; it paid Coinbase about $908 million in 2024, and Coinbase passes some of that to its users as \"rewards.\" Banks argue that is interest by another name. Who captures the seigniorage, the issuer, the exchange or the user, is now a live legal question."},{"id":"s7","h":"Digital central-bank money","t":"Several central banks are exploring a central-bank digital currency (CBDC): official money issued as digital tokens rather than paper. For seigniorage, a CBDC works like physical cash, with one difference. There's no paper or ink, so the cost of issuing falls to almost zero . The same face-value-minus-cost profit stays with the central bank, and the same assets sit behind it. The bigger effect is who earns it. If people moved savings out of bank deposits and into central-bank digital money, the profit that banks now make on deposits (Part 5) would move to the central bank, and banks would have less cheap funding to lend. That worry, along with privacy and control, is why many proposals cap how much CBDC one person can hold. The US has gone the other way: in January 2025 an executive order barred federal agencies from working to create one. Either way, it's a clean illustration of the trend across this whole story: as money grows more abstract, the cost of making it keeps falling toward zero , and the profit in issuing it keeps growing. What the issuer keeps Profit as a share of each unit's face value, by kind of money Kept by the issuer Real cost / backing Illustrative. As money moves from metal to paper to digits, the cost of creating a unit collapses and the issuer's share climbs toward the whole face value. The stablecoin keeps almost all of it to mint, then also earns the yield on the dollar of backing."},{"id":"s8","h":"The same cut, and its limit","t":"Line the eras up and the pattern is one idea wearing different clothes. One profit, many forms Who issues, what it costs, where the profit comes from (or goes) Form of money Issuer Where the seigniorage comes from Silver coin The ruler's mint A small fee to strike your metal into coins. Debased coin The ruler's mint Less silver per coin, same face value; the saved metal is spent as extra coins. Bullion coin Modern mint None by design: sold at metal value plus a premium, so the metal is fully paid for. Small coin (penny) Modern mint Negative: metal and making cost more than the face value, so the mint loses money. Paper note Central bank / treasury Face value far above the cent-level printing cost; interest on the bonds bought with new money, remitted to the treasury. Bank deposit Commercial bank Interest charged on lending that created the deposit money. Stablecoin Private issuer Near-zero cost to mint; yield on the Treasuries held as backing. Same idea, different media. The profit is largest where the money costs almost nothing to make (notes, digital tokens) and vanishes or turns negative where the metal is real (bullion) or the coin is cheap but costly to strike (the penny). There is a hard limit, and it's the same one the Roman emperors hit. Seigniorage is only profitable while the money holds its value. Create too much of it and you flood the economy with claims on the same goods, and prices rise: that's inflation . A debased coin and an over-printed currency fail the same way, by making each unit worth less than before. Economists call this the inflation tax : the issuer gains, and everyone holding the money loses a slice of its value, without a vote or a bill. Push it to an extreme and you get hyperinflation. Zimbabwe printed money to pay its bills until, in November 2008, prices were rising by an estimated 79.6 billion percent a month , doubling roughly every day; the government issued a 100-trillion-dollar note, and soon after people abandoned the currency for US dollars. At that point the seigniorage is gone too, because nobody will hold the money long enough to be taxed by it. Seigniorage is the quiet profit in being the one who makes the money. Its only enemy is making too much of it. So whenever something new claims to be money, ask two things. QUESTION 1 Where exactly does the issuer's profit come from? QUESTION 2 What stops the issuer from just making unlimited money?"},{"id":"sources","h":"Sources","t":"Where the figures and claims come from. Definition and etymology of seigniorage (the Old French seigneuriage , a lord's right to mint) and how central banks earn it on their assets: European Central Bank , Banque de France , Econlib , and Alain Ize, \"Spending Seigniorage\" (IMF Working Paper) . Debasement of the Roman denarius, from about 98% silver under Augustus to roughly 2% by the late third century (approximate, varying by source): CoinWeek / NGC and World History Encyclopedia . Gresham's law (\"bad money drives out good\") and Thomas Gresham: Britannica and the Economic History Association . Negative (reverse) seigniorage on US coins: the penny costing about 3.69 cents and the nickel about 13.78 cents to produce in fiscal 2024, and the ~$85 million penny loss (US Mint 2024 Annual Report): Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond, \"Rounding Up: The Impact of Phasing Out the Penny\" . The final circulating penny, struck 12 November 2025: US Mint ; the ~$56 million yearly saving: AP via PBS . Bullion coins carrying a symbolic face value and sold at metal value plus a mint premium (the one-ounce American Gold Eagle's $50 face value against its gold content worth thousands): Bullion Standard and FindBullionPrices . Cost to print a $100 note (about nine cents) and US currency production: Federal Reserve . US currency held abroad (about 45% of notes and two-thirds of $100 notes, roughly $950 billion, at the start of 2021): Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis , citing Federal Reserve Board estimates. Fed remittances to the Treasury of $109.0 billion in 2021 and $76.0 billion in 2022, and the suspension of weekly remittances from September 2022: Federal Reserve 2021 financial statements and Federal Reserve press release, January 2023 ; the 2023 loss of about $114 billion: Federal Reserve press release, January 2024 ; the deferred asset of about $243.5 billion at the end of 2025: New York Fed, Open Market Operations 2025 ; how the deferred asset works: St. Louis Fed . The interactive chart is a simplified model built from those figures. Stablecoin issuers taking in dollars, holding US Treasuries and keeping the yield; Tether's roughly $13 billion 2024 profit and $100 billion-plus Treasury holdings: Investopedia and Spark . The GENIUS Act's ban on issuers paying interest or yield to holders: Congress.gov, S. 1582 ; Circle's $907.9 million of 2024 payments to Coinbase and the debate over exchange \"rewards\": Columbia Law School Blue Sky Blog . How a CBDC could pull deposits from banks, and the case for holding limits: Federal Reserve, \"Money and Payments\" (2022) . Seigniorage, the inflation tax and the risk that over-issuing money causes inflation: Federal Reserve Bank of Dallas ; Zimbabwe's 79.6 billion percent monthly inflation in November 2008: Steve Hanke and Alex Kwok, as summarised in Hanke and Bostrom (Johns Hopkins) . The framing of physical cash, bank money, stablecoins and CBDCs as forms of the same profit follows the source notes this explainer was built from. Educational only. Historical figures are approximate; modern figures change over time. Nothing here is investment, legal or tax advice."}]},{"id":"asian-crisis","href":"explainers/asian-crisis.html","title":"The Domino Year: How the 1997 Asian Financial Crisis Spread","short":"The 1997 Asian crisis","topic":"Financial crises","category":"Economic history","date":"2026-11-23","dek":"The baht's collapse, the contagion across Asia, the record IMF bailouts and their conditions, and Malaysia's different path","sec":[{"id":"","h":"","t":"Explainer · Economic history · Asia The Domino Year In July 1997 Thailand ran out of dollars defending its currency and let the baht float. It fell, and the panic jumped from one Asian economy to the next: Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines, then South Korea, in a matter of months. The IMF arrived with the largest rescues in its history and conditions that still divide economists. Here is how the dominoes fell, what the bailouts asked for, and why one country refused the deal. By Jun Wei Heung · Words and graphics November 23, 2026 · 15 min read Copy link Sources This is a plain-English history of a complex, multi-country crisis, with rounded figures and simplified causes; economists still disagree about what mattered most and whether the IMF's response helped or hurt. The map is a stylised sequence of the contagion, not a day-by-day record, and the currency falls are rounded, measured from July 1997 to January 1998. Nothing here is investment advice. Key findings Several Asian economies had boomed on foreign money, much of it short-term dollar loans , while pegging their currencies to the US dollar. That left them exposed if the dollars ever left. In July 1997 Thailand ran down its reserves defending the baht, then let it float . It crashed, and firms that had borrowed in dollars were suddenly crushed by their debts. The panic spread by contagion : investors saw the same weaknesses next door and pulled money out. Within months Malaysia, the Philippines, Indonesia (rupiah down about 80%) and then South Korea were hit, Korea taking the largest IMF rescue ever at the time (~$58B). The IMF's bailouts came with austerity, high interest rates, and bank and governance reform. Supporters say they restored confidence; critics say the tight policies deepened the slump . Malaysia refused the IMF and used capital controls instead."},{"id":"s1","h":"The setup","t":"Through the early 1990s, Southeast and East Asia were the world's growth story. Thailand, Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines and South Korea pulled in huge amounts of foreign capital chasing high returns. Much of it arrived as short-term dollar loans : cheap to borrow, but repayable fast and in a currency these countries didn't print. To make the borrowing feel safe, several governments pegged their currencies to the US dollar , holding, say, the baht at a roughly fixed rate. A stable peg told foreign lenders they wouldn't lose money on exchange-rate swings. Companies borrowed dollars happily and poured the money into property and expansion. The weakness was hidden in plain sight: if confidence ever cracked and everyone tried to swap local currency back into dollars at once, the peg would need more dollars than the central bank had."},{"id":"s2","h":"Thailand breaks first","t":"Thailand was the first domino. Its exports had slowed, its property boom was wobbling, and traders began to doubt the baht's peg. In the first half of 1997, speculators attacked the baht , selling it hard and betting Thailand couldn't hold the line. To defend the peg, Thailand's central bank had to buy its own currency with dollars , spending down its foreign reserves and raising interest rates. For a while it worked. But the reserves ran low, and on 2 July 1997 Thailand gave up and let the baht float . It immediately sank. The reserves were also smaller than they looked. On paper the Bank of Thailand still had about $31 billion in June 1997. But it had quietly promised to hand over $23.4 billion to other banks in the coming year through forward contracts, deals it had used to defend the baht without the reserve figure visibly falling. When it finally disclosed them in August, it was clear how little had really been left to fight with. Why a falling currency was a disaster Thai companies borrowed in dollars but earned in baht . The loan never changed, but once the baht fell, each dollar of it cost twice as many baht to repay, while sales in baht stayed the same. Loans that were manageable on Friday were crushing on Monday. That's how a currency move bankrupted firms, and the banks that had lent to them."},{"id":"s3","h":"The contagion","t":"What turned one country's problem into a regional crisis was contagion : once the baht broke, investors looked at Thailand's neighbours and saw the same setup, short-term dollar debt behind a shaky peg, and rushed to pull their money out before those currencies fell too. The selling became self-fulfilling. Watch it spread. How the panic spread, 1997 From Thailand outward, month by month ▶ Play Early 1997 Newest to be hit In crisis Held up Not (yet) hit A simplified sequence of the 1997 contagion, not a tick-by-tick record. Badges show each currency's fall against the dollar from July 1997 to January 1998 (Goldman Sachs). The last step marks the economies that held up (see Part 9). Press play, or drag the slider. Map geometry: Natural Earth via world-atlas."},{"id":"s4","h":"Five economies, one panic","t":"The same shock hit each country differently, depending on how much dollar debt it carried and how the government reacted. Start with the currencies themselves. How far the currencies fell Value lost against the US dollar, July 1997 to January 1998 Rounded. The won is measured to its late-December 1997 low. A 56% fall means each baht bought less than half as many dollars as before, so a dollar debt cost more than twice as many baht. Source: Goldman Sachs, \"1997 Asia financial crisis\"; Hong Kong Monetary Authority for the Hong Kong dollar. Where it landed hardest The same shock, five different outcomes Country What happened Thailand The epicentre. Burned its reserves defending the baht, then floated it on 2 July 1997. The currency and property crashed; banks failed. First to take an IMF rescue. Indonesia Hit hardest. The rupiah lost about 80% of its value, ruining dollar-indebted firms. Prices and unemployment soared, and the turmoil helped bring down the Suharto government in 1998. South Korea A large, advanced economy few expected to fall. Its big industrial groups and banks couldn't roll over short-term foreign debt; the won crashed and Korea took the biggest IMF package in history to that point. Philippines Let the peso float on 11 July 1997, nine days after Thailand; it fell about 10% that day and roughly 40% by early 1998. It was already under an IMF programme and leaned on it again, but sounder banks spared it the worst. Malaysia The ringgit and stock market tumbled. But Malaysia refused the IMF and in 1998 imposed capital controls and pegged the ringgit instead, a path that drew fierce criticism at the time. Simplified summaries. Philippine figures: BusinessWorld and the IMF. Other sources in the notes."},{"id":"s5","h":"The IMF bailout","t":"As currencies and banks collapsed, Thailand, Indonesia and South Korea turned to the International Monetary Fund , the lender of last resort for countries that run out of foreign money. The IMF put together emergency loans on a scale never seen before: around $17 billion for Thailand (August 1997), $23 billion for Indonesia (November 1997) and a record ~$58 billion for South Korea (December 1997). These were packages, not just IMF money: the Fund's own share was between about a quarter and a half, with the World Bank, the Asian Development Bank and other governments adding the rest. The totals aren't quite like for like. Korea's ~$58 billion counts the backup money other governments pledged. Indonesia's $23 billion is only its \"first line\"; the US, Japan, Singapore and others pledged a \"second line of defence\" on top (the US alone offered $3 billion), which isn't in that figure. The money came with strings, the IMF's conditionality . In exchange for the loans, governments had to adopt a standard package of reforms meant to restore confidence and fix the weaknesses that caused the crisis. What the IMF asked for The conditions attached to the loans, and the aim behind each Condition Why the IMF wanted it Austerity & tight budgets Cut spending and cancel projects to steady the currency and rebuild lenders' confidence. High interest rates Make holding the local currency attractive again, to stop the fall, even though it squeezed the economy. Fix the banks Close or merge insolvent banks and set up agencies to clear out bad loans, so the financial system could function. Governance & \"crony\" reform Break the cosy ties between banks, big firms and government that had funnelled credit to weak borrowers. Open up to foreign capital Liberalise the capital account and let foreigners buy in, to attract investment back. The conditions followed the IMF's standard template for a currency-and-debt crisis. How well they fit economies whose fundamentals had looked sound is exactly what the argument below is about."},{"id":"s6","h":"What the conditions did","t":"Here the story splits. The IMF's defenders argue the rescues stopped the free-fall : once credible money and reform were in place, currencies stabilised and capital slowly returned. The economies that took the deal did recover, some within a couple of years. Critics argue the medicine made the patient sicker. Austerity and sky-high interest rates hit economies that were already collapsing, turning a currency crisis into a deep recession: firms that might have survived went under, unemployment jumped, and in Indonesia the hardship spilled into riots and the fall of a government . Many economists later agreed the IMF applied a template built for over-spending governments to a crisis that was really about private short-term debt and panic , and that the tight policies were, at least at first, the wrong tool. Everyone agreed the fire had to be put out. The fight was over whether the IMF used water or petrol."},{"id":"s7","h":"Did the bailout work?","t":"The same template met four different economies and produced four different results, and the gap between Indonesia and South Korea shows that what mattered was often not the conditions on paper but how, and whether, they were carried out . Korea owned its reforms and executed them fast; Indonesia's programme stalled on politics and reversals. The clearest way to see it is country by country: what each received, what it was told to do, how it actually carried that out, how deep the 1998 recession ran, and the verdict with hindsight. Pick a country. The bailout, country by country What each got, what happened, and the verdict Thailand Indonesia South Korea Malaysia 1998 real-GDP falls from an IMF Working Paper (1999): Indonesia &minus;13.7%, Thailand &minus;9.4%, Malaysia &minus;6.7%, Korea &minus;5.8%. \"Verdict\" is the broad judgement of later reviews, including the IMF's own Independent Evaluation Office, not a precise score. Sources in the notes."},{"id":"s8","h":"Malaysia's different path","t":"One country said no. Malaysia refused the IMF and its conditions. Instead of raising rates and opening up, Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad's government did the opposite. On 1 September 1998 it imposed sweeping capital controls : money invested in Malaysia had to stay at least a year, and the offshore ringgit market, where speculators had been borrowing ringgit to bet against it, was shut down. The next day it fixed the ringgit at 3.80 to the dollar . With money unable to rush out, Malaysia could cut interest rates and spend to support the economy, the opposite of the IMF's recipe. The peg held for almost seven years, until July 2005 , when Malaysia moved to a managed float the same day China revalued the yuan. At the time, this was treated as heresy, and many warned it would scare off investors for good. In the event, Malaysia's downturn was real but its recovery held up, and its controls are now seen, at least by some, as a defensible way to buy time and stop a panic, rather than the disaster they were billed as. It's the clearest natural experiment the crisis offered: the same shock, a very different response."},{"id":"s9","h":"Who escaped, and why","t":"Not everyone fell. Several of the region's economies, China, Hong Kong, Taiwan, Singapore and Japan , came through with only a brush rather than a collapse. None was immune: all lost some growth as regional demand and confidence drained away. But none suffered a currency-and-banking meltdown, and the reasons are the mirror image of what sank the others. Why the shock missed them The same crisis, met by stronger defences Economy Why it was shielded China A largely closed capital account : foreign money couldn't flood in or rush out freely, so there was no hot-money stampede to trigger. China also chose not to devalue the yuan , which, economists later said, helped stop the crisis becoming a region-wide currency meltdown. Hong Kong Its dollar is tied to the US dollar by a currency board , so an attack shows up as sky-high interest rates rather than a falling currency. On 23 October 1997 overnight rates briefly hit 280% and the stock market fell about 10% in a day, but the peg held. In August 1998 , facing speculators who were betting against both the currency and the stock market, the government spent HK$118 billion (about US$15 billion) buying shares over ten days. It worked, though it was hotly debated. Taiwan Strong fundamentals and few of the weaknesses: a current-account surplus , large foreign reserves , a high savings rate and little foreign debt . Its currency and stock market moved only modestly, so no panic took hold. Singapore A sound, well-regulated banking system , a flexibly managed exchange rate and large reserves as a buffer. Contagion still cost it growth, but it was never a target for speculators the way its neighbours were. Japan The regional giant, and not a peg-and-dollar-debt victim. Its problem was its own post-bubble slump (see our Japan explainer ). Japan absorbed spillover through weaker exports and bank exposure, and it helped fund the rescues rather than needing one. Less affected is not unaffected: Hong Kong, mainland China, Japan, Singapore, Taiwan, Brunei and Vietnam all took a hit to demand and confidence, just not a full-blown currency collapse. Sources in the notes. The common thread is telling. The economies that escaped either didn't let in the short-term dollar money in the first place (China's closed account), or held enough reserves and ran their currencies carefully enough that a run never started (Taiwan, Singapore), or had the reserves and the rules to win the fight when one did (Hong Kong). It's the same lesson the hit countries learned the hard way, seen from the other side."},{"id":"s10","h":"The lessons","t":"The crisis reshaped how Asia manages money. Burned by the experience, many of the hit economies spent the next decade building huge foreign-exchange reserves , a self-insurance stockpile of dollars so they'd never again be at the mercy of the IMF or a sudden outflow. The region grew warier of short-term foreign debt and fixed pegs. The deeper lesson is about how fast confidence travels . Nothing about Indonesia or Korea changed overnight in 1997; what changed was belief. Once investors decided Asian pegs were fragile, the rush for the exits made the fear come true, country after country. A crisis that began with one currency in Bangkok became a regional collapse because money, and panic, move together. So when one market cracks and others look nervous, ask two things. QUESTION 1 Do the other markets share the same hidden weakness? QUESTION 2 Does the rescue's remedy match what actually broke?"},{"id":"sources","h":"Sources","t":"Where the figures and claims come from. Overview, the July 1997 start in Thailand, the 2 July float and the spread across the region: Federal Reserve History , IMF and Reserve Bank of Australia . Thailand's reserves ($30.9 billion in June 1997) and $23.4 billion of forward obligations disclosed in August 1997: Federal Reserve Bank of San Francisco, \"Lessons from Thailand\" . Currency falls against the dollar, July 1997 to January 1998 (rupiah 81%, baht 56%, ringgit 46%, peso 41%; won 55% to its late-December low): Goldman Sachs ; consistent with the RBA's account . The four phases of the contagion: IMF World Economic Outlook, December 1997 . The Philippines floating the peso on 11 July 1997 (down about 10% that day) and its IMF support: BusinessWorld and IMF Occasional Paper 187 . Rescue sizes: Indonesia's ~$23 billion first line (IMF ~$10 billion, World Bank $4.5 billion, ADB $3.5 billion, plus Indonesia's own assets): IMF press release 97/50 ; the US $3 billion second-line pledge: NPR ; Thailand's and Korea's packages: IMF Survey, December 1997 and IMF Annual Report 1998 . The ~$58 billion Korea figure is the widely cited total including bilateral commitments. The IMF conditions and the debate over whether they helped or deepened the slump: US Congressional Research Service and the IMF's Independent Evaluation Office (2003) . 1998 real-GDP falls (Indonesia &minus;13.7%, Thailand &minus;9.4%, Malaysia &minus;6.7%, Korea &minus;5.8%): IMF Working Paper 99/138 . How the programmes were carried out. Indonesia: Suharto backtracking, the mishandled late-1997 bank closures and the slow bank-restructuring agency: IMF IEO annex on Indonesia . Korea's early-1998 gold-collecting campaign (about 3.5 million people, roughly 227 tonnes of gold). Malaysia's capital controls from 1 September 1998 and the 3.80 peg: IMF, Malaysia: Selected Issues and Bank Negara Malaysia ; the peg ending in July 2005: Taipei Times ; a favourable assessment of the controls: Athukorala and Jongwanich (ANU) . Hong Kong: overnight rates briefly at 280% on 23 October 1997: HKMA Annual Report 1997 ; the Hang Seng's 10.4% fall that day: Legislative Council paper ; the August 1998 HK$118 billion share purchases: HKMA and Dallas Fed . China's closed capital account and its decision not to devalue: Brookings and IMF . Taiwan's surplus, reserves and low external debt: Asian Crisis Project country report . Singapore's reserves and banking system: Singapore government (sg101) . Educational only. Figures are rounded and the history is simplified. Nothing here is investment, legal or tax advice."}]},{"id":"three-kingdoms","href":"explainers/three-kingdoms.html","title":"Three Kingdoms: How the Han Empire Broke Apart, 184–280","short":"The Three Kingdoms","topic":"Three Kingdoms","category":"History","date":"2026-11-23","dek":"A scrolling map of China from the Yellow Turbans to Jin's reunification, with six lives to follow through it","sec":[{"id":"","h":"","t":"Explainer · History · China Three Kingdoms In 184 a religious rising shook the Han empire, and the generals who put it down never gave their armies back. For the next century they and their heirs fought over China, until three states were left standing: Wei, Shu and Wu. Scroll through the map as the borders move, and follow one life through it. By Jun Wei Heung · Words and graphics November 23, 2026 · 30 min read 中文 Copy link Sources This follows the history, not the novel. From 155 to 220 the timeline goes year by year through the Annals of Emperor Wu , Cao Cao's own chapter in Chen Shou's Records of the Three Kingdoms (written in the 280s); after 220 it follows the other annals and biographies. Each step quotes the original text and says where it comes from. Stories that appear only in Pei Songzhi's fifth-century notes, or in later histories such as the Book of the Later Han and the Book of Jin , are labelled as such, and where the sources disagree, as over the death of Xun Yu, both versions are given. Borders are simplified to the Han provinces. Years are Western; months are the Chinese lunar months, so the twelfth month of a year often falls early in the next Western year. Key findings The Han dynasty did not fall to the Yellow Turban rising of 184. It fell to the generals who put the rising down and kept their armies. The north was settled at Guandu in 200, when Cao Cao beat Yuan Shao. The split of China was settled at Red Cliffs in 208, when Sun Quan and Liu Bei beat Cao Cao. The three-way division hardened in 219 , when Sun Quan broke his alliance with Liu Bei and seized Jing province. In 220–222 all three founders' houses declared themselves emperors. None of the three founders' families won. The Sima family took over Wei from within, and Sima Yi's grandson reunited China as the Jin dynasty in 280 ."},{"id":"s1","h":"An empire comes apart","t":"By the late second century the Han dynasty had ruled China for almost four hundred years. Its court was hollowing out: emperors came to the throne as children, and palace eunuchs and the families of empresses fought over who governed in their name. In the countryside, floods, epidemics and land-grabbing landlords drove farmers into debt and onto the roads. In 184 a healer named Zhang Jue turned that misery into a movement. His followers, the Yellow Turbans , rose across eastern China on the same day. The rising was crushed within a year, but to do it the court had let provincial officers and local strongmen raise armies of their own. When the capital itself fell into chaos in 189, those armies became the real power in the land. What followed was not one war but a century of them."},{"id":"s2","h":"The cast","t":"Six people carry the story here. Three founded states: Cao Cao in the north, Liu Bei in the west and Sun Quan in the south-east. Zhuge Liang and Guan Yu served Liu Bei. Sima Yi served the Cao family, and his family ended up taking their throne. Pick anyone on the chart to follow them through the timeline. Six lives across a century of war Lifespans against the events that framed them. Click a name to follow that person below Colours show which side each person served. Guan Yu's birth year is not recorded; the dashed bar shows that. Source: Chen Shou, Records of the Three Kingdoms ."},{"id":"s3","h":"The timeline","t":"Scroll to move through the century, year by year for Cao Cao's lifetime. The map shows who held each province; the dot and dotted trail show where the person you're following was, and where they had been. Each card has a quotation from the original text. When the record is silent about your person for a few years, the card says so rather than filling the gap. 184 Follow a life"},{"id":"s4","h":"History and the novel","t":"Most people meet this period through Romance of the Three Kingdoms , a novel put together around the 14th century and usually credited to Luo Guanzhong. It keeps the outline of events but turns Liu Bei into a saintly hero, Cao Cao into a villain and Zhuge Liang into something close to a wizard. Some of its best-known scenes are inventions or borrowings. What the novel added Twenty-nine famous scenes, what the record actually says, and the original text Scene in the novel What the history says The oath in the peach garden No oath is recorded. The history only says Liu Bei, Guan Yu and Zhang Fei were as close as brothers and shared a bed. Diaochan and Lü Bu Diaochan is fictional. The history mentions only that Lü Bu was having an affair with one of Dong Zhuo's maids and feared being found out. Borrowing arrows with straw boats Zhuge Liang did nothing of the kind. A later source tells a similar story about Sun Quan's own ship at Ruxu in 213. Summoning the east wind at Red Cliffs The fire attack was planned by Wu's commanders, Zhou Yu and his officer Huang Gai. Zhuge Liang's role was diplomatic. Guan Yu lets Cao Cao go at Huarong Invented. Cao Cao did retreat through Huarong in the mud after Red Cliffs, but no one ambushed him there. The empty fort A story in Pei Songzhi's notes, which Pei himself doubted: Sima Yi was not on that front in 228. Seven captures of Meng Huo Not in Chen Shou's history. It comes from a later source quoted in Pei's notes, and many historians treat it as legend. Sources: Chen Shou, Records of the Three Kingdoms , with Pei Songzhi's annotations; Rafe de Crespigny, Imperial Warlord , chapter on history, anecdote and fiction."},{"id":"s5","h":"What it cost","t":"The human cost was enormous, though hard to count. The Han census of 157 registered about 56 million people. When Jin counted its newly united empire in 280, it registered about 16 million . Not all of that gap was death: war and famine killed millions, but many more slipped off the registers as they fled, or were taken on as tenants and soldiers by great families whom no tax collector could touch. Either way, the state that came out of the war was far weaker than the one that went in. The unity didn't last. Within a generation of 280 the Jin princes were fighting each other, northern peoples overran the old heartland, and China split north from south for almost three centuries. The Three Kingdoms became the stuff of storytelling instead: in plays, the novel, and now films and video games. The real history is stranger, and often harsher, than the story. The Han was not toppled by its rebels. It was dismantled by the generals it sent to fight them."},{"id":"sources","h":"Sources","t":"Where the history comes from. The main source for the events, places, quotations and lives here: Chen Shou, Records of the Three Kingdoms ( Sanguozhi ), especially the Annals of Emperor Wu , with Pei Songzhi's fifth-century annotations. Full Chinese text: Chinese Text Project , Diancang and Chinese Notes ; modern Chinese translation: 211cn . Map views. People: registered population by commandery in the Han census of 140, from the treatise on commanderies of the Book of the Later Han , as tabulated in Wikipedia's list of Han provinces and commanderies and summed by the map's provinces (Liaodong, Yulin and Jiaozhi have no 140 figure; Yongchang's is disputed). National totals: 49,140,220 in 140 (Sima Biao's treatise, as given in Rafe de Crespigny's Provinces and Commandery Units of Later Han ), about 56.5 million in 157 (the Diwang Shiji , as quoted by Liu Zhao) and 16,163,863 for Jin in 280 ( Book of Jin , treatise on geography). Late figures: Wei in 263 from Huangfu Mi's Diwang Shiji ; Shu in 263 from Wang Yin's Shu Ji and Wu in 280 from the Jin Yangqiu , both quoted in Pei Songzhi's notes. Economy: places and policies from the Records (the farm colonies, Liu Bei's coin, Sun Quan's coins, Cao Pi's grain-and-silk trade, the salt officials) and the Han treatises on geography (salt and iron offices). Shu brocade: Zhuge Liang’s remark that the war depended on brocade and the Danyang Ji on Wei and Wu buying it, both quoted in the Taiping Yulan ; the “former brocade office” at Chengdu from the Huayang Guozhi . Shu’s salt and iron officials: the biographies of Wang Lian and Zhang Yi in the Records . Nanzhong’s supplies after 225: the biography of Zhuge Liang and the Huayang Guozhi (Nanzhong treatise). The Dujiangyan guard: the Shuijing Zhu (Jiang river). Events recorded outside the Records : Fan Ye, Book of the Later Han (Tao Qian, Kong Rong, Empress Fu), and the Book of Jin , Annals of Emperor Xuan (Sima Yi). Cao Cao's life and career, and how history, anecdote and fiction diverge: Rafe de Crespigny, Imperial Warlord: A Biography of Cao Cao 155–220 AD (Brill, 2010). The Sun family and the founding of Wu: Rafe de Crespigny, Generals of the South (ANU, 1990; open access). The dates of the three states, the fall of Shu in 263 and the founding of Jin: Encyclopaedia Britannica, \"Three Kingdoms\" . The registered populations of 157 and 280: the treatises on commanderies in the Book of the Later Han and on geography in the Book of Jin , as discussed in de Crespigny's work above. The novel and its relationship to the history: Columbia University, World Epics: Three Kingdoms . Province outlines on the map are simplified from historical atlases of the late Han and drawn by hand; they show which province each power held, not precise frontiers. Ages are approximate: Chinese sources count age differently, and some birth years are uncertain or unknown."},{"id":"s3","h":"155 Cao Cao is born · 曹操出生","t":"Emperor Huan reigns and the palace eunuchs are at the height of their power. Cao Cao is born in Qiao county, Pei. His ancestry will dog him all his life: his grandfather by adoption, Cao Teng, was a eunuch who served four emperors and was made a marquis, and his father Cao Song was Cao Teng’s adopted son, whose real parentage Chen Shou says no one could trace. The annals give no birth year; 155 is worked back from his age at death. 汉桓帝在位，宦官势力如日中天。这一年，曹操出生于沛国谯县。他的家世是一生的软肋：养祖父曹腾是历事四帝的宦官，官至中常侍、大长秋，封费亭侯；父亲曹嵩是曹腾的养子，生父是谁，陈寿说已无从查考。《武帝纪》没有写他的生年，155年是由他去世时“年六十六”倒推出来的。 太祖武皇帝，沛国谯人也，姓曹，讳操，字孟德，汉相国参之后。桓帝世，曹腾为中常侍大长秋，封费亭侯。养子嵩嗣，官至太尉，莫能审其生出本末。"},{"id":"s3","h":"174 A young officer in the capital · 洛阳北部尉","t":"Emperor Ling reigns. After the Proscriptions of 166 and 169 barred hundreds of scholar-officials from office for life, the eunuchs run the court. The young Cao Cao is recommended as “filial and incorrupt” and enters government. 汉灵帝在位。166年和169年两次“党锢之祸”之后，大批士人被禁锢终身，宦官把持朝政。年轻的曹操被举为孝廉，进入仕途。 年二十，举孝廉为郎，除洛阳北部尉，迁顿丘令，征拜议郎。"},{"id":"s3","h":"184 The Yellow Turbans · 黄巾起义","t":"For over a decade Zhang Jue of Julu had preached his “Way of Great Peace” across eight provinces. In 184 his thirty-six districts rose on the same day, wearing yellow headscarves. The court lifted the proscriptions and sent Huangfu Song, Zhu Jun and Lu Zhi against them; the main armies were broken within the year, but remnants and new bands such as the Black Mountain bandits fought on for years. To fight them the court let local officials raise their own troops, planting the seeds of the warlords. 巨鹿人张角以“太平道”传教十余年，信众遍布青、徐、幽、冀、荆、扬、兖、豫八州。184年，三十六方同日起事，头裹黄巾。朝廷解除党锢，派皇甫嵩、朱儁、卢植分路镇压，当年即击破主力，但各地余部和随之而起的“黑山”等武装延续多年。为平乱，朝廷允许地方募兵，种下了割据的根。 光和末，黄巾起。拜骑都尉，讨颍川贼。"},{"id":"s3","h":"187–188 Governors with armies · 废史立牧","t":"Since 184 Bian Zhang and Han Sui had been in revolt in Liang province, beating every army sent against them; the north-west slipped out of the court’s hands. In 188 Liu Yan, a member of the imperial clan, persuaded the court to turn provincial inspectors into governors with full military powers, and took Yi province for himself. The same year Emperor Ling created eight Colonels of the Western Garden under the eunuch Jian Shuo, Cao Cao among them. 凉州边章、韩遂自184年起反叛，屡败官军，西北从此脱离朝廷控制。188年，宗室刘焉建议改刺史为州牧，赋予地方长官军政大权，自己出任益州牧。同年，灵帝在西园设置八校尉，以宦官蹇硕为首，曹操也在其中。 顷之，冀州刺史王芬、南阳许攸、沛国周旌等连结豪杰，谋废灵帝，立合肥侯，以告太祖，太祖拒之。芬等遂败。"},{"id":"s3","h":"189 Dong Zhuo takes the capital · 董卓入京","t":"Emperor Ling dies in the fourth month; his son Liu Bian succeeds with the Empress Dowager He as regent. The commander-in-chief He Jin and Yuan Shao plan to wipe out the eunuchs and summon Dong Zhuo’s frontier army to frighten the court. The eunuchs kill He Jin first; Yuan Shao storms the palace and kills every eunuch he can find. Dong Zhuo enters Luoyang, absorbs He Jin’s troops, and in the ninth month deposes the boy and enthrones his half-brother Liu Xie, Emperor Xian. Yuan Shao flees to Ji. 四月灵帝驾崩，少帝刘辩即位，何太后临朝。大将军何进与袁绍谋诛宦官，召董卓等外兵进京施压，事未成而何进被宦官所杀，袁绍等随即攻入宫中尽诛宦官。董卓入洛阳，收编何进兄弟部曲，九月废少帝为弘农王，立陈留王刘协为帝，即汉献帝。袁绍出奔冀州。 卓表太祖为骁骑校尉，欲与计事。太祖乃变易姓名，间行东归。"},{"id":"s3","h":"190 The eastern coalition · 关东联军","t":"In the first month Yuan Shu, Han Fu, Kong Zhou, Liu Dai, Wang Kuang, Yuan Shao, Zhang Miao, Qiao Mao, Yuan Yi and Bao Xin all raise troops against Dong Zhuo and make Yuan Shao their leader. Dong Zhuo moves the emperor west to Chang’an, burns the palaces of Luoyang and loots the imperial tombs. The coalition lords sit in their camps; only Cao Cao and Sun Jian, Administrator of Changsha, actually fight Dong’s armies. Gongsun Du makes himself master of Liaodong, and Liu Biao rides alone into Jing and takes control of it. 正月，袁术、韩馥、孔伷、刘岱、王匡、袁绍、张邈、桥瑁、袁遗、鲍信等同时起兵，推袁绍为盟主，讨伐董卓。董卓挟献帝西迁长安，焚毁洛阳宫室，掘陵取宝。联军各怀心思，屯兵不进，只有曹操和长沙太守孙坚真正与董军交战。同年，公孙度据辽东自立，刘表单骑入荆州，平定宗贼，成为荆州之主。 到荥阳汴水，遇卓将徐荣，与战不利，士卒死伤甚多。太祖为流矢所中，所乘马被创，从弟洪以马与太祖，得夜遁。"},{"id":"s3","h":"191 Yuan Shao takes Ji · 袁绍夺冀州","t":"The coalition falls apart. Yuan Shao bullies Han Fu into handing over Ji province and becomes the strongest lord in the north, facing Gongsun Zan in You. Sun Jian routs Dong Zhuo’s army at Yangren, kills Hua Xiong and enters Luoyang; then, sent by Yuan Shu against Liu Biao, he is ambushed and killed near Xiangyang (some accounts say in 192). Zhang Lu, leader of the Five Pecks of Rice sect, seizes Hanzhong and cuts the roads. 联军瓦解。袁绍逼迫冀州牧韩馥让出冀州，成为北方最强的诸侯；公孙瓒在幽州与袁绍对峙。孙坚在阳人大破董卓军，斩华雄，进入洛阳，随后奉袁术之命攻打刘表，在襄阳附近中伏身亡（一说在192年）。五斗米道首领张鲁占据汉中，断绝斜谷道路。 袁绍因表太祖为东郡太守，治东武阳。"},{"id":"s3","h":"192 Dong Zhuo dies; the Qingzhou army · 董卓之死与青州兵","t":"In the fourth month Minister Wang Yun and Lü Bu assassinate Dong Zhuo. Dong’s generals Li Jue and Guo Si storm Chang’an, kill Wang Yun, drive out Lü Bu and take the emperor prisoner. A million Yellow Turbans from Qing pour into Yan province and kill its inspector Liu Dai, which opens the way for Cao Cao to take Yan. 四月，司徒王允与吕布刺杀董卓。董卓部将李傕、郭汜随即攻破长安，杀王允，赶走吕布，挟持献帝。青州黄巾百万之众涌入兖州，兖州刺史刘岱战死，曹操由此入主兖州。 冬，受降卒三十余万，男女百余万口，收其精锐者，号为青州兵。"},{"id":"s3","h":"193 War on Tao Qian · 征讨陶谦","t":"Yuan Shu marches north into Yan and is beaten by Cao Cao at Kuangting; he flees south to Jiujiang and takes Huainan. In You, Gongsun Zan kills the popular governor Liu Yu and rules alone. Cao Cao’s father Cao Song, sheltering in Langya, is killed in the territory of Tao Qian, governor of Xu. 袁术北上进攻兖州，被曹操在匡亭击败，南逃九江，据有淮南。公孙瓒在幽州杀害了深得人心的幽州牧刘虞，独霸幽州。曹操的父亲曹嵩避难琅琊，在徐州牧陶谦境内遇害。 秋，太祖征陶谦，下十余城，谦守城不敢出。"},{"id":"s3","h":"194 Yan rises against Cao Cao · 兖州之变","t":"While Cao Cao is again attacking Xu, Zhang Miao and Chen Gong invite Lü Bu into Yan, and nearly every county goes over to him; Cao Cao almost loses everything. Drought and locusts hit the east, grain prices soar and people eat each other. Tao Qian dies and leaves Xu to Liu Bei. In Yi, Liu Yan dies and his son Liu Zhang succeeds him. 曹操再征徐州，后方的张邈、陈宫迎吕布入兖州，各郡县纷纷响应，曹操几乎失去全部根据地。这一年关东大旱、蝗灾，粮价飞涨，人相食。陶谦病死，临终把徐州托付给刘备。益州牧刘焉去世，其子刘璋继位。 是岁谷一斛五十余万钱，人相食，乃罢吏兵新募者。"},{"id":"s3","h":"195 Yan recovered · 收复兖州","t":"Cao Cao beats Lü Bu again and again, and Lü Bu flees east to Liu Bei. In Chang’an Li Jue and Guo Si turn on each other; Emperor Xian escapes east, pursued all the way, and crosses the Yellow River to Anyi. Sun Ce borrows troops from Yuan Shu, crosses the Yangtze and sweeps through Danyang and Wu: the start of his realm in the south-east. 曹操连败吕布，吕布东奔刘备。长安城内李傕、郭汜火并，献帝趁乱东归，一路被追杀，渡过黄河到达安邑。孙策向袁术借兵渡江，横扫丹阳、吴郡，开始建立江东基业。 冬十月，天子拜太祖兖州牧。十二月，雍丘溃，超自杀。夷邈三族。"},{"id":"s3","h":"196 The emperor comes to Xu · 迎天子都许","t":"In the seventh month Emperor Xian, escorted by Yang Feng and Han Xian, reaches the ruins of Luoyang, where officials camp among the brambles and broken walls. Cao Cao comes to guard him and in the ninth month moves the court to his own base at Xu. From now on he gives orders in the emperor’s name. Lü Bu seizes Xiapi and the province of Xu while Liu Bei is fighting Yuan Shu. Yuan Shao’s son Yuan Tan takes Qing. 七月，献帝在杨奉、韩暹护送下回到残破的洛阳，百官“披荆棘，依墙壁间”。曹操入洛护驾，九月迁都许县。从此他“奉天子以令不臣”，以朝廷名义发号施令。吕布趁刘备与袁术交战，袭取下邳，夺了徐州。袁绍之子袁谭夺取青州。 是岁用枣祗、韩浩等议，始兴屯田。"},{"id":"s3","h":"197 Defeat at Wan · 宛城之败","t":"Zhang Xiu surrenders at Wan, then turns on Cao Cao and routs him; Cao Cao’s eldest son Cao Ang is killed. Yuan Shu proclaims himself emperor at Shouchun. His extravagance drives his followers away, and Sun Ce breaks with him. 张绣在宛城先降后叛，曹操惨败，长子曹昂战死。袁术在寿春称帝，国号“仲家”，奢靡无度，众叛亲离，孙策也与他断绝关系。 公与战，军败，为流矢所中，长子昂、弟子安民遇害。"},{"id":"s3","h":"198 The end of Lü Bu · 白门楼","t":"Cao Cao marches on Lü Bu, besieges Xiapi and floods it from the Si and Yi rivers. In the twelfth month of the year (early 199 by the Western calendar) Lü Bu’s officers tie up Chen Gong and open the gates; Lü Bu is captured and executed. Li Jue is killed in Chang’an the same year, and the warlords of the Wei valley submit to Cao Cao in name. 曹操东征吕布，围下邳，引泗水、沂水灌城。建安三年十二月（公历已是199年初），吕布部将缚陈宫出降，吕布被擒处死。同年，长安的李傕被朝廷所遣部将诛杀，关中诸将名义上归附曹操。 月余，布将宋宪、魏续等执陈宫，举城降，生禽布、宫，皆杀之。"},{"id":"s3","h":"199 On the eve of war · 大战前夜","t":"Yuan Shao destroys Gongsun Zan at Yijing and holds all four provinces north of the Yellow River, with over 100,000 troops, ready to march south. Yuan Shu, out of options, heads north to join Yuan Tan and dies on the way. Liu Bei, sent to intercept him, instead kills Cao Cao’s inspector of Xu at Xiapi and rebels. Cao Cao garrisons Guandu and waits for the clash. 袁绍在易京围杀公孙瓒，统一河北四州，拥兵十余万，准备南下。袁术走投无路，北投袁谭，途中病死。刘备奉命截击袁术，却在下邳杀死曹操任命的徐州刺史车胄，据徐州反曹。曹操分兵把守官渡，迎接决战。 程昱、郭嘉闻公遣备，言于公曰：「刘备不可纵。」公悔，追之不及。"},{"id":"s3","h":"200 Guandu · 官渡之战","t":"In the first month the “girdle edict” plot to kill Cao Cao is exposed, and General Dong Cheng and others are executed. Cao Cao smashes Liu Bei in the east, then kills Yuan Shao’s generals Yan Liang and Wen Chou at Baima and Yanjin, and holds Yuan’s main army at Guandu for half a year. In the tenth month Yuan’s adviser Xu You defects; Cao Cao raids Wuchao by night, burns the grain, and Yuan’s army collapses. Sun Ce is assassinated and Sun Quan, 18, succeeds him. 正月，“衣带诏”谋诛曹操之事败露，车骑将军董承等被杀。曹操东征击溃刘备，随后在白马、延津连斩袁绍大将颜良、文丑，又在官渡与袁绍主力相持半年。十月，袁绍谋士许攸来投，曹操夜袭乌巢，焚其粮草，袁军崩溃。同年，孙策遇刺身亡，十八岁的孙权继位。 时公兵不满万，伤者十二三。"},{"id":"s3","h":"201 Cangting · 仓亭","t":"Cao Cao beats Yuan Shao again at Cangting. Liu Bei stirs up Yellow Turban remnants in Runan; when Cao Cao marches south, he flees to Liu Biao in Jing and is stationed at Xinye. 曹操在仓亭再败袁绍。刘备在汝南联合黄巾余部作乱，曹操南征，刘备投奔荆州刘表，屯驻新野。 绍之未破也，使刘备略汝南，汝南贼共都等应之。"},{"id":"s3","h":"202 Yuan Shao dies · 袁绍之死","t":"In the fifth month Yuan Shao dies, vomiting blood. He had favoured his youngest son Yuan Shang over his eldest, Yuan Tan, and the two brothers each hold on to their own followers. The north turns on itself. 五月，袁绍发病呕血而死。他偏爱幼子袁尚，未立长子袁谭，两兄弟各拥部众，河北陷入内斗。 吾起义兵，为天下除暴乱。旧土人民，死丧略尽，国中终日行，不见所识，使吾凄怆伤怀。"},{"id":"s3","h":"203 Brother against brother · 二袁相争","t":"Cao Cao takes Liyang and the Yuan brothers fall back on Ye. On Guo Jia’s advice he turns south as if to attack Liu Biao and lets the brothers fight; beaten by Yuan Shang, Yuan Tan asks Cao Cao for help. 曹操攻破黎阳，袁谭、袁尚退守邺城。曹操听从郭嘉的建议佯攻刘表，任由兄弟相残；袁谭被袁尚打败，反向曹操求援。 谭、尚争冀州，谭为尚所败，走保平原。"},{"id":"s3","h":"204 The fall of Ye · 邺城","t":"Cao Cao besieges Ye, the Yuan family’s capital, digs a forty-li trench and floods the city from the Zhang River; more than half the people inside starve. In the eighth month the city falls; its commander Shen Pei refuses to surrender and is executed. Ye becomes Cao Cao’s capital and he takes the governorship of Ji. 曹操围攻袁氏的根据地邺城，掘壕四十里，引漳水灌城，城中饿死者过半。八月城破，守将审配宁死不降。曹操从此以邺城为根据地，自领冀州牧。 公临祀绍墓，哭之流涕；慰劳绍妻，还其家人宝物，赐杂缯絮，廪食之。"},{"id":"s3","h":"205 The north-east settled · 冀州平定","t":"In the first month Cao Cao kills Yuan Tan at Nanpi. Yuan Xi’s officers in You defect, and Yuan Xi and Yuan Shang flee to the Wuhuan. Most of the north is now Cao Cao’s; Zhang Yan of the Black Mountain bandits surrenders with over 100,000 followers. 正月，曹操在南皮斩杀袁谭。幽州的袁熙部将焦触等叛降，袁熙、袁尚逃往乌桓。河北大部平定，黑山军首领张燕率十余万众归降。 令民不得复私雠，禁厚葬，皆一之于法。"},{"id":"s3","h":"206 Bing province · 并州","t":"Gao Gan, Yuan Shao’s nephew and inspector of Bing, rebels and holds Huguan Pass. Cao Cao marches against him; Huguan surrenders, Gao Gan is killed fleeing south, and Bing is taken. 袁绍的外甥、并州刺史高干反叛，据守壶关。曹操亲征，壶关投降，高干南逃途中被杀，并州平定。"},{"id":"s3","h":"207 Beyond the Wall · 北征乌桓","t":"Cao Cao marches beyond the Great Wall against the Wuhuan, routs them at White Wolf Mountain and kills their chieftain Tadun. Yuan Shang and Yuan Xi flee to Liaodong, where Gongsun Kang cuts off their heads and sends them to Cao Cao. The Yuan family is finished and the north is united. In winter Liu Bei calls three times on Zhuge Liang at Longzhong. 曹操远征塞外的乌桓，在白狼山大破敌军，斩其单于蹋顿。袁尚、袁熙逃往辽东，被公孙康斩首送来，袁氏覆灭，北方统一。同年冬，刘备三顾茅庐，见到了隐居隆中的诸葛亮。 八月，登白狼山，卒与虏遇，众甚盛。……公登高，望虏阵不整，乃纵兵击之，使张辽为先锋，虏众大崩，斩蹋顿及名王已下，胡、汉降者二十余万口。"},{"id":"s3","h":"208 Red Cliffs · 赤壁之战","t":"In the sixth month the Han abolishes its three senior ministries and Cao Cao becomes Chancellor. In the eighth the scholar Kong Rong is executed (Book of the Later Han). Cao Cao marches south; Liu Biao dies and his son Liu Cong surrenders Jing, and Liu Bei flees. Zhuge Liang goes to Sun Quan, who decides to fight and sends Zhou Yu and Cheng Pu up the Yangtze. With Liu Bei they defeat Cao Cao at Red Cliffs. Cao Cao goes north, leaving Cao Ren in Jiangling. 六月，汉朝废三公，曹操出任丞相。八月，孔融被杀（见《后汉书·孔融传》）。七月曹操南征，刘表病死，其子刘琮举荆州投降，刘备南逃。诸葛亮出使江东，孙权决意抗曹，派周瑜、程普率军西上，与刘备联军在赤壁击败曹军。曹操北还，留曹仁守江陵。 公至赤壁，与备战，不利。于是大疫，吏士多死者，乃引军还。"},{"id":"s3","h":"209 Jing changes hands · 荆州易手","t":"Zhou Yu besieges Jiangling for over a year until Cao Ren abandons it. Liu Bei takes the four southern commanderies of Wuling, Changsha, Guiyang and Lingling, is acclaimed governor of Jing with his seat at Gong’an, and marries Sun Quan’s sister. Cao Cao trains a navy at Qiao and moves to Hefei. 周瑜围攻江陵一年有余，曹仁弃城北撤。刘备南征武陵、长沙、桂阳、零陵四郡，被推为荆州牧，治公安，并娶孙权之妹。曹操在谯县训练水军，进驻合肥。 秋七月，自涡入淮，出肥水，军合肥。"},{"id":"s3","h":"210 Talent above all · 唯才是举","t":"Zhou Yu dies at 36 while preparing to march on Yi. His successor Lu Su argues for lending Nan commandery in Jing to Liu Bei so the two can hold off Cao Cao together. Sun Quan sends Bu Zhi to govern Jiao, and Shi Xie and his brothers submit to Wu. Cao Cao issues his edict seeking talent and builds the Bronze Bird Terrace at Ye. 周瑜在准备西取益州时病逝，年三十六，鲁肃接任，主张借荆州南郡给刘备共拒曹操。孙权派步骘为交州刺史，士燮兄弟归附东吴。曹操发布《求贤令》，在邺城修建铜雀台。 若必廉士而后可用，则齐桓其何以霸世！……二三子其佐我明扬仄陋，唯才是举，吾得而用之。"},{"id":"s3","h":"211 Tong Pass · 潼关之战","t":"Cao Cao sends Zhong Yao and Xiahou Yuan through the Wei valley against Zhang Lu in Hanzhong. The warlords of the valley suspect they are the real target; Ma Chao, Han Sui and eight others rebel and hold Tong Pass. Cao Cao takes the field, crosses the Wei River, sows distrust between them on Jia Xu’s advice, and crushes them. Liu Zhang invites Liu Bei into Yi to help against Zhang Lu. 曹操派钟繇、夏侯渊借道关中讨伐汉中张鲁，关中诸将疑心是冲着自己来的，马超、韩遂等十部起兵，屯守潼关。曹操亲征，渡渭水后用贾诩的离间计，大破关中联军。同年，刘璋迎刘备入蜀，共拒张鲁。 公与遂交马语移时，不及军事，但说京都旧故，拊手欢笑。"},{"id":"s3","h":"212 The death of Xun Yu · 荀彧之死","t":"The emperor allows Cao Cao to be announced without his name, to walk rather than hurry at court, and to wear his sword and shoes in the hall. Ma Teng, held in the capital, is executed with his family because of Ma Chao’s revolt. When Dong Zhao and others push to make Cao Cao Duke of Wei with the Nine Bestowments, his most important adviser, Xun Yu, objects, and soon dies at Shouchun while with the army. Chen Shou says he died of worry; a later source quoted by Pei Songzhi says Cao Cao sent him an empty food box and he took poison. That is where the story of Cao Cao ordering his death comes from. At the end of the year Liu Bei turns on Liu Zhang. 献帝准许曹操“赞拜不名，入朝不趋，剑履上殿”。在许都的马腾因马超反叛被诛灭三族。董昭等人谋划为曹操晋爵魏公、加九锡，曹操最重要的谋臣荀彧表示反对，随即在随军途中死于寿春。陈寿说他“以忧薨”，裴注引《魏氏春秋》则说他收到曹操送来的空食盒后服毒自尽：“曹操赐死荀彧”一说即源于此。年底，刘备与刘璋决裂，回军攻打成都。 太祖由是心不能平。会征孙权，表请彧劳军于谯，因辄留彧……彧疾留寿春，以忧薨，时年五十。／《魏氏春秋》：太祖馈彧食，发之乃空器也，于是饮药而卒。"},{"id":"s3","h":"213 Duke of Wei · 魏公与九锡","t":"Cao Cao attacks Ruxu and withdraws after a month’s stand-off with Sun Quan. The court merges the fourteen provinces into nine, enlarging Ji, and carves ten of its commanderies into the state of Wei. In the fifth month the emperor names Cao Cao Duke of Wei with the Nine Bestowments; in the seventh the altars and ancestral temple of Wei are built, and the emperor takes three of Cao Cao’s daughters as consorts. Liu Bei besieges Luo. 曹操进攻濡须口，与孙权相持月余后撤军。朝廷合并十四州为九州，扩大冀州，以冀州十郡建立魏国。五月，献帝策命曹操为魏公，加九锡；七月建魏国社稷宗庙，献帝又聘曹操的三个女儿为贵人。刘备围攻雒城。 五月丙申，天子使御史大夫郗虑持节策命公为魏公。"},{"id":"s3","h":"214 An empress dies; Liu Bei takes Chengdu · 伏皇后之死；刘备入成都","t":"Xiahou Yuan pacifies the far west; Ma Chao flees to Zhang Lu and then to Liu Bei, and Liang is Cao Cao’s. In summer Liu Bei takes Luo, Chengdu surrenders and Liu Zhang is sent away to Jing. In the eleventh month a letter Empress Fu had written years before to her father, saying the emperor resented Cao Cao, comes to light. She is deposed and dies in confinement, and her brothers are executed. 夏侯渊平定陇右，马超投奔张鲁后又归刘备，凉州平定。夏天，刘备攻下雒城，成都投降，刘璋被迁往荆州。十一月，伏皇后多年前写给父亲伏完、说献帝怨恨曹操的书信被揭发，皇后被废，幽禁而死，兄弟皆被处死。 十一月，汉皇后伏氏坐昔与父故屯骑校尉完书，云帝以董承被诛怨恨公，辞甚丑恶，发闻，后废黜死，兄弟皆伏法。"},{"id":"s3","h":"215 Hanzhong, Hefei and the Xiang River · 汉中、合肥与湘水之盟","t":"In the first month Cao Cao’s daughter Cao Jie is made empress. Cao Cao marches on Zhang Lu, breaks through Yangping Pass and Zhang Lu surrenders: Hanzhong is his. Sun Quan seizes the moment to attack Hefei with 100,000 men and is beaten by Zhang Liao and Li Dian with a garrison of 7,000. Sun and Liu nearly go to war over Jing and split it along the Xiang River: Changsha, Jiangxia and Guiyang to Wu, Nan, Lingling and Wuling to Liu Bei. 正月，曹操之女曹节被立为皇后。曹操西征张鲁，攻破阳平关，张鲁投降，汉中归曹。孙权趁机率十万大军攻合肥，被张辽、李典以七千守军击败。孙刘为荆州几乎开战，最终以湘水为界平分：长沙、江夏、桂阳归吴，南郡、零陵、武陵归刘备。 冬十一月，鲁自巴中将其余众降。封鲁及五子皆为列侯。"},{"id":"s3","h":"216 King of Wei · 魏王","t":"In the fifth month the emperor raises Cao Cao to King of Wei, one step from the throne. Huchuquan, chanyu of the Southern Xiongnu, comes to court and is kept at Ye while the Wise King of the Right, Qubei, governs his people. The minister Cui Yan is accused of mockery in a letter, sentenced to hard labour, and then ordered to kill himself (Biography of Cui Yan). 五月，献帝晋封曹操为魏王，距离帝位只差一步。南匈奴单于呼厨泉来朝，被留在邺城，由右贤王去卑回去监国。尚书崔琰被告发书信中有讽刺之意，先被罚作徒隶，后被赐死（《崔琰传》）。 夏五月，天子进公爵为魏王。"},{"id":"s3","h":"217 An heir, and a plague · 立太子与大疫","t":"Cao Cao attacks Ruxu again; Sun Quan sends Xu Xiang to sue for peace and offer submission, and the two renew their ties. Lu Su dies and Lü Meng takes over Wu’s upriver command. In the tenth month Cao Pi is named Crown Prince of Wei: his brother Cao Zhi has lost the contest to succeed. An epidemic sweeps the north; five of the Seven Masters of the Jian’an era, Wang Can, Xu Gan, Chen Lin, Ying Yang and Liu Zhen, die. 曹操再攻濡须，孙权派都尉徐详求和请降，双方重修旧好。鲁肃去世，吕蒙接掌长江上游的兵权。十月，曹丕被立为魏太子，曹植在继承之争中落败。这一年大疫流行，建安七子中的王粲、徐干、陈琳、应玚、刘桢相继去世。 昔年疾疫，亲故多离其灾，徐、陈、应、刘，一时俱逝。"},{"id":"s3","h":"218 Revolt in Xu, war for Hanzhong · 许都之乱与汉中之争","t":"In the first month the imperial physician Ji Ben, with Geng Ji and Wei Huang, rises in Xu and burns the camp of the Chancellor’s chief clerk Wang Bi; the revolt is crushed at once. Liu Bei advances on Hanzhong and faces Xiahou Yuan and Zhang He around Yangping. Cao Cao marches west and reaches Chang’an in the ninth month. 正月，太医令吉本与少府耿纪、司直韦晃在许都起兵，火烧丞相长史王必的军营，旋即被平定。刘备进军汉中，与夏侯渊、张郃在阳平关一带相持。曹操西征，九月抵达长安。 古之葬者，必居瘠薄之地。其规西门豹祠西原上为寿陵，因高为基，不封不树。"},{"id":"s3","h":"219 Dingjun Mountain and Fancheng · 定军山与樊城","t":"In the first month Xiahou Yuan is killed by Huang Zhong at Dingjun Mountain. Cao Cao comes to Hanzhong himself, withdraws in the fifth month, and Liu Bei proclaims himself King of Hanzhong. In the seventh month Guan Yu attacks Fancheng; in the eighth the Han River floods, Yu Jin’s seven armies are drowned, Yu Jin surrenders and Pang De is executed. Guan Yu’s “fame shakes the realm.” Then Sun Quan, secretly in touch with Cao Cao, sends Lü Meng with soldiers disguised as merchants to seize Jiangling and Gong’an. Caught between two enemies, Guan Yu is captured at Linju in the twelfth month and killed. 正月，夏侯渊在定军山被黄忠斩杀。曹操亲至汉中，五月撤军，刘备称汉中王。七月关羽北攻樊城，八月汉水暴涨，于禁七军被淹，于禁投降，庞德被斩，关羽“威震华夏”。孙权暗中与曹操联络，派吕蒙白衣渡江袭取江陵、公安，关羽腹背受敌，十二月在临沮被擒杀。 八月，汉水溢，灌禁军，军没，羽获禁，遂围仁。"},{"id":"s3","h":"220 Cao Cao dies; the Han ends · 曹操去世，汉朝终结","t":"In the first month Cao Cao dies at Luoyang and Cao Pi succeeds him as King of Wei. In the tenth month Cao Pi makes Emperor Xian abdicate, takes the throne as emperor of Wei, and posthumously names his father Emperor Wu, temple name Taizu. After more than four centuries the Han dynasty is over; the last emperor becomes Duke of Shanyang. 正月，曹操在洛阳去世，曹丕继位为魏王。十月，曹丕逼汉献帝禅让，即皇帝位，改元黄初，国号魏，追尊曹操为武皇帝，庙号太祖。延续四百余年的汉朝至此终结，献帝被封为山阳公。 庚子，王崩于洛阳，年六十六。"},{"id":"s3","h":"221–222 A third emperor; Yiling · 刘备称帝与夷陵之战","t":"In the fourth month of 221 Liu Bei takes the title of emperor at Chengdu; his state calls itself Han, and history calls it Shu Han. Sun Quan declares himself Wei’s vassal and is made King of Wu. Liu Bei marches against him; Zhang Fei is murdered by his own officers before the campaign begins. In 222 the Wu general Lu Xun burns Liu Bei’s line of camps at Yiling and routs him. Wei then attacks Wu on three fronts, and Sun Quan breaks with Wei. 221年四月，刘备在成都称帝，国号汉，史称蜀汉。孙权向魏称臣，受封吴王。刘备东征孙权，出发前张飞被部将杀害。222年，吴将陆逊在夷陵（猇亭）火烧刘备连营七百里，蜀军大败。同年魏军三路伐吴，孙权与魏决裂。 章武元年夏四月丙午，即皇帝位于成都武担之南。"},{"id":"s3","h":"223 The charge at Baidi · 白帝托孤","t":"Liu Bei dies at Baidi. His son Liu Shan succeeds him, and Zhuge Liang, as Chancellor, takes charge of the whole state. He sends Deng Zhi to Wu and restores the alliance. 刘备在白帝城病逝，太子刘禅即位，诸葛亮以丞相身份开府治事，总揽蜀汉军政。诸葛亮派邓芝出使东吴，恢复了吴蜀联盟。 君才十倍曹丕，必能安国，终定大事。若嗣子可辅，辅之；如其不才，君可自取。"},{"id":"s3","h":"225–228 The south, then the north · 南征与第一次北伐","t":"In 225 Zhuge Liang pacifies the rebellious south. In 226 Cao Pi dies and Cao Rui succeeds him. In 227 Zhuge Liang submits his memorial on taking the field and moves to Hanzhong. In spring 228 he marches out by Qishan and three commanderies in the west go over to him, but Ma Su is routed at Jieting and the campaign collapses. That autumn the Wu general Lu Xun crushes Cao Xiu of Wei at Shiting. 225年，诸葛亮亲征南中，平定叛乱。226年魏文帝曹丕去世，曹叡即位。227年诸葛亮上《出师表》，进驻汉中。228年春出祁山，陇右三郡响应，但马谡在街亭大败，北伐功亏一篑。同年秋，吴将陆逊在石亭大破魏军曹休。 臣本布衣，躬耕于南阳，苟全性命于乱世，不求闻达于诸侯。"},{"id":"s3","h":"229 Sun Quan takes the throne · 孙权称帝","t":"Sun Quan proclaims himself emperor at Wuchang and moves his capital back to Jianye. Some in Shu call it usurpation, but Zhuge Liang insists on sending congratulations and keeping the alliance; the two states even agree how to divide Wei between them once it falls. Zhuge Liang’s third campaign takes the commanderies of Wudu and Yinping. 孙权在武昌称帝，随后迁都建业。蜀汉朝中有人认为这是僭越，诸葛亮仍坚持遣使祝贺、维持联盟，两国约定灭魏之后中分天下。同年诸葛亮第三次北伐，攻取武都、阴平二郡。 夏四月，夏口、武昌并言黄龙、凤凰见。丙申，南郊即皇帝位。"},{"id":"s3","h":"231–234 The Wuzhang Plains · 五丈原","t":"In 231 Zhuge Liang marches out by Qishan again, moving grain on his “wooden oxen,” and kills the Wei general Zhang He. In 234 he makes his last campaign, with “gliding horses” for supply and soldiers farming on the Wuzhang Plains, and faces Sima Yi for over a hundred days. Sima Yi will not give battle. In the eighth month Zhuge Liang dies in camp and Shu withdraws. Sun Quan’s attack on the new fort at Hefei the same year also fails. 231年诸葛亮再出祁山，用木牛运粮，射杀魏将张郃。234年他最后一次北伐，以流马运粮，在五丈原屯田，与司马懿对峙百余日。司马懿坚守不战，八月诸葛亮病逝军中，蜀军撤退。同年孙权配合出兵合肥新城，无功而返。 其年八月，亮疾病，卒于军，时年五十四。"},{"id":"s3","h":"238–239 Liaodong falls · 平定辽东","t":"Gongsun Yuan of Liaodong declares himself King of Yan, and Emperor Cao Rui sends Sima Yi against him. Sima Yi takes Xiangping and massacres many of its people, and Liaodong becomes part of Wei. Cao Rui dies in the first month of 239; the eight-year-old Cao Fang succeeds him with Cao Shuang and Sima Yi as joint regents. 辽东的公孙渊自立为燕王，魏明帝曹叡派司马懿出征。司马懿围攻襄平，城破后大肆屠戮，辽东并入魏国。239年正月曹叡病逝，年仅八岁的曹芳即位，由曹爽与司马懿共同辅政。"},{"id":"s3","h":"249 The Gaoping Tombs · 高平陵之变","t":"Cao Shuang has been monopolising power and sidelining Sima Yi. In the first month, while Cao Shuang and his brothers are outside Luoyang escorting the young emperor to Cao Rui’s tomb at Gaoping, Sima Yi shuts the city gates, takes the palace guard and has them stripped of office. Cao Shuang gives up his command; soon after he and his allies are executed with their families on a charge of treason. Power in Wei now belongs to the Sima family. 曹爽专权，排挤司马懿。正月，曹爽兄弟陪同曹芳出城拜谒明帝的高平陵，司马懿趁机关闭洛阳城门，控制禁军，奏免曹爽兄弟官职。曹爽交出兵权后，不久即以谋反罪与党羽一同被诛灭三族。魏国大权从此落入司马氏手中。 嘉平元年春正月甲午，车驾谒高平陵。太傅司马宣王奏免大将军曹爽、爽弟中领军羲、武卫将军训、散骑常侍彦官，以侯就第。"},{"id":"s3","h":"251–252 Sima Yi and Sun Quan die · 司马懿与孙权相继去世","t":"In 251 Wang Ling plots in Huainan to depose the emperor; Sima Yi marches against him and Wang Ling kills himself. Sima Yi dies in the eighth month, and his eldest son Sima Shi takes over. In 252 Sun Quan dies in the shadow of the bitter succession quarrel between two of his sons; the young Sun Liang succeeds him with Zhuge Ke as regent. 251年，太尉王凌在淮南谋废曹芳，司马懿亲自出兵，王凌自杀；同年八月司马懿去世，长子司马师继续掌权。252年，孙权在晚年储位之争（“二宫之争”）的阴影中去世，年幼的孙亮即位，诸葛恪辅政。"},{"id":"s3","h":"263 The fall of Shu · 蜀汉灭亡","t":"Sima Zhao, now ruling Wei, sends Zhong Hui, Deng Ai and Zhuge Xu against Shu. Jiang Wei holds Zhong Hui at Jiange, but Deng Ai crosses the mountains by the Yinping path and strikes at Jiangyou and Mianzhu, where Zhuge Liang’s son Zhuge Zhan and grandson Zhuge Shang die fighting. Liu Shan surrenders Chengdu. Shu Han has lasted 43 years. 掌权的司马昭派钟会、邓艾、诸葛绪三路伐蜀。姜维在剑阁挡住钟会，邓艾却从阴平小道翻山越岭直取江油、绵竹，诸葛亮之子诸葛瞻、孙子诸葛尚战死。刘禅开成都投降，蜀汉灭亡，立国四十三年。"},{"id":"s3","h":"265–266 Jin replaces Wei · 晋代魏","t":"Sima Zhao dies and his son Sima Yan succeeds him as King of Jin. In the twelfth month (early 266 by the Western calendar) Sima Yan makes the last Wei emperor, Cao Huan, abdicate and founds the Jin dynasty. Wei falls after 46 years, the same way Cao Pi took the throne from the Han. 司马昭去世，其子司马炎继任晋王。同年十二月（公历已是266年初），司马炎逼魏元帝曹奂禅让，建立晋朝，是为晋武帝。曹魏立国四十六年而亡，正如四十五年前曹丕代汉。"},{"id":"s3","h":"280 All under Jin · 三国归晋","t":"Jin attacks Wu on six fronts; Wang Jun brings a fleet of tower ships down the Yangtze from Yi to Jianye. Sun Hao, the last ruler of Wu, surrenders. Almost a century of division and war since the Yellow Turbans is over, though Jin’s unity will fall apart within twenty years in the War of the Eight Princes. 晋军六路伐吴，王濬率楼船从益州顺江而下，直抵建业。吴主孙皓投降，东吴灭亡。自184年黄巾起义算起近百年的分裂与战乱结束，但晋朝的统一不到二十年就在“八王之乱”中瓦解。 太祖运筹演谋，鞭挞宇内，揽申、商之法术，该韩、白之奇策……抑可谓非常之人，超世之杰矣。"},{"id":"s3","h":"Cao Cao · 曹操","t":"Born in Qiao county, Pei. 生于沛国谯县。 As a youth he was “chivalrous and wild, with no fixed occupation”; only Qiao Xuan and He Yong thought much of him. Qiao Xuan told him: “The world is about to fall into chaos; only a man of genius can save it, and the one to bring peace may be you.” As commandant in Luoyang he hung coloured staves at his gate and beat to death anyone breaking the curfew, however powerful, including an uncle of the favourite eunuch Jian Shuo (Pei’s notes, quoting the Cao Man Zhuan). 少年时“任侠放荡，不治行业”，只有桥玄、何颙看重他。桥玄对他说：“天下将乱，非命世之才不能济也，能安之者，其在君乎！”他任洛阳北部尉时造五色大棒悬于县门，犯禁者不避豪强一律杖杀，连宠宦蹇硕的叔父夜行也被打死（裴注引《曹瞒传》）。 As Chief Commandant of Cavalry he fights the Yellow Turbans in Yingchuan under Huangfu Song and Zhu Jun. Made chancellor of Jinan, he finds most of the heads of its ten-odd counties corrupt hangers-on of the powerful, has eight of them dismissed, and bans unofficial cults; “the wicked fled and the commandery was orderly.” 任骑都尉，随皇甫嵩、朱儁讨伐颍川黄巾。随后迁济南相：国中十余县的长吏多依附权贵、贪赃枉法，他奏免了其中八人，又禁断淫祀，“奸宄逃窜，郡界肃然”。 Offered the post of Administrator of Dong, he pleads illness and goes home to Qiao, where he builds a retreat outside the city, “reading in summer and autumn, hunting in winter and spring” (his own later account, quoted by Pei). He refuses to join Wang Fen’s plot to depose the emperor. In 188 he is made one of the eight Colonels of the Western Garden. 被任命为东郡太守，他称病不就，回到谯县，在城外筑精舍，“秋夏读书，冬春射猎”（裴注引《魏武故事》所载曹操自述）。他拒绝参与王芬废立皇帝的密谋。188年被任命为西园八校尉之一的典军校尉。 He turns down Dong Zhuo, changes his name and escapes east by back roads; at Zhongmou a local officer arrests him on suspicion, and he is freed only because someone recognises him and pleads for him. Pei gives three very different versions of how he killed members of Lü Boshe’s household on the way; one, from Sun Sheng, has him say: “Better that I betray others than that others betray me.” At Chenliu he spends his family fortune raising troops, and in the twelfth month takes the field at Jiwu. 他拒绝董卓的拉拢，改名换姓抄小路东逃；过中牟时被亭长怀疑扣押，幸有人认出他、代为求情才获释。在成皋吕伯奢家杀人一事，裴注引了三种说法，出入很大，其中孙盛《杂记》记他说：“宁我负人，毋人负我。”到陈留后，他散尽家财，招募义兵，十二月在己吾起兵。 He joins the coalition as acting General of Vigorous Might. The armies at Suanzao feast every day and none will advance; he alone marches west and is beaten at the Bian River. Back at Suanzao he berates the lords, to no effect, and goes south with Xiahou Dun to recruit in Yang province. When Yuan Shao and Han Fu propose to make Liu Yu emperor, he refuses. 以行奋武将军身份加入关东联军。诸军屯驻酸枣，日日置酒高会，无人敢先进，只有他引兵西进，在荥阳汴水大败。他回到酸枣责备诸将，无人响应，于是与夏侯惇等前往扬州募兵。袁绍、韩馥打算拥立刘虞为帝，他也拒绝了。 When the Black Mountain bandits attack Dong commandery he beats Bai Rao at Puyang and is made Administrator of Dong, his first base. 黑山军于毒、白绕等攻打东郡，他在濮阳击破白绕，被袁绍表为东郡太守，治东武阳。这是他的第一块根据地。 Bao Xin and others invite him to take charge of Yan. Fighting the Yellow Turbans east of Shouzhang, Bao Xin is killed; Cao Cao offers a reward for his body, cannot find it, and mourns over a wooden image of him. He follows the rebels to Jibei and takes the surrender of 300,000 soldiers, the core of the Qingzhou army. 济北相鲍信等人迎他领兖州牧。他在寿张以东与黄巾苦战，鲍信力战而死，曹操悬赏寻找其尸体未得，以木刻其形而哭祭。他追击黄巾至济北，受降卒三十余万，收编为“青州兵”。 He routs Yuan Shu at Kuangting and chases him to Jiujiang. In autumn, to avenge his father, he attacks Tao Qian and takes more than ten cities. Fan Ye’s Book of the Later Han says he killed hundreds of thousands, “not a dog or chicken left, and the Si River choked with bodies.” 在匡亭大破袁术，追至九江。秋天以父仇为名东征陶谦，攻下十余城。范晔《后汉书·陶谦传》说此役“凡杀男女数十万人，鸡犬无余，泗水为之不流”。 In summer he invades Xu again, “with much slaughter wherever he passed.” Zhang Miao and Chen Gong bring Lü Bu into Yan; only Juancheng, held by Xun Yu and Cheng Yu, and two other counties stay loyal. He faces Lü Bu at Puyang for over a hundred days. In one night battle his Qingzhou troops break; riding out through the flames he falls from his horse and burns his left palm, and the officer Lou Yi helps him back into the saddle. Locusts and famine end the fighting. 夏天再征徐州，“所过多所残戮”。张邈、陈宫迎吕布入兖州，只剩荀彧、程昱守住的鄄城和范、东阿二县。他回师与吕布在濮阳相持百余日：一次夜战中青州兵被冲散，他冲出火海时坠马，烧伤左手掌，靠司马楼异扶他上马才得以脱身。蝗灾饥荒之下，双方各自罢兵。 He beats Lü Bu at Dingtao and Juye. In the eighth month he besieges Zhang Miao’s brother Zhang Chao at Yongqiu; it falls in the twelfth, Zhang Chao kills himself, Zhang Miao is killed by his own men, and Cao Cao exterminates Zhang Miao’s family. In the tenth month he is formally appointed Governor of Yan. 在定陶、巨野接连击败吕布。八月围攻张邈之弟张超于雍丘，十二月城破，张超自杀，张邈被部下所杀，他诛灭张邈三族。十月被正式任命为兖州牧。 Many of his officers doubt the wisdom of taking in the emperor; Xun Yu and Cheng Yu persuade him. He goes to Luoyang, is given the insignia of command and control of the secretariat, and in the ninth month moves the court to Xu as Grand General and Marquis of Wuping. When Yuan Shao is offended at ranking below him, he yields the title to Yuan Shao and becomes Minister of Works. He takes in Liu Bei, driven out of Xu by Lü Bu. 迎天子一事诸将多有疑虑，荀彧、程昱力劝。他入洛护驾，被授予节钺、录尚书事，九月迁都许县，任大将军、封武平侯。袁绍以位在其下为耻，他把大将军让给袁绍，自任司空、行车骑将军。刘备被吕布夺去徐州后来投，他以刘备为豫州牧。 At Wan Zhang Xiu surrenders. According to Zhang Xiu’s biography, Cao Cao took the widow of Zhang Xiu’s uncle Zhang Ji as a concubine; Zhang Xiu, resentful and hearing that Cao Cao meant to have him killed, attacks the camp. Cao Cao is hit by an arrow; his eldest son Cao Ang, his nephew Cao Anmin and his bodyguard Dian Wei are killed. In the ninth month he marches on the self-proclaimed emperor Yuan Shu, who abandons his army and flees across the Huai. 正月到宛，张绣投降。《张绣传》说他纳了张绣叔父张济的遗孀，张绣怀恨，又听说曹操要除掉自己，于是突袭曹营。曹操中流矢，长子曹昂、侄子曹安民和大将典韦战死。九月他东征称帝的袁术，袁术弃军渡淮而逃。 He creates the post of Libationer of the Army for Guo Jia. In the third month he besieges Zhang Xiu at Rang and, withdrawing, ambushes the pursuers at Anzhong. In the ninth he marches on Lü Bu and floods Xiapi. When Lü Bu, captured, offers to serve him, Cao Cao hesitates; Liu Bei says, “Have you forgotten how he served Ding Yuan and Dong Zhuo?” and Cao Cao has him strangled (Biography of Lü Bu). 正月设立军师祭酒，郭嘉任之。三月围张绣于穰城，撤退时在安众设奇兵大破追兵。九月东征吕布，围下邳，引沂、泗二水灌城。吕布被擒后请求效力，曹操面有犹豫，刘备说：“明公不见布之事丁建阳及董太师乎！”曹操遂下令缢杀吕布（《吕布传》）。 He sends Liu Bei to intercept Yuan Shu and regrets it at once. In the eleventh month Zhang Xiu, on Jia Xu’s advice, comes over to him; Cao Cao ignores the death of his son and makes Zhang Xiu a marquis. In the twelfth month he moves to Guandu. 他派刘备截击袁术，随即后悔。十一月，张绣听从贾诩的建议率众来降，他不计杀子之仇，封张绣为列侯。十二月进驻官渡。 Ignoring the threat from Yuan Shao, he marches east against Liu Bei himself. In the fourth month he relieves Baima with Xun You’s feint, where Guan Yu kills Yan Liang, and kills Wen Chou at Yanjin after baiting him with the baggage train. At Guandu, short of men and food, he writes to Xun Yu proposing to fall back on Xu; Xun Yu tells him to hold. In the tenth month Xu You defects and tells him about Wuchao. He leads 5,000 men there by night, burns all the grain, kills Chunyu Qiong, and Zhang He and Gao Lan come over to him. 他不顾袁绍在背后威胁，亲率大军东征刘备。四月用荀攸声东击西之计解白马之围，关羽斩颜良；又在延津以辎重为饵，斩文丑。官渡相持中他兵少粮缺，写信给荀彧想退回许县，荀彧回信劝他坚持。十月许攸来投，献计袭乌巢，他亲率步骑五千夜袭，烧尽袁军粮草，斩淳于琼，张郃、高览来降。 In the fourth month he beats Yuan Shao again at Cangting. In the ninth he marches against Liu Bei in Runan, who flees to Jing. 四月在仓亭再败袁绍。九月南征汝南的刘备，刘备逃往荆州。 In the first month he returns to Qiao and finds the people of his home district almost all dead. He orders heirs to be found for soldiers who died without children, and gives them land, oxen and schools. Yuan Shao dies in the fifth month; in the ninth he marches north against Yuan Tan and Yuan Shang. 正月回到故乡谯县，见“旧土人民，死丧略尽”，下令为无后的阵亡将士寻立继嗣，授田、给耕牛、设学校教育其子弟。五月袁绍去世，九月他北上进攻袁谭、袁尚。 He takes Liyang in the third month and returns to Xu in the fifth. In the seventh he orders schools set up in every commandery. In the eighth, on Guo Jia’s advice, he feints south against Liu Biao while the Yuan brothers fight; when the beaten Yuan Tan asks for help, he turns north. 三月攻破黎阳，五月回许。七月下令各郡国修文学、设校官。八月听从郭嘉之计，佯攻刘表，坐等二袁内讧；袁谭兵败求援，他回师北上。 After Ye falls he goes to Yuan Shao’s tomb and weeps, comforts Yuan Shao’s widow and returns her family’s treasures. In the ninth month he excuses the north from that year’s taxes and cracks down on landlords swallowing up land. Made governor of Ji, he gives up Yan; Ye is now his capital. 攻下邺城后，他亲往袁绍墓前祭奠，痛哭流涕，抚慰袁绍的妻子，归还其家人财物。九月下令河北免除当年租赋，并严惩豪强兼并。献帝任命他兼领冀州牧，他辞去兖州牧，从此以邺城为根据地。 He kills Yuan Tan at Nanpi in the first month, pardons all who had sided with the Yuan, and bans private vendettas and lavish burials. 正月在南皮斩袁谭。他下令与袁氏同恶者一律既往不咎，禁止私自复仇和厚葬。 He marches against Gao Gan and besieges Huguan, which surrenders in the third month. In the eighth he campaigns against the pirate Guan Cheng on the coast. 亲征高干，三月围攻壶关，壶关投降，并州平定。八月东征海贼管承。 In the second month he makes over twenty of his followers marquises. In the seventh, with the coast road flooded, he takes Tian Chou’s advice and goes through Lulong Pass, “cutting through mountains and filling valleys for over five hundred li.” In the eighth he routs the Wuhuan at White Wolf Mountain. On the way back Guo Jia, the adviser he relied on most, dies aged 38; Cao Cao is grief-stricken. 二月大封功臣二十余人为列侯。七月大雨，沿海道路不通，他采纳田畴的建议改走卢龙塞，“堑山堙谷五百余里”。八月在白狼山大破乌桓。归途中，最倚重的谋士郭嘉病逝，年三十八，曹操十分悲痛。 In the first month he digs the Xuanwu Pool at Ye to train sailors. In the sixth he becomes Chancellor. Marching south, he takes 5,000 light cavalry three hundred li in a day and a night to catch and rout Liu Bei at Changban (Biography of Liu Bei). From Jiangling he writes to Sun Quan: “I now have a navy of 800,000, and will meet you for a hunt in Wu” (Pei, quoting the Jiangbiao Zhuan). In the twelfth month he is beaten at Red Cliffs, plague breaks out, and he leads the army north. 正月在邺城开凿玄武池操练水军。六月出任丞相。南征时他率五千轻骑一日一夜行三百余里，在当阳长坂追上并击溃刘备（《先主传》）。进占江陵后致书孙权：“今治水军八十万众，方与将军会猎于吴。”（裴注引《江表传》）十二月在赤壁失利，又遇大疫，引军北还。 He builds light boats and trains a navy at Qiao, and in the seventh month moves to Hefei. He orders relief for the families of the dead, appoints officials across Yang province, and starts farm colonies at Quebei. 在谯县造轻舟、练水军，七月进驻合肥。他下令为阵亡将士家属发放抚恤，在扬州设置郡县长吏，开芍陂屯田。 In spring he issues his edict seeking talent: “talent is the only test.” In winter he builds the Bronze Bird Terrace. In the twelfth month he issues an edict reviewing his life (quoted by Pei): “Had there been no me, who knows how many would have called themselves emperor, how many king?” He gives back three of his four fief counties but refuses outright to give up his army. 春天下《求贤令》，主张“唯才是举”。冬天修建铜雀台。十二月下《让县自明本志令》（见裴注引《魏武故事》），回顾自己的一生，说：“设使国家无有孤，不知当几人称帝，几人称王。”他让还阳夏、柘、苦三县封地，但明确拒绝交出兵权。 Crossing the Yellow River north of Tong Pass, he is attacked by Ma Chao with over ten thousand men; the colonel Ding Fei lets loose cattle and horses, the enemy scramble for them, and he gets across (Xu Chu’s biography says Xu Chu got him onto a boat and shielded him from arrows with a saddle). Using Jia Xu’s plan to “divide them,” he chats with Han Sui in front of the armies and then sends him a letter full of erasures, so that Ma Chao suspects Han Sui, and then wins the decisive battle. He leaves Xiahou Yuan in charge of Chang’an. 潼关北渡黄河时，马超率万余步骑突袭，校尉丁斐放出牛马引敌兵争抢，他才得以渡河（《许褚传》记许褚扶他上船，举马鞍为他遮挡箭矢）。他采纳贾诩“离之而已”的计策，阵前与韩遂叙旧，又送去涂改过的书信，使马超疑心韩遂，然后决战大胜。十月北征杨秋，十二月留夏侯渊屯长安。 Back at Ye in the first month, he is granted the honours of Xiao He: announced without his name, no need to hurry at court, sword and shoes in the hall. Xun Yu opposes making him Duke of Wei, and “from then on Cao Cao was uneasy with him.” Marching against Sun Quan in the tenth month, he asks for Xun Yu to come to Qiao to reward the troops, then keeps him with the army. Xun Yu falls ill at Shouchun and dies: of worry, says Chen Shou; of poison, after receiving an empty food box, says a source quoted by Pei. 正月回邺，被准许“赞拜不名，入朝不趋，剑履上殿，如萧何故事”。荀彧反对为他晋爵魏公，“太祖由是心不能平”。十月东征孙权时，他上表请荀彧到谯县劳军，随即把荀彧留在军中。荀彧病留寿春去世，陈寿说是“以忧薨”，裴注引《魏氏春秋》说是收到空食盒后服毒自尽。 In the first month he attacks Ruxu and takes Sun Quan’s camp on the north bank. According to a source quoted by Pei, seeing the order of Sun Quan’s fleet and troops he sighs: “If you have a son, he should be like Sun Zhongmou; Liu Biao’s sons were pigs and dogs.” In the fifth month he is made Duke of Wei with the Nine Bestowments. 正月进攻濡须口，攻破孙权的江西营寨。裴注引《吴历》，他见孙权舟船器仗军伍整肃，叹道：“生子当如孙仲谋，刘景升儿子若豚犬耳！”五月受封魏公，加九锡。 In the first month he performs the ritual ploughing. He marches against Sun Quan in the seventh month without result. In the eleventh Empress Fu is exposed, deposed and dies. 正月始耕籍田。七月东征孙权，无功而还。十一月伏皇后事发，皇后被废而死。 He marches west in the third month, breaks through Yangping Pass in the seventh and enters Nanzheng. Liu Ye and others urge him to press on into Shu; he declines (Biography of Liu Ye). Zhang Lu surrenders in the eleventh month and he and his five sons are made marquises. He leaves Xiahou Yuan to hold Hanzhong. 三月西征张鲁，七月攻破阳平关，进入南郑。刘晔等人劝他乘胜取蜀，他没有采纳（《刘晔传》）。十一月张鲁来降，他封张鲁及五子为列侯。十二月留夏侯渊镇守汉中，回军。 He becomes King of Wei in the fifth month. Cui Yan is ordered to kill himself, and Mao Jie, who protests, is dismissed (Biographies of Cui Yan and Mao Jie). In the tenth month he marches on Sun Quan again. 五月进爵魏王。崔琰被赐死，为崔琰鸣不平的毛玠也被罢官（《崔琰传》《毛玠传》）。十月再征孙权。 In the second month he attacks Ruxu and Sun Quan offers submission; the two renew a marriage pact. In the fourth month he is allowed the emperor’s banners, and in the tenth the emperor’s crown and carriage; he names Cao Pi Crown Prince. 二月进攻濡须，孙权请降，双方重修婚约。四月获准设天子旌旗，出入称警跸；十月冕十二旒、乘金根车，立曹丕为魏太子。 In the sixth month he orders his tomb prepared: “use the high ground as its base; raise no mound and plant no trees.” In the seventh he marches west against Liu Bei, reaching Chang’an in the ninth. 六月下令预作寿陵，要求“因高为基，不封不树”。七月西征刘备，九月抵达长安。 In the third month he comes through the Xie valley to Yangping, but Liu Bei holds the heights and he withdraws in the fifth (the “chicken rib” remark is from a source quoted by Pei). His secretary Yang Xiu is executed; Cao Zhi’s biography says it was because Yang Xiu was clever and a nephew of Yuan Shu. He sends Xu Huang to relieve Fancheng and camps at Mopi. When Sun Quan writes offering to attack Guan Yu and urging him to take the throne, he says: “This boy wants to sit me on a stove!” (Pei, quoting the Weilüe). 三月出斜谷至阳平，刘备据险坚守，五月撤军（“鸡肋”之叹见裴注引《九州春秋》）。主簿杨修被杀，《陈思王植传》说是因为杨修有才谋、又是袁术的外甥。他派徐晃救樊城，自己进驻摩陂。孙权上书请求讨伐关羽，并劝他称帝，他说：“是儿欲踞吾著炉火上邪！”（裴注引《魏略》） In the first month he returns to Luoyang, and Sun Quan sends him Guan Yu’s head. On the gengzi day (15 March 220) he dies at Luoyang, aged 66 by the Chinese count. His last orders: “The realm is not yet at peace; the old customs cannot be followed. When the burial is over, put off mourning… dress me in everyday clothes, and bury no gold, jade or treasure with me.” He is buried at the Gao tomb in the second month; in the tenth Cao Pi names him Emperor Wu. 正月回到洛阳，孙权送来关羽的首级。庚子日（公历220年3月15日）他在洛阳去世，年六十六。遗令说：“天下尚未安定，未得遵古也。葬毕，皆除服。……敛以时服，无藏金玉珍宝。”二月葬于高陵，谥号武王，十月被曹丕追尊为武皇帝。 The Wei his son founded holds the north and accepts Sun Quan’s submission. 曹丕建立的魏国稳据北方，并接受孙权称臣。 Wei has stopped every one of Zhuge Liang’s campaigns. 魏国挡住了诸葛亮的全部北伐。 Sima Yi, a man he himself recruited, takes power from the Cao family. 他亲自征辟的司马懿，从曹氏手中夺走了权力。 Sima Yan ends Wei, 46 years after Cao Pi took the throne. 司马炎终结魏国。曹魏自曹丕称帝起延续了四十六年。 Jin finishes the unification he began and lost at Red Cliffs. Storytellers later made him a villain; Chen Shou called him “an extraordinary man, a hero beyond his age.” 他开启、却在赤壁功亏一篑的统一，由晋完成。后世说书人把他写成奸雄，陈寿则称他“非常之人，超世之杰”。"},{"id":"s3","h":"Liu Bei · 刘备","t":"From Zhuo, a descendant of Liu Sheng, Prince Jing of Zhongshan, son of Emperor Jing of Han. His father died young, and he and his mother lived by selling sandals and weaving mats. At fifteen he studied under Lu Zhi alongside Gongsun Zan. 涿郡涿县人，汉景帝之子中山靖王刘胜之后。少年丧父，与母亲以贩履织席为业。十五岁时与公孙瓒一同师事卢植。 Funded by the horse traders Zhang Shiping and Su Shuang, he raises a band with Guan Yu and Zhang Fei against the Yellow Turbans and is made commandant of Anxi county. When an inspector refuses to see him, he ties the man up, gives him two hundred strokes, hangs his seal of office round the inspector’s neck and leaves. 得中山大商张世平、苏双资助，与关羽、张飞组织乡勇讨伐黄巾，因功任安喜县尉。督邮来县办事，他求见不得，直入将督邮捆绑，杖打二百，解下官印挂在督邮脖子上，弃官而去。 He serves as assistant magistrate of Xiami, then commandant and magistrate of Gaotang. 先后任下密丞、高唐尉，升任高唐令。 When bandits take Gaotang he joins his old schoolmate Gongsun Zan and becomes chancellor of Pingyuan. 高唐被盗贼攻破，他投奔同窗公孙瓒，因功任平原相。 When Cao Cao attacks Xu he comes with Tian Kai to help Tao Qian, who makes him inspector of Yu and stations him at Xiaopei. 曹操攻徐州，他与田楷援救陶谦，陶谦表他为豫州刺史，屯驻小沛。 Dying, Tao Qian says that only Liu Bei can hold the province. After refusing several times, he takes over Xu on the urging of Chen Deng and Kong Rong. 陶谦病重，临终嘱托“非刘备不能安此州”。他一再推辞，在陈登、孔融劝说下接管徐州。 While he holds off Yuan Shu at Xuyi and Huaiyin, Lü Bu seizes Xiapi and his wife and children. Forced to make peace and then beaten again, he goes over to Cao Cao. 他与袁术在盱眙、淮阴相持，吕布趁机袭取下邳，俘获其妻子。他被迫向吕布求和，后又被吕布击败，投奔曹操。 He marches with Cao Cao against Lü Bu, and when Lü Bu begs to serve Cao Cao, reminds him what happened to Ding Yuan and Dong Zhuo. 随曹操东征吕布。吕布被擒后请求效力，他提醒曹操想想丁原、董卓的下场。 Cao Cao tells him calmly: “The only heroes in the world are you and I.” He drops his chopsticks (Biography of Liu Bei). He secretly joins Dong Cheng’s plot under the “girdle edict.” Sent to stop Yuan Shu, he kills Che Zhou at Xiapi and holds Xu against Cao Cao. 曹操从容对他说：“今天下英雄，唯使君与操耳。”他正在吃饭，惊得筷子掉在地上（《先主传》）。他与董承秘密接受“衣带诏”，谋诛曹操。奉命截击袁术后，他在下邳杀车胄，据徐州反曹。 Cao Cao marches on him in person; beaten, he abandons his family and flees to Yuan Shao, who later sends him to rouse the Yellow Turban leader Liu Pi in Runan. 曹操亲征，他大败，弃妻子投奔袁绍；后被派往汝南联络黄巾刘辟等。 With Cao Cao coming south, he flees to Liu Biao in Jing and is stationed at Xinye. 曹操南征，他投奔荆州刘表，屯驻新野。 Only on his third visit to Longzhong does he see Zhuge Liang, and they grow close. When Guan Yu and Zhang Fei grumble, he says: “Having Kongming is like a fish having water.” 他三次前往隆中拜访诸葛亮才得相见，“由是与亮情好日密”。关羽、张飞不悦，他说：“孤之有孔明，犹鱼之有水也。” Liu Cong surrenders; he retreats south with over a hundred thousand civilians, covering barely ten li a day, and is caught and routed at Changban. He flees, leaving his family; Zhao Yun saves his son Liu Shan, and Zhang Fei holds the bridge. Falling back to Xiakou, he sends Zhuge Liang to Sun Quan and together they beat Cao Cao at Red Cliffs. 曹操大军南下，刘琮投降，他率十余万百姓南撤，日行十余里，在当阳长坂被曹操追上击溃，弃妻子逃走，赵云保护幼子刘禅，张飞据水断桥。他退到夏口，派诸葛亮联吴，与孙权共同在赤壁击败曹操。 He names Liu Qi inspector of Jing and takes the four southern commanderies. When Liu Qi dies he is acclaimed governor of Jing, at Gong’an. Sun Quan gives him his sister in marriage. 表刘琦为荆州刺史，南征四郡。刘琦病死后，众人推他为荆州牧，治公安。孙权把妹妹嫁给他。 He goes to Sun Quan at Jingkou to ask for command of Jing. Zhou Yu urges Sun Quan to keep him there as a hostage; Sun Quan declines. 他亲往京口见孙权，请求“都督荆州”。周瑜上疏建议软禁刘备，孙权没有采纳。 Liu Zhang sends Fa Zheng to invite him into Yi against Zhang Lu. Leaving Zhuge Liang and Guan Yu to hold Jing, he goes in with tens of thousands of men; Liu Zhang meets him at Fu and they feast for over a hundred days. 刘璋派法正迎他入蜀抵御张鲁，他留诸葛亮、关羽守荆州，率数万人入益州，刘璋亲到涪城相迎，欢宴百余日。 For a year at Jiameng he builds goodwill. At the end of 212 he asks Liu Zhang for troops and supplies, ostensibly to help Jing; refused, he kills Liu Zhang’s officers Yang Huai and Gao Pei and turns south against Chengdu. 他驻扎葭萌一年，广树恩德收买人心。年底借口回援荆州向刘璋要兵要粮，被拒后斩杀刘璋部将杨怀、高沛，回军南攻成都。 He besieges Luo for nearly a year; his adviser Pang Tong is killed by an arrow, aged 36. 围攻雒城近一年，谋士庞统中流矢身亡，年三十六。 He takes Luo in summer and, joined by Zhuge Liang, Zhang Fei and Zhao Yun, surrounds Chengdu. Ma Chao comes over to him, the city panics, and Liu Zhang surrenders. He becomes governor of Yi. 夏天攻下雒城，与诸葛亮、张飞、赵云会师围成都。马超来投，城中震恐，刘璋出降。他自领益州牧。 Sun Quan demands Jing back and sends Lü Meng to seize three commanderies. He comes down to Gong’an with 50,000 men, but on hearing that Cao Cao has taken Hanzhong, makes peace and divides Jing along the Xiang River. 孙权索还荆州，派吕蒙夺取长沙等三郡。他率五万人东下公安，听说曹操取了汉中，才与孙权以湘水为界讲和。 He advances into Hanzhong and faces Xiahou Yuan and Zhang He at Yangping. 进兵汉中，与夏侯渊、张郃在阳平关相持。 In the first month Huang Zhong kills Xiahou Yuan at Dingjun Mountain; Cao Cao comes in person, Liu Bei holds the heights and refuses battle, and Cao Cao leaves. In the seventh month he proclaims himself King of Hanzhong at Mianyang. At the year’s end Guan Yu is killed and Jing is lost. 正月黄忠在定军山斩杀夏侯渊；曹操亲来争夺，刘备据险不战，曹操退兵。七月他在沔阳自称汉中王。年底关羽兵败身死，荆州尽失。 Hearing a false rumour that Emperor Xian has been murdered, he holds mourning for him. 传闻汉献帝已被曹丕所害，他为献帝发丧，追谥孝愍皇帝。 He takes the throne at Chengdu in the fourth month and marches against Wu in the seventh; Zhang Fei is murdered just before. In 222 he faces Lu Xun at Yiling for half a year, is burned out of his camps, and retreats to Baidi. 四月在成都称帝。七月东征孙权，出征前张飞被部将杀害。222年他在夷陵与陆逊相持半年，被火攻大败，退回白帝城。 He dies at Baidi in the fourth month. He entrusts his son to Zhuge Liang, and leaves him a last edict: “Do not do evil because it is small; do not neglect good because it is small” (quoted by Pei from Zhuge Liang’s collected works). 四月病逝于白帝城永安宫，年六十三（虚岁）。临终托孤于诸葛亮，又留遗诏告诫刘禅：“勿以恶小而为之，勿以善小而不为。”（裴注引《诸葛亮集》） Zhuge Liang governs for his son, pacifies the south and prepares to march north. 诸葛亮辅佐刘禅，平定南中，准备北伐。 Liu Shan surrenders to Deng Ai and lives out his life in Luoyang as Duke of Anle. The state Liu Bei founded has lasted 43 years. 刘禅向邓艾投降，后在洛阳受封安乐公。刘备建立的蜀汉延续了四十三年。 A thousand years later the novel made him the virtuous heir of Han. Chen Shou judged him “broad-minded and generous, a good judge of men,” but “in strategy and resource not the equal of Cao Cao.” 一千多年后，《三国演义》把他塑造成仁德的汉室正统；陈寿则评价他“弘毅宽厚，知人待士”，但“机权干略，不逮魏武”。"},{"id":"s3","h":"Sun Quan · 孙权","t":"Born in 182 at Xiapi, where his father Sun Jian was an assistant magistrate. Sun Jian is now fighting the Yellow Turbans under Zhu Jun and making his name. 182年生于下邳，当时父亲孙坚任下邳丞。孙坚此时随朱儁讨伐黄巾，以勇猛闻名。 His father joins the war against Dong Zhuo, and his elder brother Sun Ce settles the family at Shu in Lujiang, where Sun Ce befriends Zhou Yu. 父亲孙坚随关东联军讨伐董卓，兄长孙策把家安置在庐江舒县，与周瑜结为好友。 Sun Jian beats Dong Zhuo at Yangren and enters Luoyang, then, sent by Yuan Shu against Liu Biao, is ambushed and killed near Xiangyang. Sun Ce is seventeen. 孙坚在阳人大破董卓军，攻入洛阳，随后奉袁术之命攻打刘表，在襄阳附近中伏身亡。孙策年仅十七。 Sun Ce borrows troops from Yuan Shu and conquers the south-east; Sun Quan goes with him. 兄长孙策从袁术处借兵渡江，横扫江东，孙权随兄征战。 At fifteen he is made head of Yangxian county. 十五岁时被任命为阳羡县长。 Sun Ce is killed by retainers of Xu Gong. Dying, he tells his brother: “In leading the armies of the south to decide things on the battlefield, you are not my equal; in finding able men and getting the best from them to hold the south, I am not yours.” Sun Quan, 18, succeeds him with Zhang Zhao and Zhou Yu at his side. 孙策被许贡门客刺杀，临终对他说：“举江东之众，决机于两陈之间，与天下争衡，卿不如我；举贤任能，各尽其心，以保江东，我不如卿。”十八岁的孙权继位，张昭、周瑜辅佐。 He attacks Huang Zu, the man whose troops killed his father, and carries off his people. 再次征讨杀父仇人黄祖，掳其百姓而还。 In spring he takes Jiangxia and kills Huang Zu. When Cao Cao’s threatening letter arrives, Zhang Zhao and others want to submit; Lu Su and Zhou Yu argue for war. According to a source quoted by Pei, he cuts his desk with his sword: “Anyone who speaks again of welcoming Cao Cao will end like this desk!” He sends Zhou Yu and Cheng Pu with 30,000 men, and they win at Red Cliffs. 春天攻破江夏，斩黄祖。曹操来书威胁，张昭等人主张迎降，鲁肃、周瑜力主抗战。裴注引《江表传》，孙权拔刀砍案说：“诸将吏敢复有言当迎操者，与此案同！”他派周瑜、程普率三万人西上，在赤壁击败曹操。 He besieges Hefei for over a hundred days without taking it. 亲率大军围攻合肥百余日，未能攻克。 Zhou Yu dies and Lu Su takes over. He sends Bu Zhi to govern Jiao, and Shi Xie’s family submits. 周瑜病逝，鲁肃接任。他派步骘为交州刺史，士燮兄弟归附。 He moves his seat to Moling, builds the Stone Fort and renames the city Jianye. 将治所迁到秣陵，修筑石头城，改名建业。 At Ruxu he holds off Cao Cao for a month until Cao Cao withdraws. 在濡须抵挡曹操大军月余，曹操退兵。 After Lü Meng takes three commanderies he makes peace with Liu Bei, then besieges Hefei with 100,000 men. As he withdraws, Zhang Liao’s charge nearly captures him at Xiaoyao Ford. 派吕蒙夺取长沙三郡后与刘备讲和。随即亲率十万人围攻合肥，撤退时被张辽突袭，在逍遥津几乎被擒。 When Cao Cao attacks Ruxu again he sends Xu Xiang to offer submission and the two houses renew a marriage pact. Lu Su dies and Lü Meng succeeds him. 曹操再攻濡须，他派徐详请降，两家重修婚约。鲁肃去世，吕蒙接任。 He secretly offers Cao Cao to attack Guan Yu, and Lü Meng’s men, dressed as merchants, take Jiangling and Gong’an while Lu Xun takes Yidu. Guan Yu and his son Guan Ping are captured at Linju and executed. Lü Meng dies soon after. 他暗中向曹操表示愿讨伐关羽，派吕蒙“白衣摇橹”袭取江陵、公安，又派陆逊攻取宜都。关羽在临沮被擒，与其子关平一同被杀。不久吕蒙病逝。 He moves his capital to E and renames it Wuchang, declares himself Wei’s vassal and is made King of Wu. In 222 Lu Xun destroys Liu Bei at Yiling, and he breaks with Wei. 迁都鄂县，改名武昌。向魏称臣，受封吴王。222年陆逊在夷陵大破刘备；同年他与魏决裂，改元黄武。 He becomes emperor at Wuchang in the fourth month and moves to Jianye in the autumn, agreeing with Shu how to divide the world between them. 四月在武昌称帝，秋天迁都建业，与蜀汉约定中分天下。 In concert with Zhuge Liang he attacks the new fort at Hefei with 100,000 men, without success. 配合诸葛亮北伐，亲率十万人攻打合肥新城，无功而返。 Growing suspicious in old age, he lets the inspector Lü Yi persecute officials, then has him executed and apologises to his ministers. 他晚年猜忌日深，宠信校事吕壹，大臣多受迫害，后来他杀吕壹并向群臣致歉。 The struggle between his heir Sun He and Sun Ba, Prince of Lu, splits the court; Lu Xun dies in 245 after being rebuked. In 250 he deposes Sun He, orders Sun Ba to kill himself and names his youngest son Sun Liang. He dies in the fourth month of 252. 太子孙和与鲁王孙霸争储（“二宫之争”），朝臣分裂，陆逊因受斥责忧愤而死（245年）。250年他废孙和、赐死孙霸，立幼子孙亮。252年四月去世，年七十一（虚岁）。 Wu sends troops to help Shu, too late. 吴国在他的子孙治下继续存在，此时出兵救蜀，未及而蜀已亡。 His grandson Sun Hao surrenders; Wu ends 51 years after he took the throne. The south he built up became the heartland of the Six Dynasties. 其孙孙皓投降，吴国自229年称帝起立国五十一年而亡。他开发的江南日后成为六朝的核心。"},{"id":"s3","h":"Zhuge Liang · 诸葛亮","t":"Born in 181 at Yangdu, Langya. His father Zhuge Gui, an assistant administrator of Taishan, died when he was a child. 181年生于琅琊阳都，父亲诸葛珪曾任泰山郡丞，在他幼年时去世。 He and his siblings are raised by their uncle Zhuge Xuan. 他和兄弟姐妹由叔父诸葛玄抚养。 Cao Cao ravages Xu. His uncle, appointed Administrator of Yuzhang by Yuan Shu, takes him and his younger brother south; when the court sends a rival appointee, the uncle goes on to Liu Biao in Jing. 曹操屠戮徐州。叔父诸葛玄被袁术任命为豫章太守，带他和弟弟诸葛均南下赴任；后来朝廷另派太守，诸葛玄转投荆州刘表。 After his uncle dies he farms at Longzhong, twenty li west of Xiangyang, fond of singing the Liangfu ballad, “always comparing himself to Guan Zhong and Yue Yi, though few agreed.” 叔父去世后，他在襄阳城西二十里的隆中躬耕，好为《梁父吟》，“每自比于管仲、乐毅，时人莫之许也”。 Only a few friends such as Xu Shu and Cui Zhouping take him seriously. According to a source quoted by Pei, the recluse Pang Degong calls him the “sleeping dragon.” 只有徐庶、崔州平等少数好友认可他。裴注引《襄阳记》说，名士庞德公称他为“卧龙”。 Liu Bei calls three times. Zhuge Liang lays out his plan: Cao Cao “already has a million men and holds the emperor; you cannot fight him head-on.” Sun Quan’s family has held the south for three generations; “he can be an ally, not a target.” Take Jing and Yi first, make friends with Sun Quan, and when the world shifts, march north on two fronts. 刘备三顾茅庐。他提出《隆中对》：曹操“已拥百万之众，挟天子而令诸侯，此诚不可与争锋”；孙权据有江东已历三世，“可以为援而不可图”；应先取荆、益二州，结好孙权，待天下有变，再两路北伐。 After Changban he volunteers to go to Sun Quan and persuades him at Chaisang to fight Cao Cao. 长坂败后，他自请出使江东，在柴桑说服孙权联合抗曹。 As General of the Household Advising the Army he is stationed at Linzheng, running Lingling, Guiyang and Changsha and collecting taxes for the army. 任军师中郎将，驻临烝，督零陵、桂阳、长沙三郡，征收赋税以充军需。 When Liu Bei goes west he stays behind with Guan Yu to hold Jing. 刘备入蜀，他与关羽留守荆州。 With Zhang Fei and Zhao Yun he leads an army up the Yangtze, takes the counties on the way and joins Liu Bei before Chengdu. Afterwards, as General Directing the Army, he runs Liu Bei’s headquarters and keeps Chengdu supplied with “enough food and enough soldiers” whenever Liu Bei is away. 与张飞、赵云率军溯江而上，分定郡县，与刘备会师围成都。成都平定后任军师将军，署左将军府事，刘备出征时他镇守成都，“足食足兵”。 When Liu Bei becomes emperor he is made Chancellor with charge of the secretariat. 刘备称帝，任丞相、录尚书事。 He hurries to Baidi to receive Liu Bei’s charge and replies in tears: “I will give my all, with loyalty and integrity, until death.” From then on every decision in Shu, great or small, is his. He sends Deng Zhi to Wu and restores the alliance. 赶到白帝城接受托孤，涕泣说：“臣敢竭股肱之力，效忠贞之节，继之以死！”此后开府治事，蜀汉政事无论大小都由他决断。他派邓芝出使东吴，恢复了联盟。 In 225 he campaigns in the south and has it pacified by autumn, its resources now enriching the state. In 227 he submits his memorial and moves to Hanzhong. In spring 228 he marches out by Qishan and three commanderies go over to him, but his vanguard commander Ma Su disobeys orders, camps on a hill away from water and is routed by Zhang He at Jieting. He has Ma Su executed in tears and asks to be demoted three grades. In winter he besieges Chencang and withdraws when food runs out. 225年春亲征南中，秋天平定，“军资所出，国以富饶”。227年上《出师表》，率军进驻汉中。228年春出祁山，天水、南安、安定三郡响应，但先锋马谡在街亭违背调度、舍水上山，被张郃大败。他挥泪斩马谡，上疏自贬三等。冬天出散关围陈仓，因粮尽退兵。 He sends Chen Shi to take Wudu and Yinping and is restored as Chancellor. When Sun Quan takes the throne he overrides objections and sends congratulations. 派陈式攻取武都、阴平二郡，官复丞相。孙权称帝后，他力排众议，遣使祝贺。 In 231 he marches by Qishan again, moving grain on wooden oxen. When supplies run short, Li Yan, in charge of the grain, sends word calling him back, then feigns surprise that he has withdrawn; Zhuge Liang exposes him and has him stripped of office. On the way back his archers kill the pursuing Wei general Zhang He. In spring 234 he leads 100,000 men out by the Xie valley, with gliding horses for supply, and sets soldiers to farm on the Wuzhang Plains for a long campaign. Sima Yi will not come out; Zhuge Liang sends him women’s clothes to goad him (Pei’s notes). In the eighth month he dies in camp. He asks to be buried on Dingjun Mountain in a grave just big enough for the coffin, in everyday clothes, with nothing else. 231年再出祁山，以木牛运粮。负责供粮的李严因粮草不继，派人传旨召他回军，等他退兵后又假装吃惊；诸葛亮查明后上表将李严废为平民。退兵途中，蜀军射杀了追击的魏将张郃。234年春率十万大军出斜谷，以流马运粮，在五丈原分兵屯田，作长久之计。司马懿坚守不出，他送去妇人衣服相激（见裴注）。八月病逝于军中，年五十四（虚岁）。遗命葬于定军山，“因山为坟，冢足容棺，敛以时服，不须器物”。 Jiang Wan and then Fei Yi lead Shu, keeping his policies but pausing the great campaigns. 蒋琬、费祎相继主政，延续他的方针，暂停大规模北伐。 His son Zhuge Zhan and grandson Zhuge Shang die fighting Deng Ai at Mianzhu. 他的儿子诸葛瞻、孙子诸葛尚在绵竹抵抗邓艾，双双战死。 Chen Shou called him a great statesman, the equal of the ancient ministers, but said generalship was not his strength. The novel made him a near-magician. 陈寿评他“识治之良才，管、萧之亚匹”，但“将略非其所长”；小说则把他写成呼风唤雨的半仙。"},{"id":"s3","h":"Guan Yu · 关羽","t":"From Xie county in Hedong, he fled to Zhuo for reasons the history does not give and joined Liu Bei. His birth year is not recorded. 河东解县人，因故逃亡到涿郡，加入刘备的队伍。史书没有记载他的生年。 When Liu Bei becomes chancellor of Pingyuan, he and Zhang Fei command separate detachments. The history says the three “slept in one bed, close as brothers,” while in public he and Zhang Fei stood beside Liu Bei all day. 刘备任平原相，以他和张飞为别部司马，分统部曲。史书说三人“寝则同床，恩若兄弟”，而在大庭广众之中，他和张飞“侍立终日”。 He follows Liu Bei to Xu. 随刘备入主徐州。 He besieges Xiapi with Liu Bei and Cao Cao. A source quoted by Pei says he asked Cao Cao for the wife of Qin Yilu once the city fell, and Cao Cao, seeing how beautiful she was, took her himself. 随刘备、曹操围攻下邳。裴注引《蜀记》说，他请求曹操在城破后把秦宜禄的妻子许给他，曹操见其貌美，自己纳了她。 After Liu Bei kills Che Zhou, he is left to hold Xiapi as acting administrator. 刘备袭杀车胄后，让他镇守下邳，代理太守。 When Cao Cao attacks, he is captured and made a general, treated with great honour. At Baima he spots Yan Liang’s parasol, rides into the midst of ten thousand men, stabs him and comes back with his head; he is made Marquis of Hanshou. Then he seals up all Cao Cao’s gifts, leaves a letter of farewell and goes back to Liu Bei. Cao Cao says: “Each man serves his own master. Do not pursue him.” 曹操东征，他兵败投降，被拜为偏将军，“礼之甚厚”。白马之战，他望见颜良的麾盖，策马冲入万军之中刺杀颜良、斩其首而还，被封为汉寿亭侯。此后他尽封曹操所赐，留书告辞，回到刘备身边。曹操说：“彼各为其主，勿追也。” He goes with Liu Bei to Liu Biao. 随刘备依附刘表。 He leads several hundred boats down the Han, meets the routed Liu Bei at Hanjin and takes him to Xiakou. 刘备南撤时，他率数百艘战船沿汉水而下，在汉津接应溃败的刘备，一同到达夏口。 As Administrator of Xiangyang and General Who Sweeps Away Bandits he holds the north bank of the Yangtze. 任襄阳太守、荡寇将军，驻守长江以北。 With Liu Bei in Yi, he runs Jing. When Ma Chao joins them he writes to ask Zhuge Liang how Ma Chao compares; Zhuge Liang replies that Ma Chao is Zhang Fei’s equal “but not the match of the Beard, who is beyond compare,” which delights him. An old arrow wound having poisoned his arm, he has a doctor cut it open and scrape the bone while he dines with his officers, “blood running into the dishes, carving meat and drinking, laughing and talking as usual.” 刘备入蜀后他“董督荆州事”。马超来降，他写信问诸葛亮马超的才能可与谁相比，诸葛亮回信说马超可与张飞并驾齐驱，“犹未及髯之绝伦逸群也”，他看了十分高兴。他曾中流矢，臂骨中毒，请医生刮骨去毒，“时羽适请诸将饮食相对，臂血流离，盈于盘器，而羽割炙引酒，言笑自若”。 With Sun and Liu at odds over Jing, he faces Lu Su at Yiyang. They meet with their troops a hundred paces back and the officers carrying only swords, and Lu Su rebukes Liu Bei for not returning the land (Biography of Lu Su). 孙刘争夺荆州，他与鲁肃在益阳对峙。鲁肃邀他相见，双方兵马停在百步之外，诸将只带单刀赴会，鲁肃当面责备刘备借地不还（《鲁肃传》）。 Liu Bei, now King of Hanzhong, makes him General of the Van with full powers. In the seventh month he attacks Fancheng; in the eighth heavy rain floods the Han, Yu Jin’s seven armies are drowned, Yu Jin surrenders and Pang De is executed. “His fame shook the realm,” and Cao Cao even considered moving the capital. But he was on bad terms with Mi Fang at Jiangling and Shi Ren at Gong’an, and when Lü Meng comes both surrender. His army melts away; he falls back on Maicheng and in the twelfth month is captured at Linju by the Wu officer Ma Zhong and executed with his son Guan Ping. 刘备称汉中王，拜他为前将军，假节钺。七月他北攻樊城，八月大雨，汉水泛滥，于禁七军被淹，于禁投降，庞德被斩，“羽威震华夏”，曹操一度商议迁都避其锋芒。但他与镇守江陵的糜芳、公安的士仁不和，吕蒙袭取二城时二人投降。他军心瓦解，退守麦城，十二月在临沮被吴将马忠擒获，与其子关平一同被杀。 Liu Bei goes to war with Wu to avenge him and is crushed at Yiling. 刘备为他复仇，起兵伐吴，在夷陵大败。 In 260 Liu Shan gave him the posthumous title Marquis Zhuangmou. When Chengdu falls, according to a source quoted by Pei, Pang De’s son Pang Hui wipes out the Guan family. 260年刘禅追谥他为壮缪侯。成都陷落时，据裴注引《蜀记》，庞德之子庞会诛灭了关氏家族。 Later dynasties kept raising his titles until he became the god Guan Di, still worshipped across the Chinese world for loyalty and wealth. Chen Shou judged him “unbending and proud,” flaws that brought him down. 此后历代不断加封，明清时被尊为“关圣帝君”，至今在华人世界作为忠义与财神受到供奉。陈寿评他“刚而自矜”，以短取败。"},{"id":"s3","h":"Sima Yi · 司马懿","t":"Born in 179 in Wen county, Henei, to a family of officials; his father Sima Fang was governor of the capital region, and he and his seven brothers were known as the “eight Da of the Sima.” 179年生于河内温县一个世代为官的家族，父亲司马防曾任京兆尹，兄弟八人皆有才名，号称“司马八达”。 According to the Book of Jin, when Cao Cao summoned him he did not want to serve and pretended to be paralysed; when Cao Cao sent someone to check by night, he lay perfectly still. 据《晋书·宣帝纪》，曹操任司空时征辟他，他不愿屈节，假称患风痹，卧床不起。曹操派人夜里去刺探，他坚卧不动。 When Cao Cao becomes Chancellor he summons him again with the warning “if he dawdles, arrest him.” He takes the post of literary clerk and is placed in Cao Pi’s circle. 曹操任丞相后再次征召，并说“若复盘桓，便收之”，他只好就任文学掾，随后被安排与曹丕交游。 With Cao Cao against Zhang Lu, he urges him to press on into Shu. Cao Cao replies: “Men are never satisfied. Having got Longyou, now you want Shu!” (Book of Jin). 随曹操征张鲁，劝他乘胜取蜀，曹操说：“人苦无足，既得陇右，复欲得蜀！”（《晋书·宣帝纪》） When Cao Pi becomes Crown Prince he joins his household as an adviser and wins his trust. 曹丕被立为太子，他任太子中庶子，深受信任。 With Guan Yu besieging Fancheng and Cao Cao thinking of moving the capital, he and Jiang Ji argue against it and suggest offering Sun Quan land south of the Yangtze to attack Guan Yu from behind. 关羽围樊城，曹操想迁都避锋，他与蒋济劝阻，建议许诺割江南之地给孙权，让孙权从背后袭击关羽。 When Cao Pi takes the throne he becomes a Master of Writing, then an army supervisor and Palace Assistant Imperial Clerk. 曹丕即位称帝，他任尚书，后转督军、御史中丞。 Dying in 226, Cao Pi names him regent alongside Cao Zhen, Chen Qun and Cao Xiu. Under Cao Rui he commands Jing and Yu from Wan. In 228 Meng Da of Xincheng plans to defect to Shu; without waiting for orders he marches day and night, reaches the city in eight days, and takes it and kills Meng Da in sixteen. 226年曹丕临终，以他和曹真、陈群、曹休为辅政大臣。曹叡即位后他坐镇宛城，督荆、豫二州。228年新城太守孟达谋叛归蜀，他不待奏报，倍道兼行，八日赶到城下，十六日破城斩孟达。 From 231 he takes over from Cao Zhen against Zhuge Liang. At the Wuzhang Plains he will not fight; when Zhuge Liang sends him women’s clothes, he asks permission to attack, and the emperor sends Xin Pi to forbid it, which suits him. After Zhuge Liang dies, he pursues the retreating army, sees it turn with banners and drums, and pulls back, giving rise to the saying “a dead Zhuge put a live Zhongda to flight” (Pei, quoting the Han Jin Chunqiu). 231年起接替曹真抵御诸葛亮。在五丈原他坚守不战，诸葛亮送来妇人衣服相激，他上表请战，明帝派辛毗持节阻止，他借此按兵不动。诸葛亮死后蜀军撤退，他追击时见蜀军回旗鸣鼓，急忙退兵，百姓因此说“死诸葛走生仲达”（裴注引《汉晋春秋》）。 He leads 40,000 men to Liaodong, besieges Xiangping through the summer rains, kills Gongsun Yuan and his son, and massacres over seven thousand men of fifteen and older in the city (Book of Jin). In 239 the dying Cao Rui makes him co-regent with Cao Shuang. 率四万人远征辽东，以霖雨为掩护围困襄平，城破后斩公孙渊父子，并屠杀城中十五岁以上男子七千余人（《晋书·宣帝纪》）。239年曹叡临终，他与曹爽同受遗诏辅政。 For years he has stayed home claiming illness, looking harmless to Cao Shuang. In the first month, with Cao Shuang and his brothers outside the city at the Gaoping tomb, he and his sons Sima Shi and Sima Zhao seize Luoyang, and he swears by the Luo River that Cao Shuang will be spared if he surrenders. Cao Shuang gives up his command and is soon executed with his whole clan. 他多年称病在家，示弱于曹爽。正月趁曹爽兄弟随曹芳出城谒高平陵，与儿子司马师、司马昭发动政变，占据洛阳，指洛水为誓劝曹爽投降。曹爽交出兵权后，不久即被诛灭三族。 Wang Ling plots at Shouchun to make Cao Biao emperor; he marches suddenly against him and Wang Ling kills himself. He dies at Luoyang in the eighth month. 太尉王凌在寿春谋立楚王曹彪，他率军突袭，王凌自杀。八月他在洛阳去世，年七十三（虚岁）。 His second son Sima Zhao directs the conquest of Shu. 他的次子司马昭主持伐蜀，消灭蜀汉。 His grandson Sima Yan takes the throne from Wei, founds Jin, and names him Emperor Xuan. 孙子司马炎代魏称帝，建立晋朝，追尊他为宣皇帝。 Jin conquers Wu and reunites China. The winner of the Three Kingdoms is not Cao, Liu or Sun, but the Sima family. 晋灭吴，统一天下。三国最后的赢家，不是曹、刘、孙，而是司马氏。"},{"id":"s4","h":"The oath in the peach garden · 桃园结义","t":"Liu Bei, Guan Yu and Zhang Fei swear brotherhood among peach blossoms. 刘关张在桃园焚香结拜为兄弟。 No oath is recorded; the history only says the three were as close as brothers. 史书没有结义之事，只说三人情同兄弟。 先主与二人寝则同床，恩若兄弟。而稠人广坐，侍立终日。"},{"id":"s4","h":"Zhang Fei flogs the inspector · 张飞鞭打督邮","t":"Hot-headed Zhang Fei ties up and whips a corrupt inspector. 性急的张飞把贪官督邮绑起来鞭打。 It was Liu Bei himself who did it. 动手的其实是刘备本人。 督邮以公事到县，先主求谒，不通，直入缚督邮，杖二百，解绶系其颈着马枊，弃官亡命。"},{"id":"s4","h":"Guan Yu kills Hua Xiong while the wine is warm · 温酒斩华雄","t":"Guan Yu rides out and returns with Hua Xiong’s head before his cup of wine cools. 关羽出战斩华雄，回来时酒尚温。 Hua Xiong was killed in Sun Jian’s victory at Yangren. 华雄是在孙坚的阳人之战中被斩的。 坚复相收兵，合战于阳人，大破卓军，枭其都督华雄等。"},{"id":"s4","h":"Three heroes fight Lü Bu at Hulao Pass · 三英战吕布","t":"Liu, Guan and Zhang take on Lü Bu together before the coalition armies. 刘关张三人在虎牢关前合战吕布。 No such fight is recorded, and Chen Shou does not place Liu Bei’s band in the coalition at all. 史书没有这场战斗，陈寿也没有记载刘备兄弟参加关东联军。"},{"id":"s4","h":"Diaochan and the chain plot · 连环计与貂蝉","t":"Wang Yun uses the beauty Diaochan to set Lü Bu against Dong Zhuo. 王允用美女貂蝉离间吕布与董卓。 Diaochan is fictional; Lü Bu did have an affair with one of Dong Zhuo’s maids, which made him uneasy. 貂蝉是虚构人物；史书只说吕布与董卓的侍婢私通，心中不安。 卓常使布守中閤，布与卓侍婢私通，恐事发觉，心不自安。"},{"id":"s4","h":"Heroes over plum wine · 青梅煮酒论英雄","t":"Over green plums and wine, Cao Cao names Liu Bei and himself the only heroes; Liu Bei drops his chopsticks and blames the thunder. 曹操煮酒论英雄，说天下英雄唯有你我，刘备失箸，借雷声掩饰。 The remark and the dropped chopsticks are in the history; the plums are the novel’s, and the thunder comes from a later source quoted by Pei. 这句话和刘备失箸都见于正史；“青梅煮酒”是小说的点缀，借雷声掩饰见于裴注引《华阳国志》。 曹公从容谓先主曰：「今天下英雄，唯使君与操耳。本初之徒，不足数也。」先主方食，失匕箸。"},{"id":"s4","h":"The girdle edict · 衣带诏","t":"Emperor Xian bites his finger, writes a secret order in blood to kill Cao Cao, and sews it into a jade belt for Dong Cheng; the plot is betrayed by a servant, and the physician Ji Ping dies trying to poison Cao Cao. 献帝咬破手指写下血诏，缝入玉带赐给董承，密令诛杀曹操；后因家奴告密事泄，太医吉平下毒失败而死。 The plot is real and Liu Bei was in it, but Chen Shou writes that Dong Cheng <i>said</i> he had received an edict hidden in the emperor’s girdle; the blood, the jade belt and Ji Ping’s poisoning are the novel’s. 密谋确有其事，刘备也参与其中；但陈寿只写董承“辞受”衣带中的密诏，即董承自称接受了诏书。血诏、玉带和吉平下毒都是小说的情节。 献帝舅车骑将军董承辞受帝衣带中密诏，当诛曹公。先主未发。"},{"id":"s4","h":"Guan Yu kills Yan Liang and Wen Chou · 斩颜良诛文丑","t":"Guan Yu cuts down both of Yuan Shao’s great generals. 关羽连斩袁绍两员大将颜良、文丑。 Guan Yu did kill Yan Liang. Wen Chou was killed by Cao Cao’s cavalry at Yanjin, with no mention of Guan Yu. 颜良确为关羽所斩；文丑死于延津曹军骑兵之手，史书没有提到关羽。 羽望见良麾盖，策马刺良于万众之中，斩其首还，绍诸将莫能当者，遂解白马围。"},{"id":"s4","h":"Through five passes, six generals slain · 过五关斩六将","t":"Leaving Cao Cao, Guan Yu forces his way through five checkpoints, killing six commanders. 关羽离开曹操时连闯五关，斩杀六员守将。 He simply left; Cao Cao told his men not to chase him. 关羽是辞别而去，曹操下令不许追赶。 羽尽封其所赐，拜书告辞，而奔先主于袁军。左右欲追之，曹公曰：「彼各为其主，勿追也。」"},{"id":"s4","h":"Guan Yu beheads Cai Yang at the old city · 古城斩蔡阳","t":"To prove his loyalty, Guan Yu kills Cao Cao’s general Cai Yang before Zhang Fei. 关羽在古城下斩蔡阳，向张飞表明心迹。 Cai Yang was killed in Runan by the rebel Gong Du, who had joined Liu Bei. 蔡阳（蔡扬）是在汝南被与刘备联合的共都打败所杀。 汝南贼共都等应之。遣蔡扬击都，不利，为都所破。"},{"id":"s4","h":"Zhuge Liang’s first fire at Bowang · 火烧博望坡","t":"The newly recruited Zhuge Liang burns Xiahou Dun’s army at Bowang. 刚出山的诸葛亮火烧博望坡，大败夏侯惇。 The ambush was Liu Bei’s own, years before he met Zhuge Liang. 这是刘备本人的伏击，比他认识诸葛亮早了好几年。 久之，先主设伏兵，一旦自烧屯伪遁，惇等追之，为伏兵所破。"},{"id":"s4","h":"Zhao Yun charges in and out seven times · 赵云七进七出","t":"Alone at Changban, Zhao Yun cuts through Cao Cao’s army seven times with the baby Liu Shan, killing fifty-odd officers. 赵云在长坂单骑七进七出曹营，怀抱阿斗，斩将五十余员。 He did save the baby and Lady Gan; the charges are the novel’s. Pei adds that someone told Liu Bei Zhao Yun had defected, and Liu Bei threw a halberd at him: “Zilong would not desert me.” 赵云确实救出了阿斗和甘夫人，但“七进七出”是小说的渲染。裴注引《云别传》说，有人说赵云投降了北方，刘备以手戟掷之，说“子龙不弃我走也”。 及先主为曹公所追于当阳长阪，弃妻子南走，云身抱弱子，即后主也，保护甘夫人，即后主母也，皆得免难。"},{"id":"s4","h":"Zhang Fei holds the bridge at Changban · 张飞喝断长坂桥","t":"Zhang Fei’s roar on the bridge makes Cao Cao’s army fall back. 张飞在长坂桥上一声大喝，吓退曹军。 Essentially true, minus the bridge collapsing and a general dying of fright. 基本属实，只是没有桥断、曹将被吓死的情节。 飞据水断桥，瞋目横矛曰：「身是张益德也，可来共决死！」敌皆无敢近者，故遂得免。"},{"id":"s4","h":"Zhuge Liang debates the scholars of Wu · 舌战群儒","t":"Zhuge Liang out-argues a hall of Wu officials who want to surrender. 诸葛亮在东吴舌战一班主降的文臣。 The history records only that he persuaded Sun Quan. 史书只记载他说服了孙权。 权大悦，即遣周瑜、程普、鲁肃等水军三万，随亮诣先主，并力拒曹公。"},{"id":"s4","h":"Jiang Gan steals the forged letter · 蒋干盗书","t":"Zhou Yu tricks his old friend Jiang Gan into stealing a fake letter, and Cao Cao executes his own admirals. 周瑜让老同学蒋干偷走假信，曹操误杀蔡瑁、张允。 Jiang Gan did visit Zhou Yu, to try to win him over; he came back praising him. No letter, no executions. 蒋干确曾去游说周瑜，回来后称赞周瑜，并无盗书和误杀之事。 干还，称瑜雅量高致，非言辞所间。"},{"id":"s4","h":"The beating of Huang Gai · 苦肉计（周瑜打黄盖）","t":"Zhou Yu has Huang Gai flogged so that his fake defection looks real. 周瑜当众杖打黄盖，让诈降显得可信。 The fake surrender is true, done by letter; the flogging is invented. 黄盖诈降属实，是写信诈降，“打黄盖”是虚构。 乃取蒙冲斗舰数十艘，实以薪草，膏油灌其中，裹以帷幕，上建牙旗，先书报曹公，欺以欲降。"},{"id":"s4","h":"Borrowing arrows with straw boats · 草船借箭","t":"Zhuge Liang collects 100,000 arrows from Cao Cao’s archers with straw-lined boats in the fog. 诸葛亮用草船在大雾中向曹军“借”得十万支箭。 A later source tells something similar about Sun Quan’s own ship at Ruxu in 213. 后世史料记载的类似故事，主角是213年濡须的孙权。 权乘大船来观军，公使弓弩乱发，箭著其船，船偏重将覆，权因回船，复以一面受箭，箭均船平，乃还。"},{"id":"s4","h":"Zhuge Liang summons the east wind · 借东风","t":"Zhuge Liang raises the south-east wind with rituals so the fire attack can work. 诸葛亮设坛作法，借来东南风，火攻才得以成功。 The fire attack was Huang Gai’s idea, carried out by Zhou Yu’s fleet. 火攻是黄盖提出、周瑜指挥的。 瑜部将黄盖曰：「今寇众我寡，难与持久。然观操军船舰首尾相接，可烧而走也。」"},{"id":"s4","h":"Guan Yu lets Cao Cao go at Huarong · 华容道义释曹操","t":"Guan Yu ambushes the fleeing Cao Cao, then lets him pass to repay an old debt. 关羽在华容道截住败逃的曹操，念旧情放他过去。 Cao Cao did retreat through the mud at Huarong, but nobody ambushed him there. 曹操确实经华容道在泥泞中撤退，但没有人在那里伏击。 公船舰为备所烧，引军从华容道步归，遇泥泞，道不通，天又大风，悉使羸兵负草填之，骑乃得过。"},{"id":"s4","h":"Zhou Yu angered to death three times · 三气周瑜","t":"Outwitted again and again by Zhuge Liang, Zhou Yu dies of rage. 周瑜屡被诸葛亮算计，气急而死。 He fell ill and died on the road while preparing to invade Yi. 周瑜是在准备西取益州的途中病逝的。 瑜还江陵，为行装，而道于巴丘病卒，时年三十六。"},{"id":"s4","h":"The marriage trap at Ganlu Temple · 甘露寺招亲","t":"Wu lures Liu Bei with a fake marriage offer to hold him hostage, but Zhuge Liang’s secret plans foil it. 东吴以招亲为名扣留刘备，诸葛亮锦囊妙计化解，“赔了夫人又折兵”。 The marriage was real and political; the trap, the temple scene and the secret plans are the novel’s. 这桩婚姻确有其事，是政治联姻；骗婚、甘露寺和锦囊都是小说的情节。 权稍畏之，进妹固好。"},{"id":"s4","h":"Guan Yu goes alone to the feast · 单刀赴会","t":"Guan Yu crosses to Lu Su’s camp with one blade and cows the Wu side. 关羽单刀赴鲁肃之会，威震东吴。 Lu Su set up the meeting, and it was Lu Su who did the scolding. 会面是鲁肃提出的，当场责备对方的也是鲁肃。 肃住益阳，与羽相拒。肃邀羽相见，各驻兵马百步上，但请将军单刀俱会。"},{"id":"s4","h":"Hua Tuo scrapes Guan Yu’s bone · 华佗刮骨疗毒","t":"The famous doctor Hua Tuo operates on Guan Yu, who plays chess unflinching. 名医华佗为关羽刮骨，关羽下棋谈笑自若。 The operation is real, but the doctor is unnamed; Hua Tuo had been executed by Cao Cao years before. 刮骨确有其事，但医生没有留下名字；华佗此前多年已被曹操处死。 医曰：「矢镞有毒，毒入于骨，当破臂作创，刮骨去毒，然后此患乃除耳。」"},{"id":"s4","h":"Seven captures of Meng Huo · 七擒孟获","t":"Zhuge Liang captures and frees the southern chief Meng Huo seven times until he submits. 诸葛亮七擒七纵南中首领孟获，使其心服。 Not in Chen Shou’s text; it comes from a later source quoted by Pei, and many historians treat it as legend. 陈寿正文没有，出自裴注所引后世史料，许多历史学家视为传说。 亮笑，纵使更战，七纵七禽，而亮犹遣获。"},{"id":"s4","h":"Zhuge Liang curses Wang Lang to death · 骂死王朗","t":"On the battlefield, Zhuge Liang’s tongue-lashing makes the old Wei minister Wang Lang fall dead from his horse. 诸葛亮阵前痛骂，魏国老臣王朗气绝坠马。 Wang Lang died in his bed in 228; the two never met. 王朗于228年病逝，两人从未对阵。 太和二年薨，谥曰成侯。"},{"id":"s4","h":"The empty fort · 空城计","t":"With no troops, Zhuge Liang opens the gates and plays the zither; Sima Yi suspects a trap and retreats. 诸葛亮城中无兵，大开城门、城头抚琴，司马懿疑有伏兵而退。 Told in a later source, and rejected by Pei Songzhi himself: Sima Yi was nowhere near that front. 见于裴注所引的故事，裴松之本人就驳斥了它：司马懿当时根本不在那条战线上。 案阳平在汉中。亮初屯阳平，宣帝尚为荆州都督，镇宛城，至曹真死后，始与亮于关中相抗御耳。"},{"id":"s4","h":"Liu Shan recalls Zhuge Liang · 后主召回诸葛亮","t":"Sima Yi spreads rumours in Chengdu that Zhuge Liang plans to seize the throne; Liu Shan believes them and orders him home in the middle of a winning campaign. 司马懿派人在成都散布诸葛亮欲自立为帝的谣言，后主刘禅听信，下诏召回正在得胜的诸葛亮。 In 231 Zhuge Liang did withdraw on a summons, but the history blames Li Yan, who was responsible for his grain: when supplies ran short, Li Yan sent word calling him back, then feigned surprise that he had returned. Zhuge Liang had him removed from office. 231年诸葛亮确实奉命退兵，但史书记载责任在负责运粮的李严：粮草不继时，李严派人传达旨意召诸葛亮回军，等他退兵后又假装吃惊。诸葛亮随后上表将李严废为平民。 平遣参军狐忠、督军成藩喻指，呼亮来还；亮承以退军。平闻军退，乃更阳惊，说「军粮饶足，何以便归」！"},{"id":"s4","h":"The wooden oxen and gliding horses · 木牛流马","t":"Zhuge Liang’s self-moving wooden transports haul grain over the mountains; when Sima Yi copies them, Zhuge Liang’s men twist the tongues of the captured oxen so they freeze, and carry off Wei’s grain. 诸葛亮造木牛流马翻山运粮；司马懿仿造后，诸葛亮让蜀军扭转牛马舌头使其不能行动，夺走魏军的粮草。 Both devices are real and both were Zhuge Liang’s designs, used on the 231 and 234 campaigns; how they worked is unknown. The tongue trick is the novel’s. 木牛、流马确有其物，都出自诸葛亮的设计，分别用于231年和234年的北伐，具体构造今已不详。“扭转舌头”的计策是小说虚构的。 亮性长于巧思，损益连弩，木牛流马，皆出其意。"},{"id":"s4","h":"The seven-star lamps at Wuzhang · 五丈原禳星","t":"The dying Zhuge Liang lights lamps to extend his life; Wei Yan knocks one over and dooms him. 诸葛亮病重时点七星灯禳星续命，被魏延闯入踏灭。 He simply fell ill and died in camp. 诸葛亮是在军中病逝的，没有禳星之事。 其年八月，亮疾病，卒于军，时年五十四。"}]}]};
